const e=[{id:1,code:"RED-01",icon:"Flame",accent:"#dc2626",category:"Emergency",title:"Code Red — fire response",subtitle:"Protecting the lives of patients and staff during a hospital fire",summary:"Code Red is the code a hospital uses to announce a fire. A fast, organized response protects patients' lives, because many of them cannot get out on their own. Staff follow the RACE procedure: Rescue, Alarm, Contain, and Extinguish or Evacuate. Fire extinguishers are used with the PASS technique. This lesson explains how a fire is recognized, how the alarm is raised and how a fire is safely contained and put out.",facts:[{label:"Emergency numbers",value:"Hospital internal emergency number + 115"},{label:"Standard",value:"NFPA 101 + NFPA 99"},{label:"Response procedure",value:"RACE + PASS"},{label:"Extinguisher for electrical equipment",value:"CO₂ or clean agent"}],steps:[{title:"R — Rescue",detail:"Immediately move patients near the fire and smoke out of danger. Move a bedbound patient in their bed after releasing the wheel locks. Move patients on oxygen or a ventilator with a portable oxygen cylinder or a bag-mask device.",critical:!0},{title:"A — Alarm",detail:'Pull or push the fire alarm pull station and call the hospital internal emergency number. In a calm voice say "Code Red" and give the location of the fire (floor and room). Civil Defense (115) is notified according to the hospital fire plan.',critical:!0},{title:"C — Contain",detail:"Close the doors of the fire room and the corridor so smoke and fire do not spread, but do not lock them. Do not shut the zone oxygen valve immediately: it is closed only after patients on oxygen and those on ventilators have been switched to portable cylinders or bag-mask devices, and only on the order of the responsible physician or nurse or according to the fire plan.",critical:!0},{title:"E — Extinguish",detail:"Only if all of this is true: the fire is small and just starting (no larger than a wastebasket), the extinguisher is the right type, your exit route behind you is clear, the alarm has been raised, you have been trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. If the fire does not go out at once, leave and close the door behind you. Choose the right extinguisher: CO₂ for electrical equipment, wet chemical (Class K) for cooking oil, and in the MRI room only a non-magnetic extinguisher."},{title:"E — Evacuate",detail:`If the fire is large or cannot be controlled, move patients horizontally to a safe compartment behind smoke doors on the same floor. If necessary, then move them vertically to a lower floor.
• Do not use elevators.`,critical:!0}],reference:[{heading:"The right extinguisher for each location",points:["Operating room (OR): saline for a fire on the patient, and a CO₂ extinguisher (ASA recommendation).","MRI: only a non-magnetic extinguisher labeled for MRI; a steel extinguisher becomes a projectile.","Laboratory: CO₂ or ABC depending on the materials present; a Class D unit whose label lists the metal, where combustible metals are present.","ICU: CO₂ or clean agent for electrical equipment.","Kitchen and cafeteria: wet chemical (Class K).","Corridors and public areas: ABC powder."]},{heading:"PASS — extinguisher technique",points:["Before PASS, every answer must be yes: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out.","P = Pull: pull the safety pin.","A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames.","S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle firmly.","S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side; if the fire does not go out at once, leave and close the door.","Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds (the exact time is on the label). Start from the distance on the label, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller."]},{heading:"What must not be done",points:["Do not use the elevator: it may stop at the fire floor.","Do not open windows: extra air makes the fire burn more intensely.","Do not take a steel extinguisher into the MRI room: the magnet pulls it with great force.","Do not pour water on energized electrical equipment: risk of a fatal shock.","Do not pour water on hot oil: the oil spatters violently and the fire spreads."]}],warnings:["The first step is always to rescue the people in danger. Do not shut the zone oxygen valve immediately: first switch patients on oxygen or ventilators to portable cylinders or bag-mask devices, then close the valve on the order of the clinical lead or according to the fire plan. Use an extinguisher only on a small fire; if the fire is larger than a wastebasket, evacuate the area."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): In a hospital laboratory, a bottle of 70% alcohol caught fire on the workbench. A trained nurse immediately applied RACE: 1) moved nearby people away, 2) announced Code Red on the internal number, 3) with her exit kept open behind her, put out the small fire with a CO₂ extinguisher using PASS before it could spread, 4) left, closed the laboratory door and had the room ventilated before anyone went back in. Because CO₂ leaves no residue, the laboratory equipment was not damaged. Had ABC powder been used, cleaning the equipment would have been much more difficult.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You work in the ICU. Suddenly smoke comes out of an IV pump and a small flame appears on its cable. A patient on a ventilator is 2 meters away. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I pick up the CO₂ extinguisher and start putting out the fire using PASS",correct:!1,feedback:"Too early. The patient is in danger; the first step is Rescue (R)."},{text:"I open the room door to let fresh air in",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: extra air makes the fire burn more intensely and spreads the smoke."},{text:"With my colleague, I connect the patient to a bag-mask or portable cylinder and move their bed away from the fire",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Rescue (R). Once the patient is protected, the alarm comes next."},{text:"I call the elevator to move the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators must not be used during a Code Red; they may stop."}]},{prompt:"The patient has been moved out of danger. The fire is still small and only a few seconds have passed since it started. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the extinguisher and start putting out the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"In RACE, the alarm comes before extinguishing, so that the backup teams know."},{text:"I push the fire alarm pull station and call the internal emergency number",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Alarm (A). Code Red must be announced before extinguishing."},{text:"I call a friend in the unit on my personal phone",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not the official route. The fire alarm and the internal number alert the Code Red team, and the hospital notifies Civil Defense (115) according to its plan."},{text:"I draw the curtain at the door",correct:!1,feedback:"This is part of Contain, not Alarm. The alarm comes before containment."}]},{prompt:"The fire is still small (smaller than a wastebasket), and a CO₂ extinguisher is within reach 3 meters away. What is the next step?",options:[{text:"With my exit kept open behind me, I put out the small fire with the CO₂ extinguisher; if it does not go out at once, I leave and close the door behind me",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: extinguish only while the fire is small and your exit is open; if it does not go out, leave and close the door (Contain). CO₂ is suitable for electrical equipment fires and leaves no residue; in a small room, ventilate after use."},{text:"I pick up the ABC extinguisher because it works on all fires",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC works on electrical fires, but its powder contaminates medical equipment and irritates patients' airways. In the ICU a CO₂ extinguisher is better."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the cable is still energized and an electric shock will occur."},{text:"I open the door to let fresh air in",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the door to change the air feeds the fire with oxygen and spreads smoke. Keep only your way out clear, and close the door behind you when you leave."}]}],quiz:[{question:"In the ICU, what is the first step during a Code Red?",options:[{text:"I pick up the nearest extinguisher and put out the fire before moving any patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Before extinguishing, the patient must be out of danger."},{text:"I rescue the patients in danger, and connect those on oxygen to a portable cylinder or bag-mask",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: RACE begins with Rescue. The zone oxygen valve is closed later, on the order of the person in charge."},{text:"I open the windows to let the smoke out before doing anything else",correct:!1,feedback:"This increases the air supply and makes the fire bigger."},{text:"I keep the patients in their beds and wait in the room until the 115 team arrives and takes charge",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not wait for the team; start RACE immediately."}]},{question:"Which extinguisher is suitable in the MRI room?",options:[{text:"A standard (steel) ABC extinguisher from the nurses' station",correct:!1,feedback:"A steel extinguisher is pulled strongly by the magnet and becomes a dangerous projectile."},{text:"A non-magnetic extinguisher labeled for MRI (MR Conditional)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only non-magnetic equipment may enter the MRI room (Zone IV). The MRI magnet stays active even during a power outage."},{text:"Water in a steel bucket",correct:!1,feedback:"A steel bucket also becomes a projectile, and water on electrical equipment is dangerous."},{text:"A standard CO₂ extinguisher brought in from the nearest corridor",correct:!1,feedback:"Standard corridor extinguishers are generally steel and must not be taken into the MRI room."}]},{question:"What does the letter C stand for in RACE?",options:[{text:"Call",correct:!1,feedback:"Calling is part of A (Alarm)."},{text:"Contain",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: closing doors so smoke and fire do not spread."},{text:"Cool",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; C stands for Contain."},{text:"Collect",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; C stands for Contain."}]},{question:"About how long does a small (2 kg) extinguisher last?",options:[{text:"Only a few seconds; the exact time is on its label",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a very short time, so you must aim accurately at the base of the fire. The exact discharge time is on the label."},{text:"About 5 minutes, long enough to walk around the whole fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too long; a small extinguisher empties within a few seconds."},{text:"2–3 minutes; the exact time is shown on its pressure gauge",correct:!1,feedback:"Still too long; a small extinguisher empties within a few seconds (see the label)."},{text:"One hour",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too long: a small extinguisher empties within a few seconds; the exact time is on its label."}],explanation:"A small extinguisher empties in only a few seconds, so be ready and aim accurately at the base of the fire. The exact discharge time is on its label."},{question:"During a Code Red, a colleague wants to lock the door of the fire room so no one can go in. What is right?",options:[{text:"Close the door but do not lock it, so the response team can get in",correct:!0},{text:"Lock it and leave the key at the nurses' station for the 115 team",correct:!1},{text:"Leave it wide open so the smoke can escape into the corridor",correct:!1},{text:"Wedge it half open so staff can keep checking the fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"The door is closed to hold back smoke and fire, but it is not locked, so the 115 team and the hospital's fire team can reach the fire without delay."},{question:"You used a CO₂ extinguisher on a small fire on an equipment cart in the ICU, but the fire did not go out at once. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave the room, close the door behind me and continue with RACE",correct:!0},{text:"I fetch a second extinguisher and attack the fire again on my own",correct:!1},{text:"I stay in the room and keep spraying until the extinguisher is empty",correct:!1},{text:"I open the window to clear the smoke and keep fighting the fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"An extinguisher is only for a fire that goes out at once. If it does not, leave, close the door to contain the fire and continue with evacuation according to the fire plan."}],tips:["At the start of every shift, know where the nearest fire alarm pull station, the extinguishers and your unit's zone oxygen valve are.","Keep corridors and smoke doors clear of beds, carts and equipment so the doors can close and beds can pass.","Know which of your patients are on oxygen or ventilators, and where the portable cylinders and bag-mask devices are kept.","When you call the internal emergency number, give the floor, the room and what is burning, then follow your hospital's fire plan."],activity:{prompt:"You are a nurse on a general ward at night. A visitor shouts that smoke is coming from a patient's room, where a phone charger is plugged in beside the bed. The patient is elderly and cannot walk. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I move the patient out in the bed, close the room door, then raise the alarm and call the internal emergency number",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: RACE starts with Rescue, and closing the door keeps the smoke in the room. If a colleague is near, one rescues while the other raises the alarm at the same time."},{text:"I go to the nurses' station for an extinguisher and leave the room door open so I can come back quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"The patient stays in danger and the open door lets smoke spread into the corridor. Rescue comes first, then close the door."},{text:"I pour a jug of water over the charger and the bed to stop the smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on a live electrical source can cause an electric shock, and the patient is still in the smoke. Rescue the patient first."},{text:"I call the patient's family to ask them what they want us to do",correct:!1,feedback:"This wastes time while the patient is in the smoke. Start RACE at once and follow your hospital's fire plan."}]}},{id:2,code:"BLUE-02",icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#2563eb",category:"Emergency",title:"Code Blue — cardiac resuscitation",subtitle:"Advanced CPR, the AED and the work of the Code Blue team",summary:"Code Blue is the code announced in a hospital when a patient's heart or breathing stops. Every minute of delay in cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and defibrillation lowers the chance of survival. Under AHA 2025, the check for breathing and a pulse takes no more than 10 seconds, and CPR then starts immediately. This lesson explains advanced CPR, use of the automated external defibrillator (AED), and the roles of the Code Blue team.",facts:[{label:"Chest compressions",value:"100–120 per minute"},{label:"Compression depth",value:"5–6 cm (adult)"},{label:"CPR ratio",value:"30:2 (compressions:breaths)"},{label:"Standard",value:"AHA 2025"}],steps:[{title:"1. Recognize the arrest (in less than 10 seconds)",detail:"The patient is unresponsive, is not breathing or is only gasping, and has no carotid pulse. The check must not take longer than 10 seconds.",critical:!0},{title:"2. Call a Code Blue",detail:`Press the Code Blue button on the wall or call the hospital internal emergency number.
• Do not wait for the team: start CPR immediately.`,critical:!0},{title:"3. CPR at 30:2",detail:"Place the heels of your hands on the lower half of the breastbone, keep your arms straight and compress to a depth of 5–6 cm at a rate of 100–120 per minute. Allow the chest to recoil fully. After every 30 compressions, give 2 breaths with a bag-mask device.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Use the AED",detail:`Turn the device on and place the two pads on the bare chest as shown on the pads: one on the upper right side of the chest and one on the lower left side. The device analyzes the heart rhythm by itself.
• No one touches the patient during analysis or the shock.`,critical:!0},{title:"5. Code Blue team roles",detail:"Physician: team leader. Nurse 1: chest compressions. Nurse 2: vascular access (IV/IO) and medications. Technician: AED or defibrillator. Recorder: documents times, medications and shocks. The person doing compressions is replaced every 2 minutes."},{title:"6. Medications",detail:"Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes. Amiodarone 300 mg for VF or pVT that persists after shocks (second dose 150 mg). Atropine is for bradycardia, not for cardiac arrest."}],reference:[{heading:"CPR for adults, children and infants",points:["Adult (from puberty onward): two hands, 5–6 cm, 30:2.","Child (from 1 year to puberty): one or two hands, about 5 cm (one third of the chest depth), 30:2 with one rescuer and 15:2 with two rescuers.","Infant (under 1 year): two thumbs encircling the chest or the heel of one hand, about 4 cm, 30:2 with one rescuer and 15:2 with two rescuers.","Switch the person doing compressions every 2 minutes so the compressions stay strong."]},{heading:"AED — four steps",points:["1. Turn the device on and follow its voice prompts.","2. Place the two pads on the bare chest as shown on the pads.","3. No one touches the patient during analysis or the shock.","4. Resume CPR immediately after the shock; the device will announce when it re-analyzes the rhythm."]},{heading:"Special situations",points:["Pregnant woman: manually displace the uterus to the left to reduce pressure on the great vessels.","Electric shock: cut off the power before touching the patient.","Anaphylaxis: epinephrine IM into the outer thigh.","Severe bleeding: direct pressure on the wound, and vascular access for fluids and blood.","Patient on a ventilator: ventilate with a bag-mask during transport."]}],warnings:["Do not wait for the team: start CPR immediately. Every minute of delay in CPR and defibrillation lowers the chance of survival. Press the Code Blue button and start CPR at the same time, or send someone else to raise the alarm."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A 62-year-old patient in the ICU after cardiac surgery went into cardiac arrest at 22:14. The shift nurse pressed the Code Blue button at 22:14:07 and immediately started CPR at 30:2. The Code Blue team arrived at 22:14:45. At 22:15:12 the AED detected VF and delivered the first shock. After two shocks and about 4 minutes of CPR, the heart started beating again at 22:19 (ROSC). The patient stayed in the ICU for post-cardiac arrest care and left the ICU in good condition a few days later. This example shows how important it is to start CPR within the first seconds and to defibrillate early.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the ICU shift nurse. At 23:40 a patient's monitor shows V-tach, which then becomes a flat line. You go to the bedside: the patient is unresponsive and not breathing. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I call the on-duty physician to make a diagnosis",correct:!1,feedback:"Delaying CPR for a diagnosis lowers the chance of survival. The situation is clear: cardiac arrest."},{text:"I press the Code Blue button and immediately start CPR at 30:2",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Code Blue and CPR at the same time. Do not wait for the team."},{text:"I look for the AED before starting CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"Start CPR immediately; a colleague can bring the AED."},{text:"I prepare epinephrine",correct:!1,feedback:"Medications are the Code Blue team's task after CPR has started. Chest compressions come first."}]},{prompt:'CPR has been going on for about 2 minutes. The Code Blue team has arrived and the AED pads are on. The device says: "Shock advised." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I continue chest compressions during the shock as well",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the current will strike you too. No one touches the patient during the shock."},{text:'I call out loudly "Clear!", make sure no one is touching the patient and then deliver the shock',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: everyone stands clear. CPR resumes immediately after the shock."},{text:"After the shock, I wait for the pulse to return (ROSC)",correct:!1,feedback:"Immediately after the shock, 2 minutes of CPR are given; do not wait."},{text:"I remove the AED and focus on IV access",correct:!1,feedback:"The AED must stay attached to the patient. The rhythm is re-analyzed after 2 minutes of CPR."}]},{prompt:"After two shocks and 4 minutes of CPR, the patient shows ROSC: the pulse and breathing return. What is the next step?",options:[{text:"I stop CPR; our work is done",correct:!1,feedback:"After ROSC, close monitoring is needed: monitor, airway, breathing and blood pressure."},{text:"Continuous monitoring, assessment of airway, breathing and circulation (ABC), and post-cardiac arrest care in the ICU",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: post-cardiac arrest care also includes temperature control."},{text:"I discharge the patient from the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"The patient needs intensive care in the ICU."},{text:"I give another 1 mg dose of epinephrine to sustain ROSC",correct:!1,feedback:"After ROSC, the cardiac arrest dose (1 mg) is not given. If blood pressure is low, the physician may order a vasopressor infusion."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the correct rate of chest compressions?",options:[{text:"60–80 per minute",correct:!1,feedback:"Too slow; not enough blood reaches the brain."},{text:"100–120 per minute",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to AHA 2025."},{text:"More than 150 per minute",correct:!1,feedback:"Too fast; the heart does not fill completely and compression depth decreases."},{text:"Any rate",correct:!1,feedback:"The rate matters for effective CPR."}]},{question:"How is CPR performed on an infant (under 1 year)?",options:[{text:"Two hands on the center of the chest, pressing about 6 cm deep",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too deep for an infant."},{text:"Two thumbs encircling the chest (or the heel of one hand), about 4 cm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under AHA 2025, the two-finger technique is no longer taught."},{text:"One hand, 5 cm",correct:!1,feedback:"This is for children from 1 year to puberty."},{text:"CPR is not performed on infants; only rescue breaths are given until help arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; every infant in cardiac arrest receives CPR."}]},{question:"When does the AED not deliver a shock?",options:[{text:'When the device says "No shock advised"',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the AED delivers a shock only for VF and pulseless VT."},{text:"A shock is always given at the start, whatever the heart rhythm",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: asystole is not treated with a shock."},{text:"Always before CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"CPR starts immediately, and the AED is used as soon as it arrives."},{text:"Only for adults",correct:!1,feedback:"The AED is also used for children (with pediatric pads if available)."}]},{question:"What is the first drug given in a Code Blue?",options:[{text:"Atropine 1 mg IV every 3–5 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Atropine is for bradycardia, not for cardiac arrest."},{text:"Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to AHA."},{text:"Amiodarone 300 mg IV/IO as a rapid bolus",correct:!1,feedback:"It is for VF or pVT that persists after shocks, not the first drug in every case."},{text:"Bicarbonate 50 mEq IV for every patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Only in special situations, such as hyperkalemia."}]},{question:"The AED says it is analyzing the heart rhythm, and a colleague keeps doing compressions. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Tell everyone to stand clear: no one touches the patient during analysis",correct:!0},{text:"Let the colleague continue, because compressions never stop for any reason",correct:!1},{text:"Remove the pads and switch the device off and on again",correct:!1},{text:"Give two breaths with the bag-mask while the device analyzes",correct:!1}],explanation:"Movement and touching disturb the analysis, and a person touching the patient during a shock can be injured. Stand clear during analysis and the shock, then resume CPR immediately."},{question:"The nurse doing chest compressions has been going for 2 minutes, and the compressions are getting shallow. What should the team do?",options:[{text:"Swap in a fresh person at the rhythm check, with a minimal pause",correct:!0},{text:"Let the same nurse continue until the patient's own doctor arrives on the unit",correct:!1},{text:"Stop CPR for a few minutes so the nurse can rest and recover",correct:!1},{text:"Slow the rate of compressions down so the nurse does not tire as fast",correct:!1}],explanation:"Compressions become weaker as the rescuer tires, often without them noticing. The person doing compressions is changed every 2 minutes, with as short a pause as possible."}],tips:["At the start of every shift, check the resuscitation cart and the AED or defibrillator on your unit using your hospital's checklist.","Know where the Code Blue button is in every room you work in, and know the internal emergency number.","Change the person doing compressions every 2 minutes, at the rhythm check, so compressions stay strong.","Use closed-loop communication: the leader gives a task to a named person, who repeats it back and reports when it is done."],activity:{prompt:"You are a radiology technician. A patient on the X-ray table suddenly stops responding and is only gasping. A nurse from the next room is with you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"One of us calls a Code Blue while the other starts chest compressions at once, and the AED is brought to the table",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: gasping is not normal breathing; it is a sign of cardiac arrest. Raise the alarm and start CPR at the same time, then use the AED as soon as it arrives."},{text:"We wait for the breathing to improve, because gasping shows the patient is still breathing",correct:!1,feedback:"Gasping is not normal breathing. Waiting loses minutes, and every minute of delay lowers the chance of survival."},{text:"We both leave the room to find the patient's doctor before doing anything else",correct:!1,feedback:"A patient in cardiac arrest is never left alone. Call the Code Blue from the room and start CPR; the team comes to you."},{text:"I spend a few minutes feeling for a pulse so that I am completely sure before starting",correct:!1,feedback:"The check for breathing and a pulse takes no more than 10 seconds. If you are not sure, start CPR."}]}},{id:3,code:"ORANGE-03",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#ea580c",category:"Fire",title:"Fire classes and extinguishers in the hospital — NFPA 10",subtitle:"Fire classes under NFPA 10 and choosing the right extinguisher for medical areas",summary:"Fires in hospitals have their own characteristics: oxygen, alcohol, electrical medical equipment and rooms such as MRI. Fire classes and extinguisher selection are defined by NFPA 10 (the standard for portable fire extinguishers), and NFPA 99 (the Health Care Facilities Code) sets specific procedures for oxygen and the operating room. Choosing the wrong extinguisher can injure a patient or put critical equipment out of service. In the operating room, fire spreads very quickly in an oxygen-enriched atmosphere, so a correct and rapid response saves lives. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.",facts:[{label:"Standard",value:"NFPA 10 (extinguishers) + NFPA 99"},{label:"Fire classes (NFPA)",value:"A · B · C · D · K"},{label:"OEA (oxygen-enriched atmosphere)",value:"O₂ above 23.5%"},{label:"Temperature of an electrical arc",value:"Up to about 19,400°C"}],steps:[{title:"Class A — ordinary solid materials",detail:`Paper, bed linen, fabric and plastic. Very common in hospitals. Extinguisher: water or ABC powder.
• Near oxygen, these materials burn much faster.`,critical:!0},{title:"Class B — flammable liquids and gases",detail:`Alcohol (such as hand sanitizer gel), acetone and ether. The flash point of pure ethanol is about 13°C, and that of ether is about −45°C. Extinguisher for alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop.
• Do not pour water on flammable liquids.`,critical:!0},{title:"Class C — energized electrical equipment",detail:`MRI, CT, X-ray, ventilators, defibrillators. If you can do so safely, cut off the power. Extinguisher: CO₂ or clean agent; ABC powder also works but contaminates the equipment.
• In the MRI room, only a non-magnetic extinguisher.`,critical:!0},{title:"Class D and lithium batteries",detail:`Class D covers combustible metals (such as magnesium and sodium in laboratories) and requires a Class D extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal. The lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries in medical devices, power wheelchairs and phones contain no metallic lithium: water and cooling are recommended for them. Class D is only for lithium-metal batteries.
• A Li-ion battery can reignite after being extinguished (thermal runaway); keep cooling it and monitor it.`,critical:!0},{title:"Class K — cooking oil",detail:`Oils and fats in the kitchen and cafeteria. Wet chemical extinguisher (NFPA Class K; Class F on EN 2 labels) only.
• Water on hot oil makes burning oil spatter.
• An ABC extinguisher is not suitable for a cooking oil fire, because the oil reignites.`},{title:"OEA — oxygen-enriched atmosphere",detail:"Under NFPA 99, air with an oxygen concentration above 23.5% is an OEA. In an OEA, materials ignite easily and fire spreads very quickly. In the operating room and ICU, keep cylinders away from heat sources. If a fire breaks out on a patient, the anesthesiologist immediately stops the oxygen flow to the patient.",critical:!0}],reference:[{heading:"Selection table (NFPA 10)",points:["A: water or ABC.","B: liquids: CO₂, ABC or foam (alcohol-resistant foam for alcohol and acetone); gas: stop the gas first if it is safe.","C: CO₂ or clean agent; ABC is also acceptable but contaminates equipment.","D: only a Class D extinguisher or agent listed for that metal.","K: wet chemical only."]},{heading:"Extinguishers by hospital department",points:["Operating room: CO₂ (ASA recommendation), and saline for a fire on the patient.","ICU: CO₂ or clean agent for electrical equipment.","MRI: only a non-magnetic extinguisher labeled for MRI.","Laboratory: CO₂ or ABC, a Class D unit listed for the metal where combustible metals are present, and an eyewash station nearby.","Pharmacy and storerooms: ABC, with flammable liquids kept in dedicated cabinets."]},{heading:"Operating room fire — first steps",points:["1. The anesthesiologist stops the flow of all airway gases (O₂ and N₂O).","2. Remove the drapes and all burning material from the patient.","3. Put out the fire on the patient with saline or by smothering it.","4. If the fire persists: a CO₂ extinguisher.","5. If it is not extinguished: Code Red, evacuate the patient, close the door and shut the room's medical gas valve."]}],warnings:["In the operating room, fire spreads within seconds in an oxygen-enriched atmosphere. First stop the oxygen flow to the patient at the anesthesia machine and remove burning materials, then put out the fire. Do not take a steel extinguisher into the MRI room: the magnet pulls it in and it becomes a projectile."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): In an operating room, the alcohol-based skin prep had not yet dried when the electrocautery was used, and the drapes caught fire. A staff member brought an ABC extinguisher, and the powder spread over the patient's open wound and airway. The correct procedure would have been: stop the oxygen, remove the burning drapes, extinguish with saline and, if needed, use a CO₂ extinguisher. Lesson: before electrocautery is used, the prep solution must be completely dry.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an operating room nurse. During surgery, the electrocautery ignites the alcohol-based prep solution and flames appear on the patient's chest. The patient is receiving 100% oxygen. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I spray the ABC extinguisher on the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the powder gets into the wound and the airway. ASA recommends a CO₂ extinguisher, and only after the gas flow has been stopped."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Saline can put out a fire on the patient, but until the oxygen is stopped the fire will reignite. The first step is stopping the oxygen."},{text:"I ask the anesthesiologist to stop the oxygen flow to the patient immediately",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: without excess oxygen the fire becomes much weaker. This is done at the anesthesia machine, not by shutting the zone valve."},{text:"I start horizontal evacuation",correct:!1,feedback:"The patient is on the operating table; first stop the oxygen and put out the fire."}]},{prompt:"The oxygen has been stopped, but the fire is still on the chest. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the ABC extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC powder contaminates the wound and the airway. If an extinguisher is needed, use CO₂."},{text:"I remove the burning drapes and put out the fire with saline or by smothering it",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: following ASA guidance, remove the burning materials and extinguish with saline."},{text:"I take the patient down off the table",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the patient will be injured and the fire will spread."},{text:"I wait for the Code Red team",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too late; saline and wet cloths are within your reach."}]},{prompt:"The fire is out. What is the third step?",options:[{text:"I continue the surgery as before",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the patient must be fully assessed and the incident must be documented."},{text:"I announce Code Red, examine the patient for burns and document the incident",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Code Red, a burn assessment and an incident report."},{text:"I leave the room and say nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"An incident report is needed for patient safety and for learning."},{text:"I transfer the patient to another hospital so the surgery can be continued there",correct:!1,feedback:"A full assessment is needed before any transfer."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Which extinguisher is used in the MRI room?",options:[{text:"A standard ABC extinguisher from the corridor",correct:!1,feedback:"A steel extinguisher is pulled in by the magnet and becomes a projectile."},{text:"A non-magnetic extinguisher labeled for MRI",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only non-magnetic equipment is permitted in the MRI room (Zone IV)."},{text:"Water in a steel bucket",correct:!1,feedback:"A steel bucket also becomes a projectile."},{text:"Any CO₂ extinguisher, since CO₂ leaves no residue",correct:!1,feedback:"Only a non-magnetic (MR Conditional) CO₂ extinguisher may enter the room."}]},{question:"What is OEA?",options:[{text:"Fire Class D",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; Class D covers combustible metals."},{text:"Air in which the oxygen concentration is above 23.5%",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Oxygen-Enriched Atmosphere under NFPA 99."},{text:"An NFPA standard for oxygen piping in hospitals",correct:!1,feedback:"OEA is not a standard but a condition of the air, defined in NFPA 99 as oxygen above 23.5%."},{text:"A safety device that measures the oxygen level in the room",correct:!1,feedback:"OEA is not a device but air with oxygen above 23.5%, in which materials ignite easily."}],explanation:"Under NFPA 99, an oxygen-enriched atmosphere (OEA) is air with more than 23.5% oxygen; in it, materials ignite easily and fire spreads very quickly."},{question:"How is a fire in the lithium-ion (Li-ion) battery of a medical device put out?",options:[{text:"Only with a Class D extinguisher or dry sand, because water always causes an explosion in any lithium battery",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: that applies to lithium-metal batteries. Li-ion batteries contain no metallic lithium."},{text:"With water or a water-based extinguisher and continuous cooling; Class D only for lithium-metal batteries",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cooling prevents thermal runaway and reignition."},{text:"CO₂ to put out the flames, and then I leave the battery alone to cool by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"CO₂ puts out the flames but does not cool the battery; it can reignite."},{text:"ABC, and then I do nothing more",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC does not cool the battery; thermal runaway continues."}]},{question:"During an operating room fire, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the extinguisher and spray it on the surgical field",correct:!1,feedback:"Before using the extinguisher, stop the oxygen flow."},{text:"Stopping the flow of oxygen and other gases to the patient",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under ASA guidance, airway gases are stopped immediately."},{text:"Removing the patient from the room before anything else",correct:!1,feedback:"Later, if the fire is not brought under control."},{text:"Pouring water on the electrical equipment",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: water on energized equipment is dangerous."}]},{question:"A small fire starts in a waste bin in a ward corridor. You have the right extinguisher, but smoke is spreading toward your only way out. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not use the extinguisher: I leave, close the doors and raise the Code Red",correct:!0},{text:"I fight it quickly, because the fire is small and the extinguisher is right",correct:!1},{text:"I move through the smoke to get closer, so the spray reaches the base",correct:!1},{text:"I spray from where I stand and then crawl out under the smoke",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every point of the go/no-go check must be yes. Smoke blocking the way out is a no: leave, contain the fire by closing doors and raise the alarm."},{question:"A small fire starts in a power strip behind an ICU monitor. Every point of the go/no-go check is yes. Which choice is right?",options:[{text:"Cut the power if it is safe, then use CO₂ or clean agent as the label says",correct:!0},{text:"Use a water extinguisher, because water cools the fire the fastest",correct:!1},{text:"Use foam, because foam works on every kind of hospital fire",correct:!1},{text:"Throw a wet towel over the live monitor and the power strip",correct:!1}],explanation:"A fire in energized equipment is Class C in NFPA letters. Cut the power if you can do it safely and use CO₂ or clean agent; water and foam conduct electricity."}],tips:["Learn the extinguishers on your own unit: read each label for the fire classes it covers and how to use it.","Before you use any extinguisher, run the go/no-go check: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is right, the exit is behind you, the alarm is raised, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out.","If an extinguisher is missing, blocked or used, or its pressure gauge shows it is not ready, report it at once to your hospital's safety or engineering team.","Never take an extinguisher into the MRI room unless its label says it is for MRI."],activity:{prompt:"You work in the hospital kitchen. Oil in a deep fryer catches fire. A dry powder (ABC) extinguisher hangs on the wall and a wet chemical (Class K) extinguisher stands next to the fryer. The alarm has been raised and the exit is behind you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I switch off the fryer if it is safe, use the wet chemical extinguisher as its label says, and leave if the fire does not go out at once",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a cooking oil fire needs a wet chemical extinguisher (Class K; Class F on EN 2 labels). Turning off the heat helps stop it reigniting. If it does not go out at once, leave and close the door."},{text:"I throw a bowl of water on the fryer to cool the oil quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on burning oil turns to steam at once and throws burning oil out of the fryer. Never use water on a cooking oil fire."},{text:"I use the ABC powder extinguisher from the wall, because ABC covers every class",correct:!1,feedback:"ABC covers classes A, B and C only, not cooking oil. The oil can reignite after powder. Use the wet chemical extinguisher."},{text:"I carry the fryer outside so that the fire can burn out in the open air",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving a container of burning oil can spill it onto you and others and spread the fire. Leave it in place."}]}},{id:4,code:"PED-04",icon:"Baby",accent:"#ec4899",category:"Medical",title:"Pediatric and infant CPR",subtitle:"NICU, pediatric Code Blue and special procedures",summary:"Pediatric CPR differs from adult CPR in compression depth, hand technique and drug doses. Under AHA 2025, an infant is younger than 1 year (excluding newly born infants), and a child is from 1 year to puberty. The CPR sequence for infants and children is C-A-B: chest compressions, then airway and breathing. Most cardiac arrests in children result from respiratory problems, so good ventilation with oxygen is very important. Newly born infants in the delivery room and NICU are managed with the dedicated Neonatal Resuscitation Program (NRP).",facts:[{label:"Infant (under 1 year)",value:"Two thumbs or heel of the hand · about 4 cm · 100–120 per minute"},{label:"Child (1 year to puberty)",value:"One or two hands · about 5 cm · 100–120 per minute"},{label:"Newborn temperature",value:"36.5–37.5°C"},{label:"Standard",value:"AHA PALS 2025 + NRP"}],steps:[{title:"1. Recognize the arrest",detail:"The child is unresponsive, is not breathing or is only gasping. Check the pulse at the brachial artery in an infant, and at the carotid or femoral artery in a child, for no more than 10 seconds.",critical:!0},{title:"2. The C-A-B sequence and the importance of breathing",detail:"Under AHA, CPR for infants and children starts with chest compressions, followed by opening the airway and giving breaths (C-A-B). Because most cardiac arrests in children result from inadequate breathing, effective breaths with a bag-mask and oxygen must start early. Keep the infant's head in the neutral position; tilting the head too far back blocks the airway.",critical:!0},{title:"3. CPR for an infant (under 1 year)",detail:"Place both thumbs on the lower half of the breastbone and encircle the chest with your hands (two-thumb encircling technique); if you cannot encircle the chest, use the heel of one hand. The two-finger technique is no longer taught. Depth about 4 cm (one third of the chest depth). 30:2 with one rescuer, 15:2 with two rescuers.",critical:!0},{title:"4. CPR for a child (1 year to puberty)",detail:"The heel of one hand, or both hands for a larger child, on the lower half of the breastbone. Depth about 5 cm (one third of the chest depth), rate 100–120 per minute. 30:2 with one rescuer, 15:2 with two rescuers.",critical:!0},{title:"5. AED for children",detail:"For children younger than 8 years, use pediatric pads or a pediatric dose attenuator if available; if not, use adult pads. The pads must not touch each other; if the chest is small, place one on the front of the chest and one on the back (anterior-posterior). The 8-year threshold applies only to the AED dose, not to the definition of a child."},{title:"6. NICU — neonatal resuscitation (NRP)",detail:"Keep the body temperature at 36.5–37.5°C. The first step is positive-pressure ventilation (PPV). If the heart rate is still below 60 after 30 seconds of effective ventilation: chest compressions with two thumbs encircling the chest, to about one third of the chest depth, a ratio of 3:1 (90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute) and 100% oxygen. If the heart rate is still below 60 after 60 seconds of compressions: epinephrine 0.01–0.03 mg/kg IV, repeated every 3–5 minutes if needed. During a fire, move newborns in a crib or on a cart, not in your arms."}],reference:[{heading:"Weight-based drug doses (PALS)",points:["Epinephrine: 0.01 mg/kg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes (maximum 1 mg).","Amiodarone: 5 mg/kg IV/IO for persistent VF or pVT.","Atropine: 0.02 mg/kg IV/IO for vagally mediated bradycardia, after oxygen and ventilation.","Adenosine: 0.1 mg/kg (maximum 6 mg) for SVT.","Glucose: 0.5–1 g/kg if the blood sugar is low (hypoglycemia)."]},{heading:"Danger signs in an infant",points:["Bluish skin (cyanosis): inadequate breathing.","Heart rate below 60 per minute with signs of poor perfusion: start CPR.","Weakness and reduced responsiveness (lethargy).","Weak cry or no cry.","Pause in breathing (apnea) longer than 20 seconds."]},{heading:"Fire in pediatric units",points:["Incubators are electrical devices and contain oxygen; the fire risk is high.","Infants cannot escape on their own; horizontal evacuation comes first.","Several infants can be moved together in one crib or cart.","The oxygen valve is closed only after the infants have been switched to portable oxygen, and on the order of the person in charge.","Move them to a safe compartment on the same floor."]}],warnings:["Do not shake an infant violently. Keep the infant's head in the neutral position; tilting the head too far back blocks the airway. AED pads must not touch each other on a small chest; in that case use anterior-posterior placement. Do not give a child the adult epinephrine dose (1 mg); the dose is weight-based: 0.01 mg/kg."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): In the NICU, a 10-day-old newborn (2.3 kg) with pneumonia stopped breathing at 03:12 and the heart rate slowed. The NICU nurse immediately started positive-pressure ventilation (PPV) with a mask and called a pediatric Code Blue. At 03:13, after 30 seconds of effective ventilation, the heart rate was still below 60; the team started chest compressions with two thumbs at a ratio of 3:1 with 100% oxygen. At 03:14 the heart rate was still below 60, so epinephrine 0.02 mg/kg IV (about 0.05 mg) was given. At 03:16 the heart rate rose above 60 and compressions were stopped; ventilation continued until the heart rate was above 100. The infant stayed in the NICU for several days and then recovered well.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse in the pediatric intensive care unit (PICU). At 02:40 the monitor of a 2-month-old infant shows a slow heart rate (50 per minute). The infant is cyanotic and breathing weakly, but you can feel a brachial pulse. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I immediately start chest compressions without ventilation",correct:!1,feedback:"The infant has a pulse. Under PALS for bradycardia, first open the airway and ventilate with oxygen; if the heart rate stays below 60, compressions are started."},{text:"I open the airway, ventilate with a bag-mask and oxygen and call for help (Code Blue)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: for an infant with a pulse and bradycardia, oxygen and effective ventilation come first (PALS). If the infant is pulseless, CPR starts in the C-A-B sequence."},{text:"I prepare epinephrine",correct:!1,feedback:"Medications come later; oxygen and ventilation come first."},{text:"I call the elevator to move the infant",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving the infant at this point is dangerous; start treatment where the infant is."}]},{prompt:"After effective ventilation with oxygen, the infant's heart rate is still 40 per minute and there are signs of poor perfusion. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I continue ventilation only",correct:!1,feedback:"A heart rate below 60 with poor perfusion, despite good ventilation, requires CPR."},{text:"I start CPR: two thumbs encircling the chest, about 4 cm, at a ratio of 15:2 (two rescuers)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under PALS, a heart rate below 60 with hypoperfusion = CPR. The ratio is 15:2 with two rescuers."},{text:"I give very forceful breaths",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: forceful breaths damage the lungs (barotrauma). CPR is needed."},{text:"I wait for the physician",correct:!1,feedback:"Start CPR; do not wait for the team."}]},{prompt:"The team has arrived. The infant's rhythm changes to VF and a shock is needed. There is no manual defibrillator, and the AED has only adult pads. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not use the AED",correct:!1,feedback:"VF requires a shock; an AED with adult pads is better than no shock at all."},{text:"I apply the adult pads: one on the front of the chest and one on the back (anterior-posterior)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: on a small chest, anterior-posterior placement keeps the pads from touching."},{text:"I place both pads close together on the front of the chest",correct:!1,feedback:"The pads will touch each other and the shock will not be effective."},{text:"I just continue CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"In VF, CPR alone is not enough; a shock is needed."}]}],quiz:[{question:"How is CPR performed on an infant (under 1 year)?",options:[{text:"Two hands on the center of the chest, pressing about 6 cm deep",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too deep for an infant."},{text:"Two thumbs encircling the chest (or the heel of one hand), about 4 cm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under AHA 2025; the two-finger technique is no longer taught."},{text:"One hand, 5 cm",correct:!1,feedback:"This is for children from 1 year to puberty."},{text:"CPR is not performed on infants; only rescue breaths are given until help arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Every infant in cardiac arrest receives CPR."}]},{question:"What is the pediatric epinephrine dose in cardiac arrest?",options:[{text:"1 mg IV/IO every 3–5 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"This is the adult dose; it is dangerous for a child."},{text:"0.01 mg/kg IV/IO",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: weight-based, up to a maximum of 1 mg."},{text:"0.5 mg IV",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too much for an infant."},{text:"0.1 mg/kg IV/IO",correct:!1,feedback:"Ten times the correct dose; dangerous."}]},{question:"For infants and children in cardiac arrest, what is the CPR sequence under AHA?",options:[{text:"B-C-A: rescue breaths first, then chest compressions, then airway",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: AHA uses C-A-B for infants and children. Resuscitation of newly born infants (NRP) is different."},{text:"C-A-B: chest compressions, then airway and breathing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: breaths follow every 30 (or 15) compressions and are very important."},{text:"Epinephrine first",correct:!1,feedback:"Medications come later."},{text:"AED first, then CPR after the first shock",correct:!1,feedback:"CPR starts immediately, and the AED is used as soon as it arrives."}]},{question:"Using an AED on an infant: which pads?",options:[{text:"An AED is never used on an infant; only CPR is given until the ambulance arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; an AED is used when needed."},{text:"Pediatric pads or a dose attenuator; if unavailable, adult pads in the anterior-posterior position",correct:!0,feedback:"Pediatric pads or an attenuator lower the infant's dose; if unavailable, use adult pads front and back so they do not touch."},{text:"Adult pads close together on the front of the chest, as for an adult, so the shock reaches the heart",correct:!1,feedback:"The pads will touch each other."},{text:"Homemade pads",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not what the lesson teaches: use pediatric pads or an attenuator, or adult pads anterior-posterior."}],explanation:"For children under 8, use pediatric pads or a dose attenuator if available; otherwise adult pads. On a small chest, place them anterior-posterior so they do not touch."},{question:"You and a colleague are giving CPR to a 6-month-old infant. Which approach is right?",options:[{text:"Two thumbs encircling the chest, about one third of its depth, 15:2",correct:!0},{text:"Two fingers on the breastbone, as taught in older courses",correct:!1},{text:"The heels of both hands, pressing as deep as for an adult",correct:!1},{text:"Compressions only with no breaths, since infants rarely need them",correct:!1}],explanation:"With two rescuers, infant CPR uses two thumbs encircling the chest (or the heel of one hand), about one third of the chest depth (about 4 cm), at 15:2. The two-finger technique is no longer taught."},{question:"A child's cardiac arrest followed a severe asthma attack. A colleague says compressions alone are enough until the team arrives, although a bag-mask and oxygen are at the bedside. What is right?",options:[{text:"Give compressions with early effective bag-mask breaths and oxygen",correct:!0},{text:"Give compressions only, as a bystander would for an adult, until the team arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Give oxygen by face mask only and wait for the pulse to come back",correct:!1},{text:"Give breaths only after the first AED shock has been delivered",correct:!1}],explanation:"Most cardiac arrests in children come from breathing problems, so children especially need breaths. When a bag-mask and oxygen are at hand, trained staff give compressions with early effective breaths."}],tips:["Know where the pediatric resuscitation equipment is kept on your unit, including child and infant bag-masks and pediatric AED pads.","Use the weight-based dose chart or tool that your hospital approves; do not work out a child's dose from memory under stress.","Practice infant and child CPR on manikins in your unit's drills, so that hand position and depth become automatic.","During a fire, move newborns in a crib or on a cart, not in your arms."],activity:{prompt:"You are a nurse on the pediatric ward. You find a 4-year-old child in bed who is unresponsive and not breathing. A colleague is in the next room. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I shout for my colleague to call a Code Blue and bring the AED, and start CPR at once: compressions first, then breaths (C-A-B)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: do not wait for the team. A child gets CPR in the C-A-B order with early effective breaths, because most child arrests come from breathing problems."},{text:"I give rescue breaths only and wait for the team, because children do not need compressions",correct:!1,feedback:"A child who is unresponsive and not breathing needs full CPR: compressions and breaths. Waiting for the team loses time."},{text:"I shake the child hard to wake them, then wait a few minutes to see if they respond",correct:!1,feedback:"Violent shaking can injure a child, and waiting delays CPR. Check responsiveness gently and start CPR straight away."},{text:"I leave the child to fetch the AED myself before I start anything",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not leave the child: shout for help and start CPR. Someone else brings the AED."}]}},{id:5,code:"O2-05",icon:"Wind",accent:"#0891b2",category:"Safety",title:"Oxygen safety",subtitle:"Cylinders, the medical gas pipeline system (MGPS) and the OEA hazard",summary:"Oxygen saves lives, but it is also dangerous. Oxygen does not burn by itself, but it makes a fire much stronger and faster and lets materials ignite at a lower temperature. A full oxygen cylinder holds very high pressure (often about 200 bar); if its valve breaks off, the cylinder behaves like a rocket. The medical gas pipeline system (MGPS) and cylinders are organized and inspected according to NFPA 99. In the operating room, oxygen, the electrocautery unit and alcohol-based prep solutions together are a major fire hazard.",facts:[{label:"Full cylinder pressure",value:"About 200 bar (2,900 psi)"},{label:"OEA threshold",value:"O₂ above 23.5% (NFPA 99)"},{label:"O₂ cylinder color",value:"White (ISO 32) / green (United States)"},{label:"MGPS standard",value:"NFPA 99"}],steps:[{title:"1. Storing and moving cylinders",detail:'Always move a cylinder on a dedicated cart, never by hand or on your shoulder. Secure cylinders to the wall with a chain or a dedicated restraint so they cannot fall. Keep empty and full cylinders apart and mark them with an "EMPTY" or "FULL" tag.',critical:!0},{title:"2. Oil and grease: two enemies of oxygen",detail:"Do not leave oil or grease on oxygen valves and fittings: it can cause ignition and explosion. Do not touch valves with hands that have cream or oil on them. In the operating room, the alcohol-based skin prep must be completely dry before the electrocautery is used.",critical:!0},{title:"3. MGPS — medical gas pipeline system",detail:"Every unit or floor has a zone oxygen valve (zone valve box) in the corridor. Inspection and maintenance are carried out according to the hospital program and NFPA 99. During a fire, the zone valve is closed only after patients on oxygen have been switched to a portable cylinder or bag-mask, and only on the order of the clinical lead or according to the fire plan.",critical:!0},{title:"4. OEA — recognition and reduction",detail:"If the oxygen concentration in a room is above 23.5%, it is an OEA. Oxygen can pool under drapes and in hair. Use the lowest oxygen concentration needed, allow oxygen to escape from under the drapes and reduce oxygen near the electrocautery and other hot devices.",critical:!0},{title:"5. A fallen cylinder and a broken valve",detail:"If a cylinder falls and its valve breaks off, the gas escapes with great force and the cylinder can fly like a rocket and punch through walls. Do not try to catch it; clear the area and protect yourself. Prevention is the best approach: cylinders are always secured, and the protective cap is on the valve during transport."},{title:"6. Medical gas colors (ISO 32)",detail:"O₂: white. N₂O: blue. CO₂: gray. Helium: brown. Medical air: white and black. Colors differ in some countries (in the United States O₂ is green), so always read the gas name on the cylinder. Do not use an unlabeled cylinder: connecting the wrong gas can kill a patient."}],reference:[{heading:"Oxygen and fire: what happens?",points:["Fire burns much faster and hotter.","Materials that are normally hard to burn ignite easily in an OEA.","Oil or grease in contact with pressurized oxygen causes ignition and explosion.","Fabric, plastic and hair become highly flammable in an OEA.","Materials ignite at a lower temperature."]},{heading:"Daily staff check",points:["Cylinders are secured with a chain or a dedicated restraint.","Gas pressure at the zone valve is normal.","The FULL or EMPTY tag is correct.","There is no oil or grease nearby.","The gas alarms are working."]},{heading:"During a fire in the operating room or ICU",points:["First: rescue patients in danger; connect those on ventilators to a bag-mask or portable oxygen.","Second: move patients to a safe compartment.","Third: close the zone valve only after that, and on the order of the clinical lead or according to the fire plan.","Full spare cylinders are ready for every patient who needs oxygen.","Continuous communication between the anesthesiologist, the surgeon and the nurses."]}],warnings:["Do not leave oil or grease near oxygen, including hand creams and oil-based skin products. In the operating room, the alcohol-based prep solution must be completely dry before electrocautery is used; for ChloraPrep, at least 3 minutes on hairless skin and up to 1 hour in hair. Do not close the zone oxygen valve before patients on oxygen have been switched over."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A storeroom worker was moving a full oxygen cylinder without a cart and without the protective cap. The cylinder fell at a doorway and its valve broke off. The gas escaped at high pressure, and the cylinder shot down the corridor like a rocket, hit a wall and injured several staff members. Had the cylinder been moved on a dedicated cart with its protective cap on, this incident would not have happened.",caseStudy:[{prompt:`You are an operating room nurse. The patient is receiving 100% oxygen. You see that the alcohol-based prep solution is still wet on the patient's skin, and the surgeon says: "Get the electrocautery ready." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I tell the surgeon immediately and ask them to wait until the solution is completely dry (at least 3 minutes for ChloraPrep)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: alcohol, electrocautery and oxygen together = the risk of a rapid fire. Speak up."},{text:"I only reduce the oxygen to 40%",correct:!1,feedback:"This alone is not enough: the wet solution is still flammable, and 40% is still an OEA."},{text:"I blow air on it with a fan so it dries faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: moving air spreads the oxygen; wait for it to dry naturally."},{text:"I do nothing; this is not my job",correct:!1,feedback:"Patient safety is the whole team's responsibility. Speak up!"}]},{prompt:"The surgeon agreed and started the operation once the solution was completely dry. After a while, a small flame appears on the patient's chest. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Before the extinguisher, the oxygen flow to the patient must be stopped."},{text:"I alert the anesthesiologist to stop the O₂ flow to the patient, and I remove the burning drapes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the anesthesiologist controls the patient's gases. Stopping the gas and removing burning materials come before the extinguisher."},{text:"I call another nurse",correct:!1,feedback:"Time is short; the anesthesiologist is in the room and can stop the gas immediately."},{text:"I evacuate the operating room",correct:!1,feedback:"The patient is on the table; first stop the oxygen and put out the fire."}]},{prompt:"The O₂ has been stopped and saline has been poured on, but the fire is still burning. Which extinguisher is suitable in the operating room?",options:[{text:"An ABC powder extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"The powder contaminates the patient's wound and airway and damages the equipment."},{text:"A CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: ASA and ECRI recommend a CO₂ extinguisher for the operating room."},{text:"Water on the electrical equipment",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the equipment is energized."},{text:"I wait for the firefighting team and do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"The fire is on the patient and time is very short. Use CO₂ and announce Code Red."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What does oxygen do to a fire?",options:[{text:"It puts the fire out",correct:!1,feedback:"The opposite: oxygen does not burn by itself, but it makes a fire much stronger and faster."},{text:"It makes the fire much stronger and faster",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oxygen is an oxidizer."},{text:"It has no effect, because oxygen itself does not burn",correct:!1,feedback:"It has a major effect."},{text:"It cools the fire and slows down how fast it spreads",correct:!1,feedback:"The opposite: with more oxygen, fire burns faster and hotter, and materials ignite at a lower temperature."}],explanation:"Oxygen does not burn by itself, but as an oxidizer it makes fire much stronger and faster and lets materials ignite at a lower temperature."},{question:"What happens if an oxygen cylinder falls and its valve breaks off?",options:[{text:"It is a minor problem",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; it is a deadly hazard."},{text:"The cylinder can fly like a rocket and kill people",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: that is why cylinders must be secured and have the protective cap on."},{text:"The pressure drops by itself and there is no danger",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; high pressure produces a great force."},{text:"Only opening a window is needed",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough; clear the area."}]},{question:"What can oil or grease in contact with pressurized oxygen cause?",options:[{text:"It has no effect",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; there is a risk of ignition and explosion."},{text:"Ignition or explosion",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oil near pressurized oxygen can ignite spontaneously."},{text:"The oil dries out",correct:!1,feedback:"Oil or grease does not dry near pressurized oxygen; it can ignite and explode."},{text:"It changes the color of the cylinder",correct:!1,feedback:"Color is not the issue: oil or grease in contact with pressurized oxygen causes ignition and explosion."}],explanation:"Oil or grease in contact with pressurized oxygen can ignite and explode, so keep valves and fittings free of oil, grease and hand cream."},{question:"What is the OEA threshold under NFPA 99?",options:[{text:"O₂ above 21%",correct:!1,feedback:"Normal air contains about 20.9% oxygen."},{text:"O₂ above 23.5%",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Oxygen-Enriched Atmosphere."},{text:"O₂ above 50%",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; an OEA begins well below 50%."},{text:"O₂ below 19.5%",correct:!1,feedback:"That is oxygen deficiency, not an OEA."}]},{question:"A full oxygen cylinder falls in the corridor and its valve breaks off. Gas is escaping with a loud noise. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not try to catch it: I clear the area, protect myself and report it",correct:!0},{text:"I grab the cylinder and hold it down until the gas has run out",correct:!1},{text:"I try to screw the broken valve back on as quickly as I can",correct:!1},{text:"I stand the cylinder upright again and chain it to the wall",correct:!1}],explanation:"A cylinder with a broken valve can fly like a rocket and punch through walls. Do not go near it: clear the area, protect yourself and report it according to your hospital's plan."},{question:"A patient needs oxygen now. In the store room you find a cylinder labeled oxygen and a white cylinder with no label. Which do you take?",options:[{text:"The cylinder labeled oxygen; a cylinder with no label is never used",correct:!0},{text:"The white one, because white means oxygen under ISO 32",correct:!1},{text:"Whichever is fuller, after I check both pressure gauges",correct:!1},{text:"The unlabeled one, after I briefly smell the gas to check it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Colors differ between countries and can be wrong, so always read the gas name on the label. Connecting the wrong gas can kill a patient."}],tips:["Never leave an oxygen cylinder standing unsecured, even for a moment: chain it, put it in its holder or keep it on its cart.","Ask patients on oxygen and their visitors not to smoke or use lighters, and not to put oil-based creams on their hands or face near the oxygen.","Always read the gas name on the cylinder label before you connect it; do not rely on the color.","Close the cylinder valve when the oxygen is not in use, and report a hissing sound or a burning smell near oxygen equipment to the person in charge at once."],activity:{prompt:"You are a porter taking a patient on oxygen to the X-ray department. The cylinder is lying loose on the bed beside the patient's legs, and a relative has just rubbed hand cream on the patient's hands. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I secure the cylinder in a holder or on a cart before moving, and ask for the cream to be wiped off before anyone handles the oxygen fittings",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a loose cylinder can fall and its valve can break, and oil or grease near oxygen fittings can cause ignition. Keep the oxygen running as prescribed."},{text:"I carry the cylinder on my shoulder so that it does not roll around on the bed",correct:!1,feedback:"A cylinder is never carried by hand or on the shoulder: move it on a dedicated cart or in a holder."},{text:"I leave everything as it is, because the trip to X-ray is short",correct:!1,feedback:"A loose cylinder can fall on the way and its valve can break, and the cream stays near the oxygen. A short trip does not remove the danger."},{text:"I turn the oxygen off for the whole trip so that the cylinder is safe",correct:!1,feedback:"Prescribed oxygen is not stopped without a clinical order: the patient can be harmed. Secure the cylinder instead."}]}},{id:6,code:"OR-06",icon:"Stethoscope",accent:"#7c3aed",category:"Safety",title:"Fire safety in the operating room (OR)",subtitle:"The fire triangle in the operating room and the fire time-out",summary:"The operating room (OR) is one of the highest-risk places for fire in a hospital, because all three sides of the fire triangle are present together: 1) oxygen or N₂O, 2) fuels (alcohol-based prep solutions, drapes, hair), 3) ignition sources (electrocautery, laser, drills). Surgical fires occur most often in head, neck and chest surgery. Under AORN and ASA guidance, the fire risk must be assessed before every operation.",facts:[{label:"Fire triangle",value:"Oxygen + fuel + ignition source"},{label:"Open oxygen near the head",value:"30% or less if the patient can tolerate it (APSF)"},{label:"High-risk procedures",value:"Head, neck, chest"},{label:"Standard",value:"AORN + ASA + ECRI"}],steps:[{title:"1. Fire time-out before surgery",detail:"Before starting, the team asks three questions: 1) How much oxygen or N₂O is in use, and is it near the surgical site? 2) What is the ignition source? 3) Where are the fuels (alcohol, drapes)? If all three are present, the procedure is high risk and needs a specific plan.",critical:!0},{title:"2. Letting alcohol-based prep dry",detail:"Before electrocautery, the alcohol-based skin prep must be completely dry according to the manufacturer's instructions: for ChloraPrep, at least 3 minutes on hairless skin and up to 1 hour in hair. Do not let the solution pool under the patient or in the drapes.",critical:!0},{title:"3. Oxygen control",detail:"For head and neck surgery, if the patient can tolerate it, open oxygen delivery (by mask or nasal cannula) should be 30% or less. If more oxygen is needed, secure the airway with an ETT or LMA. Before using an ignition source near the airway, reduce or stop the oxygen and wait about 1 minute.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Controlling ignition sources",detail:"When the electrocautery is not in use, keep it in its protective holster, not on the drapes. Laser: announce before use and move fuels away. Do not leave the tip of the fiber-optic light cable on the drapes while it is on. Do not place hot devices on flammable materials.",critical:!0},{title:"5. If a fire breaks out on the patient",detail:"1. Stop: halt the procedure and announce the fire. 2. The anesthesiologist stops the flow of O₂ and N₂O. 3. Remove the drapes and burning materials and put out the fire with saline. 4. If it persists: a CO₂ extinguisher. 5. If it is not extinguished: Code Red and evacuate the patient.",critical:!0},{title:"6. Airway fire",detail:"A fire inside the endotracheal tube (ETT). At the same time: 1) stop the flow of all gases and disconnect the circuit, 2) remove the ETT, 3) pour saline into the airway. Then: 4) ventilate by mask, and do not use supplemental oxygen until you are sure no fire remains, 5) examine the airway by bronchoscopy."}],reference:[{heading:"The fire triangle in the OR",points:["Oxidizer: O₂ (especially in an OEA) or N₂O.","Fuel: alcohol, ChloraPrep, drapes, gauze, the patient's hair.","Ignition source: electrocautery, laser, drill, light cable.","If one of the three sides is missing, no fire can start."]},{heading:"High-risk procedures",points:["Tracheostomy: oxygen + ETT + electrocautery.","Eye surgery under local anesthesia with open oxygen.","ENT (head and neck): oxygen very close to the surgical site.","Oral surgery: oxygen inside the mouth.","Cosmetic surgery of the head and face: ChloraPrep + electrocautery."]},{heading:"Fire time-out (per AORN)",points:['Surgeon: "What is the oxygen level? What is the ignition source?"','Anesthesiologist: "Oxygen is at 21%; I am ready to stop it."','Nurse: "The prep solution is completely dry."',"Record the result on the operating room board.","Repeat it for every operation, not just once."]}],warnings:["The fire time-out is the whole team's job: surgeon, anesthesiologist and nurse. If any one of them is not satisfied, the operation does not start until the risk is resolved. In an airway fire, stopping the gases and removing the ETT come first, not the extinguisher."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): During a tonsillectomy on a 7-year-old child, the 100% oxygen had not been reduced before electrocautery was used. The electrocautery ignited near the airway and the ETT caught fire (airway fire). The team did not stop the gases or remove the ETT, and brought an ABC extinguisher instead. The child suffered severe burns to the airway and mouth. The correct procedure: simultaneously stop the gases, remove the ETT and pour in saline. Lesson: reduce oxygen before electrocautery, and train regularly on the airway fire protocol.",caseStudy:[{prompt:`You are an operating room nurse. An ENT operation on a 6-year-old child is about to start. The surgeon says: "We don't need a fire time-out; this is a routine operation." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I accept it; the surgeon is the expert",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the time-out is required (AORN), and you are also responsible for the patient's safety."},{text:'I say respectfully: "Under the National Patient Safety Goals (NPSG), the time-out is required; it only takes a few seconds."',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: speak up. ENT procedures with oxygen and electrocautery are high risk."},{text:"Without telling the surgeon, I go straight to the nursing supervisor",correct:!1,feedback:"That may be needed later, but first speak directly with the surgeon."},{text:"I say nothing and carry on with my work",correct:!1,feedback:"Silence puts the patient at risk; you need to speak up."}]},{prompt:'The time-out has been done. Oxygen has been reduced to 30% and ChloraPrep has been applied. After 30 seconds the surgeon says: "Get the electrocautery ready." The solution is still wet. What do you do?',options:[{text:"I agree, because there is a lot of work to do",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a fire risk: ChloraPrep needs at least 3 minutes to dry completely."},{text:"I ask them to wait until the solution is completely dry (at least 3 minutes, per the manufacturer's instructions)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a wet alcohol-based solution is a fuel."},{text:"I set up a fan so it dries faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: moving air spreads the oxygen."},{text:"I work quickly and do not wait",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a fire risk."}]},{prompt:"The operation has started. After 20 minutes, fire appears inside the ETT. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I pick up the extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Before the extinguisher, the gases must be stopped and the ETT removed."},{text:"At the same time, we stop the gas flow and remove the ETT, then pour saline into the airway",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the airway fire protocol (ASA)."},{text:"I only reduce the oxygen",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough; the gases must be stopped completely and the ETT removed."},{text:"I wait for the Code Red team",correct:!1,feedback:"There is no time; the operating room team must act immediately."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What three elements make up the fire triangle in the operating room?",options:[{text:"Heat, alcohol, fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; fire is the result, not a side of the triangle."},{text:"Oxygen, fuels, ignition source",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oxidizer + fuel + ignition."},{text:"Water, medical gases, electrical current from devices",correct:!1,feedback:"These are not the three sides; in the OR they are an oxidizer, fuels and an ignition source."},{text:"Physician, nurse, equipment",correct:!1,feedback:"These are the team, not the triangle; but the fire time-out is the whole team's job."}],explanation:"In the OR, fire needs an oxidizer (O₂ or N₂O), a fuel (prep solution, drapes, hair) and an ignition source (electrocautery, laser); if one side is missing, no fire can start."},{question:"How long does ChloraPrep need to dry before electrocautery?",options:[{text:"30 seconds on any skin, then electrocautery can start",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too short."},{text:"At least 3 minutes on hairless skin (up to 1 hour in hair)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to the manufacturer's instructions (ChloraPrep IFU)."},{text:"It does not need to dry if the oxygen is turned down below 30%",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a wet solution is always flammable."},{text:"There is no need to wait",correct:!1,feedback:"A wet alcohol-based prep is fuel; wait until it is completely dry before electrocautery."}],explanation:"An alcohol-based prep is fuel until it is completely dry. For ChloraPrep, the manufacturer requires at least 3 minutes on hairless skin and up to 1 hour in hair before electrocautery."},{question:"In an airway fire, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"Use a clean agent extinguisher first, then stop the gases",correct:!1,feedback:"Later, if needed."},{text:"Stop the gases, disconnect the circuit and remove the ETT",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the airway fire protocol."},{text:"Only reduce the FiO₂ and carry on with the operation",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too little."},{text:"Start CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"CPR does not stop an airway fire; stop the gases, disconnect the circuit, remove the ETT and pour in saline."}],explanation:"In an airway fire, at the same time stop all gases and disconnect the circuit, remove the ETT and pour saline into the airway; the extinguisher is not the first step."},{question:"Which procedure carries a high fire risk?",options:[{text:"Appendectomy and other abdominal procedures",correct:!1,feedback:"Its risk is lower."},{text:"Tracheostomy and ENT procedures",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oxygen and electrocautery near the airway."},{text:"Hernia repair",correct:!1,feedback:"Its risk is lower."},{text:"C-section under spinal anesthesia",correct:!1,feedback:"Its risk is lower."}]},{question:"Flames appear on the drapes during neck surgery. Which order is right?",options:[{text:"Stop and announce, gases off, remove burning drapes, saline, then CO₂ if needed",correct:!0},{text:"CO₂ extinguisher first, then gases off, then announce the fire to the team",correct:!1},{text:"Move the patient out of the room first, then stop the gases and the surgery",correct:!1},{text:"Increase ventilation with oxygen, then pour saline over the burning drapes",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a fire on the patient: stop the procedure and announce the fire, the anesthesiologist stops O₂ and N₂O, remove the drapes and burning materials and use saline, then a CO₂ extinguisher if the fire persists, and Code Red if it does not go out."},{question:"The surgeon will use the electrocautery near the airway in head and neck surgery. Which action removes one side of the fire triangle?",options:[{text:"Reducing or stopping the open oxygen and waiting before using it",correct:!0},{text:"Turning up the room's air conditioning to cool the surgical field",correct:!1},{text:"Applying more alcohol-based prep so the skin is cleaned better",correct:!1},{text:"Resting the active cautery tip on the drapes between uses",correct:!1}],explanation:"The fire triangle needs oxygen, fuel and an ignition source. Reducing or stopping the oxygen and waiting before an ignition source is used near the airway removes the oxidizer side."}],tips:["Speak up in the fire time-out: any member of the team can stop the start of surgery until the risk is resolved.","Keep the electrocautery in its holster whenever it is not in use, never on the drapes.","Before every high-risk case, know where the saline and the CO₂ extinguisher are, and have them ready.","Arrange the drapes so that oxygen can escape and does not collect under them near the surgical site."],activity:{prompt:"You are the scrub nurse in eye surgery under local anesthesia. The patient gets oxygen through an open face mask under the drapes. The surgeon asks to use the electrocautery near the face. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I speak up and ask the anesthesiologist to reduce or stop the open oxygen and wait before the cautery is used",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: oxygen, drapes and the cautery complete the fire triangle. Reducing or stopping the oxygen and waiting before the cautery is used near the airway removes one side of the triangle."},{text:"I say nothing, because the oxygen is under the drapes and away from the cautery",correct:!1,feedback:"Oxygen collects under drapes and creates an oxygen-enriched atmosphere right at the surgical site. Anyone in the team must speak up."},{text:"I ask for more oxygen so that the patient stays comfortable during the cautery",correct:!1,feedback:"More oxygen makes any fire stronger and faster. Use the lowest oxygen the patient needs."},{text:"I pour saline over the drapes in advance so that they cannot catch fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Wet drapes do not remove the oxygen or the ignition source, and fluid can pool under the patient. Control the oxygen and the cautery instead."}]}},{id:7,code:"EVAC-07",icon:"DoorOpen",accent:"#9333ea",category:"Emergency",title:"Hospital evacuation — RACE",subtitle:"The evacuation plan for patients who cannot escape on their own",summary:"Evacuating a hospital is different from evacuating other buildings: patients on ventilators and in the NICU and ICU cannot leave immediately. Under NFPA 101 (the Life Safety Code), a hospital works at three levels: 1) defend in place, 2) horizontal evacuation to a smoke compartment on the same floor, 3) vertical evacuation to a lower floor. Full evacuation of the building is the last resort.",facts:[{label:"Level 1 — defend in place",value:"Close the doors and stand ready"},{label:"Level 2 — horizontal",value:"To a safe compartment on the same floor"},{label:"Level 3 — vertical",value:"To a lower floor"},{label:"Full evacuation",value:"Last resort"}],steps:[{title:"R — Rescue (rescuing patients)",detail:"First move patients near the fire and smoke to a safe compartment. Then follow the plan: patients who can walk, those in wheelchairs, those in beds, and last ICU patients and those on ventilators, because they need a team and preparation. Never leave a patient alone.",critical:!0},{title:"A — Alarm",detail:"Push the fire alarm and call the internal emergency number; the hospital notifies 115. Code Red is announced over the public address system, the incident command system (ICS) is activated and all staff are alerted.",critical:!0},{title:"C — Contain",detail:"Close the room doors and the smoke compartment doors. The zone oxygen valve is closed only after patients on oxygen have been switched to a portable cylinder or bag-mask, and on the order of the person in charge. The ventilation system (HVAC) is controlled by the engineering team according to the fire plan.",critical:!0},{title:"E — Extinguish or Evacuate",detail:"If the fire is small (no larger than a wastebasket), you are trained, the alarm has been raised and your exit is behind you: the appropriate extinguisher; if it does not go out at once, leave and close the door. If it is large: horizontal evacuation. Each floor is divided into two or more smoke compartments by smoke doors; patients move from the fire compartment to the safe compartment."},{title:"Horizontal evacuation — three methods",detail:`Sheet drag: the patient lies in bed and two people pull.
• Wheelchair: for patients who cannot walk far.
• Moving the bed: two people, release the wheel locks, one at the front and one at the back.`,critical:!0},{title:"Vertical evacuation — four methods",detail:`Cradle carry: for children and infants.
• Two-person seat carry: for a conscious patient who cannot walk.
• Stair chair: for bedbound patients, two people.
• Evac sled: for ICU patients and patients on ventilators, 3–4 people.`}],reference:[{heading:"Evacuation priority",points:["1. Anyone in immediate danger (near the fire and smoke).","2. Patients who can walk, with guidance.","3. Patients in wheelchairs.","4. Bedbound patients: by sheet or in their bed.","5. ICU and ventilated patients: bag-mask and 3 or more people.","6. NICU infants: several infants together in one crib or cart."]},{heading:"What must not be done",points:["Do not use the elevator; it may stop.","Do not move critically ill patients without a plan and equipment.","Do not leave a patient alone.","Do not open windows without a reason.","Do not carry personal belongings or valuables.","Do not go back into the fire compartment."]},{heading:"Smoke compartments",points:["Each floor is divided into two or more compartments by smoke walls and smoke doors (NFPA 101).","Smoke doors close automatically when the fire alarm sounds.","The size of each compartment is limited by NFPA 101.","One compartment is the fire compartment; the other is the safe compartment.","Horizontal evacuation moves toward the safe compartment."]}],warnings:["Do not use elevators during a fire: they may stop and trap people inside. Use vertical evacuation only when horizontal evacuation is not enough. Do not go back into the fire compartment without permission from the 115 team and the incident commander."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A fire broke out in a storeroom on the first floor of a hospital. There were 140 patients on four floors. The incident command system (ICS) was activated quickly. Most patients were moved horizontally to safe compartments on the same floor, and only the patients near the storeroom were moved vertically. Four ICU patients on ventilators were moved with a bag-mask and three people for each patient. No one died. Two reasons for the success: regular ICS training, and smoke doors that worked properly.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse on the third-floor ICU. A Code Red has been announced in a storeroom at the end of the corridor on the same floor. You have eight patients: four in the ICU (two on ventilators) and four general patients. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I move all the patients vertically to the first floor",correct:!1,feedback:"Horizontal evacuation comes first and is much faster; vertical only if horizontal is not enough."},{text:"Using RACE: I rescue the patients near the fire and prepare the safe compartment behind the smoke doors on the same floor, while raising the alarm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Rescue and horizontal evacuation. Move the patients to the safe compartment."},{text:"I leave on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: protecting the patients is your responsibility."},{text:"I wait for the 115 team",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not wait; the patients are in danger."}]},{prompt:"The other compartment of the third floor is safe. Two patients are on ventilators. Which patients are moved first?",options:[{text:"The patients who can walk, because they are easier",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: risk is assessed first, not ease."},{text:"The ICU patients on ventilators: 3 people, a bag-mask and an evac sled",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the correct way to move them, but they do not go first if other patients are closer to the fire."},{text:"The patients closest to the smoke and fire first, then in the order set by the plan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Rescue begins with those in immediate danger."},{text:"All patients at the same time",correct:!1,feedback:"That is not realistic; priorities are needed."}]},{prompt:"All patients have been moved and you are in the safe compartment. The 115 team has arrived. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I go home",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: you must inform the team and protect the patients."},{text:"I count the patients and brief the 115 team and the incident commander: which patients are where and what is needed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: patient accountability and a short report to the team."},{text:"I go back to the fire compartment",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back without permission is prohibited."},{text:"I take the patients outside the building",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not necessary if the internal safe compartment is protected."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the first level of evacuation?",options:[{text:"Full evacuation of the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the last resort."},{text:"Defend in place",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: protect first, then move if necessary."},{text:"Vertical evacuation",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the third level."},{text:"Moving people outside the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the last resort."}]},{question:"During an evacuation, which patients are moved first?",options:[{text:"ICU patients, because they are the most critically ill",correct:!1,feedback:"They are in the priority order, but they do not go first unless they are in immediate danger."},{text:"Patients in immediate danger (near the fire)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Rescue."},{text:"Patients who can walk, because they can get out fastest",correct:!1,feedback:"After those in immediate danger."},{text:"Valuables",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: life comes first."}]},{question:"How is an ICU patient on a ventilator moved?",options:[{text:"One person and a wheelchair, with the ventilator on the patient's lap",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too few."},{text:"3 people, a bag-mask and an evac sled or the bed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a team is needed."},{text:"Only by elevator, on the bed with the ventilator running",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are prohibited during a fire."},{text:"I leave the patient where they are",correct:!1,feedback:"Never leave a patient alone; ventilated patients are moved last, but with a bag-mask and a team of 3."}],explanation:"A ventilated ICU patient needs a team: a bag-mask and at least 3 people, with an evac sled or the bed. Never leave a patient alone."},{question:"What is a smoke compartment?",options:[{text:"A room where smoke collects and is then removed by exhaust fans",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; it is a section designed to stop smoke from spreading."},{text:"A section of a floor separated by smoke walls and smoke doors",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: each floor has two or more compartments."},{text:"A smoke extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"It is not equipment but a section of a floor, enclosed by smoke walls and smoke doors."},{text:"The ventilation system that pushes smoke out of the building",correct:!1,feedback:"The ventilation system (HVAC) is controlled by engineering; a smoke compartment is a floor section behind smoke doors."}],explanation:"Under NFPA 101, each floor is divided into two or more smoke compartments by smoke walls and smoke doors, so patients can move horizontally from the fire compartment to the safe one."},{question:"Patients have reached the safe compartment. A visitor wants to go back to the room to fetch a bag. What do you say?",options:[{text:"No one goes back without permission from 115 and the incident commander",correct:!0},{text:"They can go if they are quick and keep low under the smoke",correct:!1},{text:"They can go once the smoke doors have been opened to clear the air",correct:!1},{text:"They can go if a member of staff goes in with them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Smoke and fire can spread quickly, and the smoke doors must stay closed. Nobody goes back into the fire compartment without permission from the 115 team and the incident commander."},{question:"Horizontal evacuation is complete, but smoke starts to come into the safe compartment too. What is the next step?",options:[{text:"Vertical evacuation by the stairs, on the incident commander's order",correct:!0},{text:"Gather everyone in the elevator lobby and wait for the elevators",correct:!1},{text:"Go back to the first compartment, as the fire may be out by now",correct:!1},{text:"Open all the windows and keep everyone where they are now",correct:!1}],explanation:"When horizontal evacuation is not enough, patients are moved vertically to a lower floor by the stairs, following the incident commander. Elevators are not used."}],tips:["Know where your unit's smoke compartment doors are and which compartment is the safe one for each part of the floor.","Keep an up-to-date list of patients who need help to move: on ventilators or oxygen, in beds or in wheelchairs.","Know where the evacuation equipment is kept (evac sleds, stair chairs, portable cylinders, bag-masks) and practice with it in drills.","In the safe compartment, count your patients against your list and report anyone missing to the person in charge at once."],activity:{prompt:"You are the charge nurse on a surgical ward. Smoke is coming from a linen room in your compartment and horizontal evacuation has been ordered. Your patients: two can walk, one is in a wheelchair and one is in bed on oxygen. A colleague suggests taking everyone down in the bed elevator. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I move patients through the smoke doors to the safe compartment on our floor, those nearest the smoke first, with the oxygen patient on a portable cylinder",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the order is horizontal evacuation to a safe compartment on the same floor, starting with those in immediate danger. The patient on oxygen moves with a portable cylinder."},{text:"I agree to use the bed elevator, because it is the fastest way to move the bed patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are not used during a fire: they may stop at the fire floor or fill with smoke."},{text:"I move the patients who can walk and leave the oxygen patient in bed with the door closed",correct:!1,feedback:"Never leave a patient alone. The patient in bed is moved in the bed or by sheet, on a portable cylinder."},{text:"I take everyone straight down the stairs to the ground floor",correct:!1,feedback:"Vertical evacuation is used only when horizontal evacuation is not enough, and on the incident commander's order."}]}},{id:8,code:"ELEV-08",icon:"ArrowUpDown",accent:"#64748b",category:"Safety",title:"Elevator rules in the hospital",subtitle:"Use, entrapment in an elevator and the ban during fires",summary:"Elevators in a hospital are important for moving patients and equipment, but they carry their own risks. Under ASME A17.1 and NFPA 101, during a fire elevators automatically go to a designated floor, and only the firefighting team (115) uses them, with a special key (firefighter service mode). Being trapped in an elevator is generally not life-threatening if people stay calm and call for help the right way; there is air inside the elevator.",facts:[{label:"Use during a fire",value:"Prohibited (NFPA 101)"},{label:"Firefighter service mode",value:"The 115 team only"},{label:"When trapped",value:"Stay calm: there is air inside"},{label:"Alarm and emergency phone",value:"Required (ASME A17.1)"}],steps:[{title:"1. Normal use",detail:"Patients on beds and in wheelchairs go first. Two staff members for every bedbound patient. Do not load more than the weight shown on the elevator capacity plate."},{title:"2. During a fire — prohibited",detail:"During a Code Red, elevators automatically go to the designated floor (Phase I recall). After that, only the 115 team can use them, with a special key (Phase II). Staff and visitors use the exit stairs.",critical:!0},{title:"3. Entrapment — first steps",detail:"Stay calm; there is air in the elevator. If a patient is with you, first check their condition (Code Blue if needed). Then press the alarm button and call for help on the elevator phone.",critical:!0},{title:"4. The elevator phone",detail:"Every elevator must have an emergency communication system (ASME A17.1) that is answered 24 hours a day: by the maintenance team or security. Say: which elevator, between which floors, how many people and whether anyone is injured.",critical:!0},{title:"5. What is prohibited while trapped",detail:`Do not force the doors open: you could fall into the elevator shaft.
• Do not climb out through the roof hatch of the car: deadly danger.
• Do not panic; the air will not run out.
• Save your phone battery for necessary calls.`,critical:!0},{title:"6. Rescue from an elevator",detail:"Only the trained maintenance team or the 115 team, not staff or visitors. Procedure: 1) the elevator is brought to the nearest floor, 2) the door is opened with a special key, 3) the people get out safely, 4) the elevator is inspected before it is used again."}],reference:[{heading:"Phase I and Phase II",points:["Phase I (recall): during a fire, the elevator automatically goes to the designated floor.","Phase II (service): only the 115 team, with a special key.","For staff and visitors: completely prohibited.","Use the exit stairs only."]},{heading:"Elevator buttons and devices",points:["Alarm: the alarm bell.","Phone: emergency phone answered 24 hours a day.","Stop: only when necessary and with instructions.","Door hold: only for loading and moving beds.","Fire service key: the 115 team only."]},{heading:"Moving special patients",points:["ICU and ventilated patients: a cart, a bag-mask and a spare battery.","Trauma patients: a dedicated stretcher and two people.","Bariatric patients: the dedicated large elevator.","Wheeled stretcher: two people and the wheel locks.","Wheelchair: one person is enough."]}],warnings:["Do not use the elevator during a fire: it may stop at the fire floor, or smoke may enter its shaft. Phase I recall is automatic. If you are trapped in an elevator, stay calm, use the alarm button and the elevator phone and do not force the doors open."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): At 22:30, three staff members were in an elevator with a 75-year-old bedbound patient. The power went out and the elevator stopped between the second and third floors. One staff member immediately pressed the alarm button and alerted the maintenance team on the elevator phone. The patient had diabetes; the staff checked his condition and blood glucose every 5 minutes. The maintenance team arrived 22 minutes later and everyone got out safely. Had the doors been forced open, there would have been a risk of falling into the elevator shaft.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse in an elevator with an 80-year-old patient on a bed. The elevator has stopped and the power is out, but the emergency lighting (UPS) is working. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I force the doors open",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: you could fall into the elevator shaft."},{text:"I check the patient's condition: are they conscious? How are their airway, breathing and circulation (ABC)?",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the patient's safety comes first."},{text:"I immediately press the alarm button and do not check the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Raising the alarm is important and comes early, but a quick check of the patient comes first."},{text:"I climb out through the roof hatch of the car",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger."}]},{prompt:"The patient is conscious and calm, and the ABCs are normal. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I press the alarm button and call for help on the elevator phone",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: alarm and phone. The maintenance team answers 24 hours a day."},{text:"I do nothing and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Without an alarm, no one knows you are trapped."},{text:"I only call a friend on my personal phone",correct:!1,feedback:"The elevator phone is connected directly to the maintenance team and is better."},{text:"I press the floor buttons",correct:!1,feedback:"The elevator does not work without power; this does no good."}]},{prompt:`The maintenance team says: "We'll be there in another 15 minutes." The patient is still stable. What do you do until the team arrives?`,options:[{text:"I climb out through the roof hatch of the car",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger."},{text:"I force the doors open",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: there is a risk of falling."},{text:"I check the patient every 5 minutes (vital signs), talk with them and keep them calm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: monitoring and emotional support."},{text:"I talk on my phone nonstop until the battery runs out",correct:!1,feedback:"Save the battery; the elevator phone is more important."}]}],quiz:[{question:"During a fire, what is the rule for elevators?",options:[{text:"Use them to speed up the evacuation of bedridden patients",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: they are prohibited."},{text:"They are prohibited; only the 115 team uses them",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: NFPA 101."},{text:"Only for patients",correct:!1,feedback:"Not for patients either: during a fire, only the 115 team uses the elevators."},{text:"Allowed until Code Red is officially announced",correct:!1,feedback:"The ban applies throughout the fire, not only after the announcement; staff and visitors use the exit stairs."}],explanation:"During a fire, elevators go automatically to the designated floor (Phase I) and only the 115 team uses them with a special key (Phase II). Staff and visitors use the exit stairs."},{question:"When trapped in an elevator, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"Forcing the doors open",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger."},{text:"Climbing out through the roof hatch of the car",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger."},{text:"Checking the patient, then raising the alarm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: ABC comes first."},{text:"Panicking",correct:!1,feedback:"Panic does not help, and the air will not run out; check the patient, then use the alarm and the phone."}],explanation:"There is air in the elevator, so stay calm: first check the patient with you (Code Blue if needed), then press the alarm button and use the elevator phone."},{question:"What is Phase I recall?",options:[{text:"The 115 team uses the elevator with a special key to reach the fire floor",correct:!1,feedback:"That is Phase II."},{text:"During a fire, the elevator automatically goes to the designated floor",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: it is automatic."},{text:"Rescuing people trapped in the elevator when the power fails",correct:!1,feedback:"Rescue from an elevator is done by the trained maintenance team or the 115 team; Phase I is the automatic recall."},{text:"Staff training",correct:!1,feedback:"Phase I is not training but the automatic return of the elevator to the designated floor in a fire."}],explanation:"Phase I recall is automatic: during a fire, the elevator goes to the designated floor. Phase II is the 115 team's use of the elevator with a special key."},{question:"What applies to the phone inside an elevator?",options:[{text:"It is not required",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: it is required (ASME A17.1)."},{text:"It is required and is answered 24 hours a day",correct:!0,feedback:"Every elevator must have an emergency phone (ASME A17.1), answered 24 hours a day by maintenance or security."},{text:"It is required, but answered only during working hours",correct:!1,feedback:"It must be answered 24 hours a day, not only in working hours: entrapment can happen at any time."},{text:"Only for the maintenance team to call the control room",correct:!1,feedback:"It is for everyone."}],explanation:"ASME A17.1 requires an emergency communication system in every elevator, answered 24 hours a day by the maintenance team or security."},{question:"A Code Red is announced while you wait for an elevator with a patient in a wheelchair. The elevator arrives and its doors open. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not use it: I follow the evacuation plan by the stairs or to the safe compartment",correct:!0},{text:"I use it quickly before the recall starts, since it has already arrived",correct:!1},{text:"I use it to go down one floor only, then change to the stairs",correct:!1},{text:"I use it but keep holding the door-open button the whole way",correct:!1}],explanation:"During a Code Red elevators are recalled automatically and only the 115 team uses them. An elevator may stop at the fire floor or fill with smoke. Use the stairs and the evacuation plan."},{question:"You are trapped in an elevator with a colleague and a patient. Maintenance says they are on their way. Your colleague wants to open the doors because the car looks level with a floor. What is right?",options:[{text:"Keep the doors closed: only maintenance or 115 opens them with a special key",correct:!0},{text:"Open them, because the car looks level with the floor outside",correct:!1},{text:"Open them a little to let more fresh air into the car",correct:!1},{text:"Open them if the patient starts to feel anxious or upset",correct:!1}],explanation:"The car can move without warning, and a gap can drop a person into the shaft. Only the trained maintenance team or the 115 team opens the doors, using a special key."}],tips:["Read the capacity plate and do not overload the elevator with beds, staff and equipment.","Learn where the alarm button and the emergency phone are in the elevators you use with patients.","When a Code Red is announced, do not wait for an elevator: use the exit stairs and follow your unit's evacuation plan.","Before moving a patient on oxygen in an elevator, check that the portable cylinder has enough oxygen for a possible delay."],activity:{prompt:"You are moving a patient on oxygen in an elevator. It stops between floors and switches to emergency lighting. The patient is anxious, and the portable cylinder is the only oxygen supply. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I check the patient and the oxygen, then use the alarm and the elevator phone and say that the patient is on oxygen",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: check the patient first (Code Blue if needed), then call for help. Give the elevator, the floors, the number of people and the patient's condition, including the oxygen, so the responders know how urgent it is."},{text:"I force the doors open by hand so that we can climb out to the floor above",correct:!1,feedback:"Forcing the doors can drop you into the shaft. Only the trained maintenance team or 115 opens the doors."},{text:"I climb out through the roof hatch to go and find help",correct:!1,feedback:"Climbing out of the roof hatch is a deadly danger. Stay in the car and use the alarm and the phone."},{text:"I wait quietly without calling anyone, to save my phone battery",correct:!1,feedback:"The elevator alarm and phone are there to be used at once. Saving your phone battery does not mean not calling for help."}]}},{id:9,code:"SHOCK-09",icon:"Zap",accent:"#eab308",category:"Safety",title:"Electric shock and LOTO",subtitle:"Lockout/tagout, responding to electric shock, macroshock and microshock",summary:"A hospital has hundreds of electrical devices: monitors, ventilators, pumps, defibrillators and MRI. A current of about 50 mA passing through the skin (macroshock) can be fatal. Patients with catheters or pacemaker wires that lead directly to the heart are sensitive to far smaller currents as well (microshock); that is why the permitted leakage current limit for devices connected to the heart is 10 μA. Under NFPA 99 and OSHA 1910.147, lockout/tagout (LOTO) is required for every repair of an electrical device.",facts:[{label:"Dangerous macroshock",value:"About 50 mA through the skin"},{label:"Leakage current limit",value:"10 μA (for devices connected to the heart)"},{label:"Protection in wet locations",value:"IPS or GFCI (NFPA 99)"},{label:"LOTO standard",value:"OSHA 1910.147"}],steps:[{title:"1. Macroshock and microshock",detail:"Macroshock: current passes through the skin; about 50 mA can put the heart into VF. Microshock: current reaches the heart directly through a catheter or pacemaker wire; a very small current (about 100 μA) can cause VF, so the safety limit is 10 μA. This risk exists in the ICU and CCU.",critical:!0},{title:"2. LOTO — lockout/tagout",detail:'Six steps: 1) preparation, 2) shutting down the device, 3) isolating it from the power supply, 4) applying the lock and a "Do Not Operate" tag, 5) releasing stored energy (such as in capacitors), 6) verifying that the device does not start (try). Each worker has their own lock and key: one key, one person.',critical:!0},{title:"3. Electric shock — what not to do first",detail:"Do not touch the shocked person until the power has been cut; the current will strike you too. First cut off the power: at the circuit breaker or by pulling the plug. If you cannot, use a dry, non-conductive object (wood or plastic) to move the person away from the power source, never a metal one.",critical:!0},{title:"4. After the shock — treatment",detail:"Check ABC. If the heart has stopped: CPR and an AED. Obtain an ECG for every person who has had an electric shock. Cardiac monitoring (usually 24 hours) is for those who lost consciousness, have an abnormal ECG or cardiac symptoms, or received a high-voltage shock. Electrical burns have an entry and an exit point: examine both.",critical:!0},{title:"5. Types of electrical circuits in a hospital",detail:"Normal: regular power (220–230 V). The essential electrical system has three branches: the life safety and critical branches (ICU, OR, laboratory), whose power is restored from the generator within 10 seconds (NFPA 99 and NFPA 110, Type 10), and the equipment branch, which is connected later with an automatic delay. The most critical devices also have a UPS.",critical:!0},{title:"6. Protection in wet locations: IPS, GFCI and RCD",detail:'Under NFPA 99, wet procedure locations, such as some operating rooms, need special protection: an isolated power system (IPS), or a GFCI if a power interruption would not endanger the patient. A US-type GFCI cuts the power at about 4–6 mA; an RCD in the IEC system usually trips at 30 mA or 10 mA. Test it regularly with the "TEST" button.'}],reference:[{heading:"Signs of electrical injury",points:["Burns at the entry and exit points of the current (examine both).","Heart rhythm disturbance (arrhythmia).","Muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis).","Compartment syndrome.","Neurological signs: altered consciousness, seizure, numbness.","Hidden internal injury: blood tests (CK, troponin, K+)."]},{heading:"LOTO — equipment and procedure",points:["A dedicated LOTO lock.","Tag: Do Not Operate, name and date.","One key per person (not shared).","LOTO log: which device, who and when.","Try: confirm that the power has actually been cut off.","Bypassing LOTO is prohibited and is a legal violation."]},{heading:"Protecting high-risk patients",points:["ICU, CCU and temporary pacemakers: risk of microshock.","Medical devices must be grounded and inspected regularly.","Wet locations: IPS or GFCI under NFPA 99.","Defibrillators: trained staff only.","MRI: the 5 gauss line; no magnetic metals nearby.","Do not touch external pacemaker wires with wet hands."]}],warnings:["Do not touch a shocked person until the power has been cut off; you will be shocked too. In the ICU, a very small current through a catheter or pacemaker wire can stop the heart. Bypassing LOTO puts staff lives at risk and is a legal violation."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A technician started repairing an IV pump in the ICU without LOTO, because he thought the power had been cut off. When he touched it, he received a 220-volt shock and his heart went into VF. A nearby staff member first cut off the power by pulling the plug (had that not been possible, a dry plastic rod could have been used to push him away from the device), then started CPR; the AED arrived within 45 seconds, and after two shocks his heart started beating again (ROSC). After several days in the ICU, he made a full recovery. Lesson: LOTO and verifying that the power is off (try), every time.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an ICU nurse. An electrician has collapsed on the floor near an IV pump, with a power cable in his hand. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I pull him away with my hands",correct:!1,feedback:"Deadly danger: the current will strike you too."},{text:"I cut off the power at the floor circuit breaker or at the plug",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the power is always cut off first."},{text:"I immediately start CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"Until the power is cut off, touching him is dangerous."},{text:"I pour water on him to cool him down",correct:!1,feedback:"Water and electricity together: deadly danger."}]},{prompt:"The power has been cut off. The worker is unconscious. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I check ABC, call a Code Blue and start CPR if needed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cardiac arrest often follows an electric shock."},{text:"I use the AED immediately, without an assessment",correct:!1,feedback:"First check breathing and pulse and call a Code Blue; the AED is used when it arrives."},{text:"I call the electrical staff",correct:!1,feedback:"Later; the patient comes first."},{text:"I examine the burn at the current's entry point",correct:!1,feedback:"Later; ABC comes first."}]},{prompt:"After CPR, the worker's heart started beating again (ROSC) and he is conscious. The first ECG is normal. What do you do next?",options:[{text:"I send him home",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: he had a cardiac arrest and needs close monitoring."},{text:"Transfer to the ICU, cardiac monitoring (usually 24 hours) and blood tests (CK, troponin, K+)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: after a cardiac arrest, there is a risk of arrhythmia and rhabdomyolysis."},{text:"I only examine the entry/exit burns",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough; a full assessment is needed."},{text:"I do nothing; ROSC is enough",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; the period after ROSC is very important."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Why is microshock dangerous in the ICU?",options:[{text:"Because it only occurs at 50 mA, the level that is felt through the skin",correct:!1,feedback:"That is macroshock through the skin."},{text:"Because the current reaches the heart directly; about 100 μA can cause VF, and the safety limit is 10 μA",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a danger for patients with catheters and pacemakers."},{text:"Because the voltage is 220 V",correct:!1,feedback:"The danger depends on the current passing through the heart, not on voltage alone."},{text:"Because the current is always greater than 1 ampere, which is enough to burn the heart muscle from the inside",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; even a very small current is enough."}]},{question:"What are the six steps of LOTO?",options:[{text:"Preparation, shutdown, isolation, lock and tag, release of stored energy, verification (try)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: OSHA 1910.147."},{text:"Preparation, lock and tag, start the work, then verify and release stored energy only afterwards",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong order: shutdown, isolation, release of stored energy and verification all come before any work starts."},{text:"Lock only, since a lock alone keeps the equipment switched off",correct:!1,feedback:"The tag and verification are also required."},{text:"Tag only",correct:!1,feedback:"The lock is also required."}]},{question:"A person has received an electric shock; what is the first step?",options:[{text:"I grab their hand to pull them away",correct:!1,feedback:"The current will strike you too."},{text:"I cut off the power",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: always first."},{text:"CPR immediately",correct:!1,feedback:"After the power is cut off."},{text:"I throw water on the person",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: water carries electricity."}]},{question:"After an electric shock, who needs cardiac monitoring?",options:[{text:"Everyone, for 1 hour only",correct:!1,feedback:"The monitoring period depends on each person's risk, not on a fixed number."},{text:"ECG for all; monitoring (usually 24 hours) after loss of consciousness, an abnormal ECG, cardiac symptoms or a high-voltage shock",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: after a low-voltage shock with no symptoms and a normal ECG, prolonged monitoring is generally not needed."},{text:"An ECG only for those with burns; 5 hours of monitoring for everyone else, whatever the voltage, the symptoms or the first ECG result",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: everyone gets an ECG, and the monitoring period is set by risk, not by one fixed number."},{text:"No tests are needed, not even an ECG",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: an ECG is needed for everyone who has had an electric shock."}]},{question:"A biomedical technician has switched off an infusion pump, unplugged it and fitted his lock and tag. What must he do before he opens the casing?",options:[{text:"Release stored energy and try to start the pump to confirm it stays off",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: releasing stored energy and the try step are the last two LOTO steps before any work starts."},{text:"Give his key to a colleague so the next shift can finish the repair",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: one key, one person; each worker keeps their own lock and key."},{text:"Take the tag off so the ward staff can see the pump is being repaired",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the tag stays on with the lock; it tells others not to operate the device."},{text:"Start at once, because the lock alone proves that the power is off",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a lock proves nothing on its own; stored energy is released and the device is tested first."}],explanation:"Under OSHA 1910.147, work starts only after shutdown, isolation, lock and tag, release of stored energy and a try that confirms the device does not start."},{question:"A colleague has collapsed next to the nurses' station, touching a damaged extension cable that is sparking. Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Pulling him off the cable by the arm, because it is faster than finding the breaker",correct:!0,feedback:"This is the wrong action: touching him before the power is cut puts you in the circuit as well."},{text:"Switching off the breaker or pulling the plug, if it can be reached safely",correct:!1,feedback:"This is right: cutting the power is always the first step."},{text:"Using a dry wooden or plastic object to move the cable away if the power cannot be cut",correct:!1,feedback:"This is right when the power cannot be cut; never use a metal object."},{text:"Calling the Code Blue team while the power is being cut",correct:!1,feedback:"This is right: cardiac arrest often follows an electric shock, so help is called early."}],explanation:"Do not touch a person who has had an electric shock until the power is cut. Cut the power first, or move him away from the source with a dry wooden or plastic object, and call for help."}],tips:["Know where the circuit breakers for your ward are and keep the way to them clear.","Before using a device, look at the plug, cable and casing; take a device with a damaged cable or a liquid spill out of use, label it and report it to biomedical engineering.","Do not touch external pacemaker wires or catheter connections with wet hands or while you are touching another device.","Never remove another person's LOTO lock or tag and never switch on a tagged device; only the person who applied the lock removes it."],activity:{prompt:"During a night shift a cleaner calls you to the corridor: a patient's relative is lying on a wet floor next to a wall socket, still holding the cable of an electric heater. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I keep everyone back, cut the power at the breaker or plug without touching him or the water, call for help, then check ABC",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the power is isolated first, without touching the person or the wet floor; only then is it safe to assess him and start CPR if needed."},{text:"I pull him away by his clothes, because cloth does not carry electricity",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: wet clothing and the wet floor carry the current to you; do not touch him until the power is isolated."},{text:"I start chest compressions at once, because every second counts in cardiac arrest",correct:!1,feedback:"Not yet: while the power is on, touching him puts you in the circuit too; isolate the power first, then assess and start CPR if needed."},{text:"I wait at a distance for the electrician to come before doing anything",correct:!1,feedback:"Too slow: if the breaker or plug can be reached safely, cut the power yourself and call for help at the same time; his heart may have stopped."}]}},{id:10,code:"HAZ-10",icon:"Biohazard",accent:"#16a34a",category:"Safety",title:"Hazardous waste",subtitle:"Infectious waste, sharps, cytotoxic waste and the OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard",summary:"According to WHO, about 85% of hospital waste is general, non-hazardous waste and about 15% is hazardous: infectious, toxic, chemical or radioactive. Under the OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard (29 CFR 1910.1030) and WHO guidance, waste must be correctly segregated at the point of generation. Segregation errors can lead to 1) infections such as HIV, HBV and HCV, 2) legal violations, 3) environmental damage. Needlestick injuries are one of the most common hazards for health care workers.",facts:[{label:"Hazardous waste",value:"About 15% (WHO)"},{label:"General waste",value:"About 85% (WHO)"},{label:"PEP for HIV",value:"As soon as possible, up to 72 hours"},{label:"Standard",value:"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1030"}],steps:[{title:"Waste categories and segregation",detail:`Infectious waste: blood, body fluids and laboratory cultures.
• Sharps: needles, blades and broken glass, in rigid containers.
• Cytotoxic: cancer drugs (chemotherapy).
• Pharmaceutical: expired medications.
• Chemical: formalin and mercury.
• Radioactive: under specific regulations.
• General non-hazardous waste.
Bag and container colors follow the Ministry of Health and hospital code.`,critical:!0},{title:"PPE required for the work",detail:"Gloves (nitrile, because of latex allergy), a mask (surgical or N95 depending on the risk), goggles or a face shield, a disposable protective gown and closed shoes. For cytotoxic waste: two pairs of chemotherapy-rated gloves and a chemotherapy gown.",critical:!0},{title:"Needlestick injury — immediate steps",detail:"1. Immediately wash the wound with soap and water (not alcohol or bleach). 2. Let it bleed freely, but do not squeeze hard or suck the wound. 3. Report immediately to your supervisor and to Occupational Health. 4. If HIV PEP is needed, start it as soon as possible, ideally within a few hours, and not after 72 hours; the course lasts 28 days. 5. Baseline HIV, HBV and HCV testing, then follow-up according to the Occupational Health schedule.",critical:!0},{title:"Hazardous material spill — five steps",detail:"1. Move people away from the area. 2. Put on PPE. 3. Read the material's SDS. 4. Use a spill kit (absorbent and disinfectant). 5. After cleanup, ventilate the area and write a report. For a cytotoxic spill: the pharmacy's dedicated team, following the protocol.",critical:!0},{title:"Sharps container",detail:"A rigid, puncture-resistant container. When it is three quarters full, close and replace it, and do not fill it further. Do not recap needles (recapping is prohibited). Keep the container close to the work area and at a suitable height."},{title:"Cytotoxic — cancer drugs",detail:"These drugs are dangerous for staff as well. The USP 800 standard: two pairs of chemotherapy gloves (ASTM D6978), a chemotherapy gown and, if there is a risk of splashing or powder, eye and face protection and an N95. A dedicated chemotherapy spill kit. Every exposure must be reported."}],reference:[{heading:"Waste colors (WHO example)",points:["Colors vary from country to country; follow the code of the Ministry of Health and your hospital.","Yellow: infectious waste.","Yellow with the sharps symbol: rigid sharps containers.","Brown: chemical and pharmaceutical waste.","Cytotoxic: a specific label (often purple).","Radioactive: the radiation symbol and a dedicated container.","Black: general non-hazardous waste."]},{heading:"Needlestick — first steps",points:["Wash immediately with soap and water.","Do not squeeze hard or suck the wound.","Report immediately to Occupational Health.","HIV PEP: as soon as possible, up to 72 hours, for 28 days.","Baseline HIV, HBV and HCV testing.","Follow-up testing according to the Occupational Health schedule."]},{heading:"PPE by level of risk",points:["Standard: gloves, plus a mask and goggles if there is a risk of splashing.","Contact: + a protective gown.","Droplet: + a surgical mask.","Airborne: + an N95 and a negative-pressure room.","Cytotoxic: + two pairs of gloves and a chemotherapy gown.","BSL-3: + an N95 or PAPR based on the risk assessment."]}],warnings:["Do not recap needles; recapping is one of the most common causes of needlestick injuries. Do not fill a sharps container more than three quarters full. A cytotoxic spill is cleaned up only by a trained team with a dedicated spill kit. Segregate waste according to the hospital color code."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): After drawing blood, a nurse recapped the needle and it went into his finger. The patient was HCV-positive. The nurse immediately washed the wound with soap and water and reported to Occupational Health within the first 45 minutes. There is no PEP for HCV, so follow-up testing was done. The first test was negative, but a later test was positive. Antiviral treatment (DAA) was given and the nurse recovered. Lesson: recapping is prohibited, and immediate reporting ensures that follow-up begins.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse. After drawing blood, in a hurry and out of habit, you recap the needle and it goes into your finger. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I do nothing; the wound is small",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: exposure to HIV, HBV and HCV."},{text:"I wash the wound with alcohol",correct:!1,feedback:"Soap and water are needed, not alcohol."},{text:"I immediately wash the wound with soap and water and let it bleed freely, without squeezing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the first step. Then report immediately, so that PEP can be started as soon as possible if needed."},{text:"I recap the needle and throw it into the sharps container",correct:!1,feedback:"Recapping is prohibited."}]},{prompt:"The wound has been washed. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"I go home",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: assessment and follow-up are required."},{text:"I notify Occupational Health immediately and file a report, and HIV, HBV and HCV testing of the source patient is requested",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an immediate assessment allows PEP to be started as soon as possible if needed."},{text:"I put a piece of cloth on it and carry on working",correct:!1,feedback:"A report is required."},{text:"I wait until next week",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too late: PEP is generally not recommended after 72 hours."}]},{prompt:"The patient is HIV-positive. Occupational Health recommends PEP. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I refuse because the wound is small",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a risk of HIV; PEP clearly reduces the risk."},{text:"I accept PEP, take the full 28-day course and attend the follow-up testing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: PEP and follow-up according to CDC and USPHS guidance."},{text:"I take PEP for one week only",correct:!1,feedback:"The full 28-day course is required."},{text:"I stop PEP before the test results are back",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: PEP continues according to the physician's decision."}]}],quiz:[{question:"When is a sharps container closed and replaced?",options:[{text:"When it is completely full to the top",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: needles stick out of the container."},{text:"When it is three quarters full",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to WHO guidance."},{text:"When it is half full",correct:!1,feedback:"Too early, and containers are used up unnecessarily."},{text:"Whenever you like",correct:!1,feedback:"The guidance is three quarters."}]},{question:"Why is recapping prohibited?",options:[{text:"Because it takes too much time during busy shifts",correct:!1,feedback:"The reason is the risk, not the time."},{text:"Because it is one of the most common causes of needlestick injuries",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: do not recap needles."},{text:"Because it is expensive",correct:!1,feedback:"Cost is not the reason: recapping is one of the most common causes of needlestick injuries."},{text:"Because it damages the needle tip and makes the needle less effective",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; the reason is staff safety."}],explanation:"Recapping is prohibited because it is one of the most common causes of needlestick injuries; put used needles straight into a rigid sharps container."},{question:"When is HIV PEP started after an exposure?",options:[{text:"Only within the first 2 hours after the exposure; after that it is of no use",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the sooner the better, but PEP can be started up to 72 hours."},{text:"As soon as possible, ideally within a few hours, and up to 72 hours",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: according to the USPHS 2025 guidelines."},{text:"After a week",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too late."},{text:"Timing does not matter",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: any delay reduces its effectiveness."}]},{question:"Which gloves are used for cytotoxic drugs?",options:[{text:"One pair of thick latex examination gloves",correct:!1,feedback:"Latex causes allergies, and one pair is not enough."},{text:"Two pairs of chemotherapy gloves (ASTM D6978)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: USP 800."},{text:"No gloves",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous."},{text:"Two pairs of vinyl gloves, changed every 30 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"The drugs can pass through vinyl."}]},{question:"While tying a black general waste bag, a cleaner sees a needle sticking out through the plastic. What is the correct action?",options:[{text:"Not touch the needle, keep the bag apart and report it to the supervisor",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a needle in general waste is a segregation error; handling it by hand risks a needlestick."},{text:"Pull the needle out by hand and drop it into the nearest sharps container",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: pulling a needle out by hand is exactly how needlestick injuries happen."},{text:"Push the needle back inside and tie the bag tighter so that no one gets pricked",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: pushing it in by hand risks a prick now, and the needle still endangers the waste handlers later."},{text:"Leave it, because the bag goes to general waste and nobody opens it",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: porters and waste workers handle the bag; a hidden needle can injure them."}],explanation:"Waste is segregated where it is produced. A needle in a general bag endangers everyone who handles the bag: do not touch it, keep the bag apart and report it."},{question:"A bag of chemotherapy waste splits open on the floor of the oncology ward. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Keep people away and call the trained team with the dedicated chemotherapy spill kit",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a cytotoxic spill is cleaned up only by a trained team with a dedicated spill kit."},{text:"Wipe it up quickly with paper towels while wearing one pair of gloves",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: cytotoxic drugs are dangerous for staff; ordinary gloves and towels do not protect you."},{text:"Mop it with the ward's usual mop and disinfectant, then put the mop back",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the mop spreads the drug across the ward and contaminates the next area it touches."},{text:"Cover it with a sheet and leave it for the cleaners at the end of the shift",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: people can still walk through it, and untrained cleaners are put at risk."}],explanation:"Cytotoxic drugs are dangerous for staff too. A spill is cleaned up only by a trained team with dedicated PPE and a chemotherapy spill kit, and every exposure is reported."}],tips:["Keep the sharps container within reach of where you use the needle, so you never carry a used needle across the room.","Look at the fill line before each use; at three quarters full, close the container and ask for a new one.","Decide which bag the waste belongs in before you throw it, using your hospital's color code; a wrong bag cannot be sorted safely later.","Know where your ward's spill kit is and how to reach Occupational Health on every shift."],activity:{prompt:"A nurse pricks her finger with a used needle after giving an injection. She says it is only a small prick and wants to finish her medication round first. What do you advise her?",options:[{text:"Wash it now with soap and water and report at once to the supervisor and Occupational Health",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: washing and reporting at once let Occupational Health assess the risk early; if PEP is needed, the sooner it starts the better."},{text:"Squeeze the finger hard and clean it with alcohol, then finish the round",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: do not squeeze hard and do not use alcohol; wash with soap and water and report without delay."},{text:"Finish the round first and report at the end of the shift if she feels unwell",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: infection after a needlestick gives no symptoms at first; delaying the report also delays PEP if it is needed."},{text:"Report only if the patient's blood test later comes back positive",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: every exposure is reported at once; waiting for results can push the start of PEP past the time when it helps."}]}},{id:11,code:"LAB-11",icon:"Microscope",accent:"#0d9488",category:"Safety",title:"Laboratory safety",subtitle:"BSL levels, spills, eyewash stations and the fume hood",summary:"Hospital laboratories contain hazardous materials: chemical (acids, bases, solvents), biological (bacteria, viruses, fungi) and physical (glass, pressure, heat). Under the CDC BMBL (Biosafety in Microbiological and Biomedical Laboratories) and OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1450, there are four biosafety levels (BSL). Most clinical hospital laboratories work at BSL-2, and some work (such as TB culture) requires BSL-3. Splashes into the eyes are a major hazard, so the eyewash station must be close by and unobstructed (ANSI Z358.1).",facts:[{label:"BSL levels",value:"Four levels (BSL-1 to BSL-4)"},{label:"Clinical laboratory",value:"Usually BSL-2; some work BSL-3"},{label:"Reaching the eyewash",value:"Within 10 seconds (ANSI Z358.1)"},{label:"Eye flushing time",value:"At least 15 minutes"}],steps:[{title:"The four BSL levels",detail:"BSL-1: agents that do not cause disease in healthy people (such as E. coli K-12). BSL-2: moderate risk (Salmonella, HBV, HIV); most clinical laboratories. BSL-3: dangerous agents transmitted through the air (TB cultures and SARS-CoV-2 cultures). BSL-4: the deadliest agents (Ebola, Marburg). Each level requires specific PPE and containment equipment.",critical:!0},{title:"PPE for the work",detail:"Routine: nitrile gloves, a lab coat and safety glasses. If there is a risk of splashing: + a face shield. If aerosols may be generated: work inside a biosafety cabinet, with an N95 or PAPR based on the risk assessment. For cytotoxic drugs: two pairs of gloves and a chemotherapy gown.",critical:!0},{title:"Fume hood — correct use",detail:"Keep the front sash at the marked height, not fully open. Check the airflow indicator before starting; the face velocity of the hood is generally about 0.4–0.6 m/s. Place items at least 15 cm inside the hood. The hood is inspected and certified every year. A hood is not a storage area; it is only for working.",critical:!0},{title:"Chemical spill",detail:"1. Move people away from the area. 2. Put on PPE (splash goggles and acid-resistant gloves). 3. Read the SDS. 4. Spill kit: sodium bicarbonate for acids; citric acid for bases; absorbent material or activated carbon for solvents. 5. Mercury: only a dedicated mercury kit; do not clean it up with a broom or a vacuum. 6. Write a report and ventilate the area.",critical:!0},{title:"Eyewash and safety shower",detail:"The eyewash must be reachable within 10 seconds (about 17 meters), on the same floor and unobstructed (ANSI Z358.1). Flush the eyes with water for at least 15 minutes, both eyes if both were splashed, and hold the eyelids open. Safety shower: the same access limit and at least 15 minutes. Run the eyewash and shower every week to flush the water and to confirm that they work.",critical:!0},{title:"Broken glass and sharps",detail:"Do not pick up broken glass by hand; use tongs, or a broom and dustpan. Broken glass goes in the dedicated glass container, and Pasteur pipettes and needles go in the sharps container. Do not throw broken glass into general waste."}],reference:[{heading:"BSL in the hospital laboratory",points:["BSL-2: HIV, HBV, HCV, Salmonella.","PPE: gloves, a lab coat and safety glasses.","Containment equipment: Class II biosafety cabinet (BSC).","Practice: eating, drinking and applying makeup in the laboratory are prohibited.","BSL-3: TB culture, Histoplasma.","PPE: + respiratory protection (N95 or PAPR) and a negative-pressure room."]},{heading:"Chemical hazards",points:["Flammable: ethanol, acetone, ether.","Acids: HCl, H₂SO₄, acetic acid.","Bases: NaOH, KOH, ammonia.","Toxic: mercury, cyanide, formaldehyde.","Reactive: sodium, peroxides.","Oxidizers: bleach, hydrogen peroxide."]},{heading:"Eyewash — 15 minutes",points:["Chemical splash: at least 15 minutes.","Strong bases may require longer flushing; check the SDS and inform the physician.","Remove contact lenses during flushing if they come out easily.","Hold the eyelids open so the water reaches the whole eye.","Flush both eyes if both were splashed.","Then: Occupational Health and an ophthalmologist."]}],warnings:["Eating, drinking and applying makeup in the laboratory are prohibited. Do not clean up mercury with a broom or a vacuum; use only a dedicated mercury kit. Check the eyewash every week. A fume hood with its front sash fully open does not work properly; work at the marked height."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A laboratory technician was working with 98% H₂SO₄ without safety glasses. The bottle fell and acid splashed into his eye. A colleague immediately took him to the eyewash, and his eyes were flushed with water for 15 minutes with the eyelids held open. Occupational Health arrived 20 minutes later, and an ophthalmologist examined him an hour later. After two weeks of treatment his eye recovered. Lesson: eye protection is required when working with acids, and the eyewash must be close by and unobstructed.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a laboratory technician. While the centrifuge is running, a tube breaks and blood spreads inside the machine. You are wearing gloves but no protective gown. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I open the machine right away and clean it",correct:!1,feedback:"Full PPE is required, and there is a risk of aerosols."},{text:"I stop the centrifuge and keep the lid closed, wait 30 minutes, put on full PPE and get the spill kit ready",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: under biosafety guidance, 30 minutes allows the aerosols to settle."},{text:"I open the centrifuge lid to take a look",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: aerosols will spread."},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous and a violation."}]},{prompt:"After 30 minutes, the centrifuge has been opened and the spill kit is ready. What is the second step?",options:[{text:"Cleaning with bleach diluted 1:10 (or an approved disinfectant)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: 1:10 bleach for blood spills."},{text:"Cleaning with water only",correct:!1,feedback:"Without a disinfectant, the hazard remains."},{text:"With a brush only",correct:!1,feedback:"A disinfectant is required."},{text:"I stop work and leave it for someone else",correct:!1,feedback:"You are trained and prepared; finish the job properly."}]},{prompt:"The cleanup is finished. What is the third step?",options:[{text:"I write an incident report and check myself and my PPE",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: documentation and checking yourself for exposure."},{text:"I do nothing; the cleanup is enough",correct:!1,feedback:"An incident report is required."},{text:"I leave the laboratory",correct:!1,feedback:"Not before the report."},{text:"I immediately start another tube",correct:!1,feedback:"Not before the machine has been checked."}]}],quiz:[{question:"At which BSL level does a hospital clinical laboratory generally work?",options:[{text:"BSL-1 for all routine patient samples",correct:!1,feedback:"BSL-1 is for agents that pose no risk (such as E. coli K-12)."},{text:"BSL-2, and some work at BSL-3",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: HIV and HBV at BSL-2; TB culture at BSL-3."},{text:"BSL-4 for all work with blood samples",correct:!1,feedback:"BSL-4 is for Ebola and exists only in specialized laboratories."},{text:"BSL-0",correct:!1,feedback:"There is no such level."}]},{question:"How long should the eyes be flushed after a chemical splash?",options:[{text:"2 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too short."},{text:"At least 15 minutes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: ANSI Z358.1."},{text:"5 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Too short."},{text:"Until the pain stops",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect; at least 15 minutes."}]},{question:"A mercury spill: what do you use?",options:[{text:"An ordinary broom to sweep up the drops",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the mercury droplets scatter and vaporize."},{text:"A dedicated mercury kit",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: for example sulfur or zinc powder, which binds the mercury."},{text:"Soap and water",correct:!1,feedback:"It does not work."},{text:"A vacuum cleaner",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the mercury vaporizes and spreads."}]},{question:"After a tube breaks in the centrifuge, how long do you wait before opening it?",options:[{text:"I open it immediately",correct:!1,feedback:"Risk of aerosols."},{text:"30 minutes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the aerosols settle."},{text:"5 minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Too short."},{text:"24 hours",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too long."}]},{question:"While you are working at the fume hood, its airflow indicator shows low flow. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop work, close the containers, lower the sash and report the hood",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: with low airflow the hood no longer protects you; stop and report it."},{text:"Raise the sash fully so that more air can flow into the hood",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a fully open sash makes the hood work worse, not better."},{text:"Carry on, because the hood passed its yearly inspection",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the indicator shows the hood's state now; a past inspection does not protect you today."},{text:"Move the work out of the hood and finish it on the open bench",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: work that needs a hood is never moved to an open bench, where the fumes reach you directly."}],explanation:"A fume hood protects only when the airflow is correct and the sash is at the marked height. A low airflow reading means stop and report."},{question:"A glass beaker breaks on the bench. How are the pieces cleared up?",options:[{text:"With tongs or a broom and dustpan, into the dedicated broken glass container",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: broken glass is never picked up by hand and goes into the dedicated glass container."},{text:"I pick up the large pieces carefully by hand while wearing gloves",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: glass can cut through gloves; use tongs or a broom and dustpan."},{text:"I wrap the pieces in paper and put them in the general waste bin",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: broken glass never goes into general waste, where it injures the waste handlers."},{text:"I sweep them under the bench and leave them for the cleaners",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: hidden pieces injure someone else later; clear them up at once and correctly."}],explanation:"Broken glass is cleared with tongs or a broom and dustpan into the dedicated glass container; Pasteur pipettes and needles go into the sharps container."}],tips:["Before you start work, check that the way to the eyewash and the safety shower is clear.","Read the SDS of a chemical before you use it for the first time, and keep it where the team can find it.","Label every container, including a solution you have just prepared, with its contents and the date.","Eat, drink and rest only in the staff room, never at the bench."],activity:{prompt:"A technician preparing a reagent gets a splash of a strong base in one eye. He is in pain and says he will rinse it at the sink and carry on. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I take him to the eyewash and flush for at least 15 minutes with the eyelids held open",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: flushing starts at once and lasts at least 15 minutes; strong bases may need longer, so the SDS and the physician decide the next step."},{text:"I let him rinse briefly at the sink and we see whether the pain settles",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a short rinse is not enough and pain is not a guide; flush at the eyewash for at least 15 minutes."},{text:"I wipe the eye with a clean tissue to remove the chemical before flushing",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: rubbing or wiping pushes the chemical into the eye and delays flushing."},{text:"I look up the SDS first, then decide whether he needs to flush",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: flushing comes first and cannot wait; a colleague can read the SDS while he flushes."}]}},{id:12,code:"INF-12",icon:"ShieldPlus",accent:"#0e7490",category:"Safety",title:"Infection control",subtitle:"The WHO 5 Moments, isolation precautions and preventing health care-associated infections (HAI)",summary:"Health care-associated infections (HAI) are a major risk for patients. According to the 2022 WHO report, out of every 100 patients in acute care hospitals, about 7 in high-income countries and 15 in low- and middle-income countries acquire at least one HAI; up to 70% of them can be prevented through hand hygiene and other IPC practices. Under the WHO 5 Moments and the CDC Standard Precautions, hand hygiene is the simplest and most effective measure.",facts:[{label:"HAI (WHO 2022)",value:"7 in 100 (high income) · 15 in 100 (low and middle income)"},{label:"Prevention through IPC",value:"Up to 70% (WHO)"},{label:"Standard",value:"WHO 5 Moments + CDC"},{label:"Hand hygiene time",value:"Soap and water 40–60 seconds · alcohol 20–30 seconds"}],steps:[{title:"The WHO 5 Moments for hand hygiene",detail:"1. Before touching a patient. 2. Before a clean or aseptic procedure. 3. After exposure to blood or body fluids. 4. After touching a patient. 5. After touching the patient's surroundings.",critical:!0},{title:"Hand hygiene technique",detail:"Soap and water: 40–60 seconds (when hands are visibly soiled). Alcohol-based gel: 20–30 seconds. The WHO steps: palm to palm, backs of the hands, between the fingers, backs of the fingers, thumbs, fingertips and nails, and wrists.",critical:!0},{title:"Isolation precautions",detail:"Standard: for all patients. Contact (MRSA, C. difficile, VRE): gown and gloves. Droplet (influenza, mumps): surgical mask. Airborne (TB, measles, varicella): N95 and a negative-pressure room (AIIR) with the door closed.",critical:!0},{title:"PPE — donning and doffing order",detail:"Donning: 1) gown, 2) mask or N95, 3) goggles or face shield, 4) gloves. Doffing: 1) gloves, 2) goggles, 3) gown, 4) mask or N95 (the N95 outside the room). Perform hand hygiene after removing all PPE, and also between steps if your hands become contaminated.",critical:!0},{title:"Multidrug-resistant organisms (MDRO)",detail:"MRSA: methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus. VRE: vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus. CRE: bacteria resistant to carbapenems, which are among the last-resort antibiotics. C. difficile: its spores are not killed by alcohol; soap and water are needed. Appropriate antibiotic use (antibiotic stewardship) prevents these organisms from emerging.",critical:!0},{title:"Surveillance and outbreaks",detail:"Every unit should have an infection control nurse. Four major infections: CLABSI (bloodstream, via a central line), CAUTI (urinary tract, via a catheter), VAP (lungs of patients on ventilators), SSI (surgical site). If cases rise above the usual level or several linked cases appear, notify the infection control team immediately. The hospital reports to the Duhok General Directorate of Health and the Kurdistan Region Ministry of Health according to its protocol."}],reference:[{heading:"The WHO 5 Moments — memory aid",points:["1. Before touching a patient.","2. Before a clean (aseptic) procedure.","3. After exposure to body fluids.","4. After touching a patient.","5. After touching the patient's surroundings.",'Memory aid: two "befores" and three "afters".']},{heading:"Isolation signs on the door",points:["The colors and design of the signs vary from hospital to hospital; follow your hospital's IPC plan.","Standard: for all patients, no sign needed.","Contact: gown and gloves.","Droplet: surgical mask.","Airborne: N95 and a negative-pressure room (AIIR).","Protective: protecting immunocompromised patients."]},{heading:"C. difficile — special features",points:["Alcohol-based gel does not kill the spores; soap and water are needed.","Clean surfaces with bleach or sporicidal disinfectants.","Contact precautions according to the hospital plan (at least until 48 hours after the diarrhea stops).","A single room with its own toilet.","PPE: gown and gloves at all times.","Dedicated equipment for the patient (such as a stethoscope)."]}],warnings:["Alcohol-based gel does not kill C. difficile spores; soap and water are needed. The spores can survive for months on surfaces and equipment; use bleach or sporicidal disinfectants. Handwashing takes 40–60 seconds, not 10 seconds. Incorrect removal of PPE leads to contamination of the staff member themselves."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): On the oncology unit of a hospital, four patients developed C. difficile infection within two weeks. The infection control nurse investigated and found that staff were using only alcohol-based gel, not soap and water, and that surfaces were not being cleaned with a suitable disinfectant. The infection control team responded immediately: investigation, retraining, cleaning with bleach and ongoing surveillance, with a report to the health directorate according to protocol. Within a month the outbreak stopped. Lesson: staff must know the special features of C. difficile and the WHO 5 Moments well.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse in the ICU. A patient with suspected TB arrives, and the unit's negative-pressure room is out of service. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I put the patient in a regular room with the door open",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a risk of airborne spread throughout the unit."},{text:"I just put on an N95 and start working",correct:!1,feedback:"PPE alone is not enough; the patient needs a negative-pressure room."},{text:"I put a surgical mask on the patient, place them in a single room with the door closed and notify infection control immediately so they can be moved to a negative-pressure room",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: TB requires an airborne infection isolation room (AIIR)."},{text:"I leave the door open so the air circulates",correct:!1,feedback:"Risk of airborne spread."}]},{prompt:"The negative-pressure room is ready. You are about to go in. What do you do before entering?",options:[{text:"I go straight in",correct:!1,feedback:"PPE comes first."},{text:"I put on PPE in the correct order: gown, N95, goggles, gloves",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the N95 is put on before entering and a fit check is performed."},{text:"I put on the N95 after entering",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the N95 must be put on before entering."},{text:"Only a surgical mask",correct:!1,feedback:"TB requires an N95."}]},{prompt:"The work is done and you are about to leave. What is the correct order for removing PPE?",options:[{text:"Gloves, goggles, gown, then the N95 outside the room",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the N95 is removed outside the room, followed by hand hygiene."},{text:"N95, gown, goggles, gloves",correct:!1,feedback:"The N95 is removed last, and outside the room."},{text:"I take everything off together quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a risk of contaminating yourself."},{text:"I only take off the gloves; the rest is not necessary",correct:!1,feedback:"All PPE must be removed correctly."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the first of the WHO 5 Moments?",options:[{text:"After touching a patient",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the fourth moment."},{text:"Before touching a patient",correct:!0,feedback:'Hand hygiene comes before touching the patient; the five moments are two "befores" and three "afters".'},{text:"Before eating",correct:!1,feedback:"That is not one of the WHO 5 Moments."},{text:"After leaving the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"Not one of the 5 Moments; all five relate to the patient, a clean procedure, body fluids or the patient's surroundings."}],explanation:'The WHO 5 Moments are two "befores" and three "afters": moment 1 is before touching a patient, moment 4 after touching a patient.'},{question:"For C. difficile, what are hands cleaned with?",options:[{text:"Alcohol-based gel",correct:!1,feedback:"It does not kill the spores."},{text:"Soap and water",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: rubbing and rinsing remove the spores."},{text:"Both are equally effective",correct:!1,feedback:"Alcohol-based gel is not enough here."},{text:"It is not necessary",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous."}]},{question:"What does airborne isolation for TB require?",options:[{text:"A surgical mask and a regular single room",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough."},{text:"An N95 and a negative-pressure room",correct:!0,feedback:"TB is spread through the air, so it needs an N95 and a negative-pressure room (AIIR) with the door closed."},{text:"Only an N95",correct:!1,feedback:"A dedicated room is also required."},{text:"No PPE is needed",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous."}],explanation:"TB needs airborne precautions: an N95 and a negative-pressure room (AIIR) with the door closed. A surgical mask is for droplet precautions."},{question:"In the PPE doffing sequence, which item is removed first?",options:[{text:"Gloves",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: gloves are the most contaminated."},{text:"Mask",correct:!1,feedback:"The mask is removed last."},{text:"Gown",correct:!1,feedback:"Later."},{text:"Goggles",correct:!1,feedback:"Later."}]},{question:"You have emptied a urinary catheter bag and taken off your gloves. Your next task is the IV line of the same patient. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Clean my hands, because removing gloves does not replace hand hygiene",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: two of the 5 Moments meet here: after exposure to body fluids and before a clean procedure."},{text:"Go straight to the IV line, because the gloves kept my hands clean",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: gloves can have tiny holes, and hands get contaminated while the gloves come off."},{text:"Put on a new pair of gloves at once, without cleaning my hands",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: new gloves over contaminated hands carry the germs to the IV line."},{text:"Wipe the same gloves with alcohol and put them back on",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: gloves are single use; they are not cleaned and reused."}],explanation:"Gloves do not replace hand hygiene. Clean your hands after taking them off, especially after exposure to body fluids and before a clean procedure."},{question:"A patient admitted to a general ward is now suspected of having TB. What is done?",options:[{text:"Move him to an airborne isolation room (AIIR) with the door closed; staff wear an N95",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: TB spreads through the air, so airborne precautions are needed."},{text:"Keep him in the shared room and give the staff surgical masks",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a surgical mask does not protect staff from TB, and the other patients in the room stay at risk."},{text:"Use contact precautions only: a gown and gloves at the bedside",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a gown and gloves do not protect against a disease that spreads through the air."},{text:"Leave him where he is until the culture result comes back",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: precautions start on suspicion, not after the result; waiting puts staff and patients at risk."}],explanation:"TB spreads through the air: an N95 and a negative-pressure room (AIIR) with the door closed, from the moment it is suspected."}],tips:["Keep alcohol gel at the point of care, so hand hygiene takes seconds, not a walk to the sink.","Read the isolation sign before you enter, and put on the PPE outside the room.","Clean shared equipment such as a stethoscope or blood pressure cuff between patients.","Take off your gloves and clean your hands before you touch the computer, phone or patient chart."],activity:{prompt:"You are about to enter the single room of a patient with C. difficile to take a blood sample. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Gown and gloves before entering, and soap and water for my hands after leaving",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: contact precautions, and soap and water because alcohol gel does not kill the spores."},{text:"Gloves only, and alcohol gel on my hands after leaving the room",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a gown is also needed, and alcohol gel does not kill C. difficile spores."},{text:"A surgical mask and alcohol gel, because diarrhea spreads by droplets",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: C. difficile spreads by contact; a gown, gloves and soap and water are needed."},{text:"An N95 and a request to move the patient to a negative-pressure room",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: airborne precautions are for diseases such as TB; C. difficile needs contact precautions and soap and water."}]}},{id:13,code:"MED-13",icon:"Pill",accent:"#d97706",category:"Medical",title:"Medication safety — the five rights",subtitle:"IPSG 3, anaphylaxis and barcode verification",summary:"Medication errors are one of the leading causes of harm in hospitals, and most of them are preventable; WHO estimates their global cost at about US$42 billion a year. Under the International Patient Safety Goals (JCI IPSG 3) and ISMP, the five rights of medication are fundamental but not sufficient: two patient identifiers, barcode scanning and an independent double-check for high-alert medications are required. Common errors: the wrong dose (such as a tenfold error), the wrong patient and the wrong route (IV instead of PO).",facts:[{label:"Cost of medication errors",value:"About US$42 billion a year (WHO)"},{label:"High-alert medications",value:"Insulin, heparin, opioids, KCl"},{label:"Standard",value:"JCI IPSG 3 + ISMP"},{label:"Anaphylaxis (adult)",value:"Epinephrine 0.3–0.5 mg IM"}],steps:[{title:"The five rights — the foundation",detail:`1. Right patient (two identifiers: name and date of birth, or medical record number, MRN). 2. Right medication. 3. Right dose. 4. Right route (IV/IM/PO/SC). 5. Right time.
• All five are required, not just some of them.`,critical:!0},{title:"Additional rights",detail:"6. Right documentation. 7. Right reason. 8. Monitoring the patient's response (right response). Many guidelines have added these rights. For high-alert medications: an independent double-check.",critical:!0},{title:"Anaphylaxis — the first step",detail:"Ask about allergies before every medication. Signs: flushed skin, red raised welts (hives), shortness of breath, high-pitched breathing (stridor), low blood pressure. Treatment: epinephrine 0.3–0.5 mg IM (1 mg/mL) immediately, into the anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis). IV epinephrine is generally given only in cardiac arrest or by specialists. Call for help (Code Blue).",critical:!0},{title:"High-alert medications — double-check",detail:"Insulin, heparin and other anticoagulants, opioids (morphine, fentanyl), concentrated KCl and chemotherapy. All of them require an independent double-check by two staff members and barcode scanning.",critical:!0},{title:"Barcode medication administration (BCMA)",detail:"BCMA: barcode medication administration. Scan: 1) the patient's wristband, 2) the medication, 3) the system confirms the match. Studies have shown that BCMA clearly reduces medication administration errors. Bypassing BCMA is not allowed without an official, documented reason.",critical:!0},{title:"Look-alike, sound-alike medications (LASA)",detail:"Medications whose names or appearance are similar (look-alike, sound-alike): hydromorphone and morphine, heparin and Hespan, vincristine and vinblastine, Humalog and Humulin. ISMP recommends tall man lettering, such as hydroMORPHONE and morphine. Do not work from memory: read, verify, scan."}],reference:[{heading:"Rights checklist",points:["R1: two identifiers (not the room or bed number).","R2: the medication matches the medication administration record (MAR).","R3: the dose matches (mL, mg, units).","R4: the route matches (IV/IM/PO).","R5: the time matches (hour and frequency).","R6: documentation in the MAR.","R7: allergy check.","R8: monitoring the response after administration."]},{heading:"Anaphylaxis protocol",points:["First: epinephrine 0.3–0.5 mg IM into the thigh.","Code Blue and call for help.","IV access and intravenous fluids.","ABC and oxygen.","Antihistamines and steroids only as adjunct therapy, not in place of epinephrine.","Repeat IM epinephrine every 5–15 minutes if needed.","Observation for several hours depending on the severity."]},{heading:"Common errors",points:["1. Wrong dose (such as a tenfold error).","2. Wrong patient.","3. Wrong route (IV instead of PO).","4. Wrong time.","5. Drug interactions.","6. Missed allergy.","7. Mixing up LASA medications.","8. Verbal order error.","9. Dose calculation error.","10. Bypassing BCMA."]}],warnings:["Do not accept a verbal order without read-back: 1) write it down, 2) read it back to the physician, 3) get their confirmation. Concentrated KCl must not be stocked on patient units; only premixed solutions. KCl is never given by IV push: it is lethal. Bypassing the barcode without an official reason is prohibited."],realCase:"Practical case (training example): A nurse had two patients: one needed IV KCl as a slow infusion (per protocol, generally no more than 10 mEq per hour through a peripheral vein), and the other needed only saline. In a hurry and without scanning the barcode, concentrated KCl was given by IV push. Shortly afterward the patient's heart went into VF. Code Blue, CPR and an AED; 4 minutes later the heart started beating again (ROSC). The investigation found: BCMA had been bypassed, there was no double-check and concentrated KCl was stocked on the unit. After that, concentrated KCl was removed from the units and BCMA became a hard stop.",caseStudy:[{prompt:'You are an ICU nurse. The physician says verbally: "Give KCl 20 mEq IV to the patient in Room 302." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I give the medication right away; the physician is the expert",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a verbal order requires read-back (JCI)."},{text:'I write it down and read it back: "KCl 20 mEq IV as an infusion for the patient in Room 302, correct?" and ask for the order to be entered in the system',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: write it down, read it back and confirm."},{text:"I refuse; all verbal orders are prohibited",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: verbal orders are not prohibited, but read-back is required."},{text:"I do nothing until the order appears in the system",correct:!1,feedback:"A delay can harm the patient; do a read-back."}]},{prompt:"The physician has confirmed. You go to the medication cabinet (Pyxis). There is no premixed KCl 20 mEq, only concentrated KCl 40 mEq. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I give half of the concentrated KCl by IV push",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: concentrated KCl is never given by IV push."},{text:"I call the pharmacy and ask for premixed KCl",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: only premixed solutions, as a slow infusion."},{text:"I give it by IV push because it is faster",correct:!1,feedback:"Lethal: VF."},{text:"I give it by mouth (PO)",correct:!1,feedback:"The route cannot be changed without a new order."}]},{prompt:"The pharmacy has sent premixed KCl 20 mEq/100 mL. There are two patients in Room 302: one is awake and the other is asleep. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I start the infusion; it is the right room",correct:!1,feedback:"A room number is not an identifier; two identifiers are required."},{text:"I scan the wristband and confirm two identifiers (name and date of birth)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: IPSG 1 and BCMA."},{text:"I ask the other patient which one needs the medication",correct:!1,feedback:"That is not proper identification."},{text:"I only read the MAR",correct:!1,feedback:"The wristband and barcode are also required."}]}],quiz:[{question:"IV KCl: what is never done?",options:[{text:"Giving a premixed solution from the pharmacy",correct:!1,feedback:"A premixed solution as a slow infusion is the correct method."},{text:"IV push (especially concentrated KCl)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: it is lethal."},{text:"A slow infusion with an infusion pump",correct:!1,feedback:"A slow infusion of a premixed solution according to protocol is safe."},{text:"PO",correct:!1,feedback:"The oral route can be safe if there is an order."}]},{question:"In anaphylaxis, what is the first drug?",options:[{text:"Benadryl PO",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too slow; epinephrine comes first."},{text:"Epinephrine 0.3–0.5 mg IM",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: into the thigh (vastus lateralis)."},{text:"IV fluids: 1 liter of normal saline rapidly",correct:!1,feedback:"Later; epinephrine comes first."},{text:"O₂ only, by mask at 15 L/min",correct:!1,feedback:"Without epinephrine, the patient's life is in danger."}]},{question:"Which two identifiers are required?",options:[{text:"Name only",correct:!1,feedback:"One identifier is not enough."},{text:"Name and date of birth, or MRN",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: IPSG 1."},{text:"Name and the room number on the door",correct:!1,feedback:"A room number is not an identifier."},{text:"Bed number",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous; patients get moved."}]},{question:"Verbal orders: is read-back needed?",options:[{text:"It is not needed",correct:!1,feedback:"It is needed: never accept a verbal order without writing it down, reading it back and getting confirmation."},{text:"It is required: write down, read back and confirm (JCI)",correct:!0,feedback:"Writing the order down, reading it back to the physician and getting confirmation prevents verbal order errors (JCI)."},{text:"Only for high-alert medications such as insulin and heparin",correct:!1,feedback:"It is required for all verbal orders."},{text:"Only in the ICU, where orders change most often",correct:!1,feedback:"It is required on all units."}],explanation:"Never accept a verbal order without read-back: write it down, read it back to the physician and get their confirmation. This applies to every verbal order on every unit."},{question:"Two patients with similar names are in the same room. Which check prevents a wrong-patient error?",options:[{text:"Name and date of birth, or MRN, checked against the wristband",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: two identifiers checked against the wristband, not the bed or room number."},{text:"The bed number and the room number written on the door",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: patients change beds; a bed or room number is not an identifier."},{text:"Saying the patient's name and waiting for him to say yes",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a confused or hard-of-hearing patient may say yes; ask him to state his name and date of birth and check them against the wristband."},{text:"The diagnosis written on the chart at the end of the bed",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: two patients can have the same diagnosis; a diagnosis is not an identifier."}],explanation:"The first right (right patient) needs two identifiers: name and date of birth, or MRN, checked against the wristband at the bedside."},{question:"A colleague going on her break asks you to give her patient an unlabeled syringe she has drawn up. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Decline, because I cannot verify an unlabeled syringe that I did not prepare",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: without a label nobody knows which drug or dose it is, so the right medication, dose and route cannot be checked."},{text:"Give it, because she is an experienced nurse and knows the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: experience does not replace checking; you would be giving an unknown drug."},{text:"Give it after asking the patient's name, since that confirms the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the patient is only one of the five rights; the drug and dose in the syringe are still unknown."},{text:"Give it and sign the record in her name so that the time is right",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: documentation must be true; each person signs only for what they did themselves."}],explanation:"An unlabeled medication is not given: the right medication, dose and route cannot be checked, and each person documents only what they did."}],tips:["Check the two identifiers against the wristband at the bedside every time, even for a patient you know well.","Ask about allergies before every new medication, and check that the answer matches the record.","If an order or a dose looks unusual, stop and check with the prescriber or the pharmacist before you give it.","Read the full label and scan it; never choose a medication by the color of its cap or its place in the drawer."],activity:{prompt:"On a busy shift the barcode scanner will not read a patient's wristband before a high-alert medication. A colleague suggests giving it now and scanning later. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not bypass it: I get the wristband replaced and scan before giving it",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: BCMA is not bypassed without an official, documented reason, and a high-alert medication always gets an independent double-check. If scanning is impossible, follow your hospital's documented procedure."},{text:"I give it now and scan the wristband later, when the scanner works again",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: scanning after the dose cannot prevent an error; the check must come before it."},{text:"I scan a spare wristband kept at the nurses' station instead",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a wristband that is not on the patient proves nothing about who receives the medication."},{text:"I check only the bed number on the chart and give it as ordered",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a bed number is not an identifier; two identifiers are required."}]}},{id:14,code:"ICS-14",icon:"ClipboardList",accent:"#a855f7",category:"Emergency",title:"Incident Command System (ICS) and hospital codes",subtitle:"HICS, color codes and casualty triage",summary:"The incident command system (ICS) is a standardized approach to managing disasters. Hospitals use HICS (Hospital Incident Command System). Color codes are used for rapid internal alerts, but the meaning of some codes differs from one hospital to another, so every staff member must know their own hospital's code list. Under the Joint Commission and NIMS, a hospital must have an emergency operations plan (EOP) and conduct emergency exercises at least twice a year.",facts:[{label:"Standard",value:"HICS + NIMS"},{label:"Emergency exercises",value:"At least 2 times a year"},{label:"Hospital codes",value:"According to your own hospital's list"},{label:"Triage",value:"START (adults) + JumpSTART (children)"}],steps:[{title:"HICS — five main sections",detail:"1. Command: the incident commander. 2. Operations: patient care, infrastructure and security. 3. Planning: situation status, documentation and resources. 4. Logistics: supplies, communications and food. 5. Finance/Administration: time records, procurement and compensation. Each section has a section chief.",critical:!0},{title:"Color codes (example)",detail:`Code Red: fire.
• Code Blue: cardiac or respiratory arrest.
• Code Black: bomb threat.
• Code Orange: hazardous materials (HazMat).
• Code Yellow: disaster or mass casualty incident (MCI).
• Code Green: evacuation.
• Code Gray or Violet: violent person.
• Code Pink: infant abduction.
• Code Brown: severe weather.
The meaning of codes varies between hospitals; follow your own hospital's list.`,critical:!0},{title:"START triage — for adults",detail:`Simple Triage And Rapid Treatment. Four categories:
• Green (minor): can walk.
• Yellow (delayed): serious injury, but can wait.
• Red (immediate): life-threatening, immediate treatment.
• Black (expectant/deceased): dead or no chance of survival.
Assess each casualty in about 30–60 seconds. If not breathing: open the airway; if breathing starts: red; if not: black. RPM: respirations above 30 per minute = red; no radial pulse or capillary refill longer than 2 seconds = red; cannot follow simple commands = red; otherwise: yellow.`,critical:!0},{title:"JumpSTART — for children (about 1–8 years)",detail:"If the child is not breathing: open the airway; if breathing starts: red. If still not breathing: check for a peripheral pulse; if there is none: black. If there is a pulse: give 5 rescue breaths; if breathing starts: red; if not: black. If the child is breathing: a respiratory rate below 15 or above 45 per minute = red. If it is between 15 and 45, check the peripheral pulse: absent = red. Then AVPU: inappropriate P or U = red; A, V or appropriate P = yellow.",critical:!0},{title:"EOP — emergency operations plan",detail:"Six critical areas: 1) communications, 2) resources and supplies, 3) safety and security, 4) staff roles, 5) utilities (water, power, gas), 6) patient care. A hazard vulnerability analysis is carried out every year. At least two emergency exercises a year, plus separate fire drills under NFPA 101.",critical:!0},{title:"Communication during a major disaster",detail:"First: the hospital telephone system and pagers. Second: cell phones and radios. Third: staff runners who carry messages. Backup: satellite phone. NIMS recommends the use of plain language; many hospitals issue alerts in plain language instead of codes."}],reference:[{heading:"Example hospital codes",points:["Red: fire.","Blue: cardiac or respiratory arrest.","Black: bomb threat.","Orange: hazardous materials.","Yellow: disaster or MCI.","Green: evacuation.","Gray: violent person.","Violet: violent person (in some hospitals).","Pink: infant abduction.","Brown: severe weather.","Silver: person with a weapon (in some hospitals).","The meaning of codes varies; know your own hospital's list."]},{heading:"START triage categories",points:["Green: minor injuries, can walk.","Yellow: serious but stable, can wait.","Red: life-threatening, immediate.","Black: deceased or expectant.","Attach the triage tag to the casualty.","Repeat triage (re-triage) regularly and whenever the condition changes."]},{heading:"HICS roles",points:["Incident Commander.","Public Information Officer (PIO).","Liaison Officer.","Safety Officer.","Operations Section Chief.","Planning Section Chief.","Logistics Section Chief.","Finance/Administration Section Chief."]}],warnings:["Outside the hospital and with the public, use plain language, not codes. Internally, use only the codes on your own hospital's list. JumpSTART is for children aged about 1–8 years; START is for adults. In JumpSTART, a respiratory rate below 15 or above 45 = red (immediate), not black. Do not remove the triage tag from a casualty."],realCase:"Practical case (training example for drills): A car explosion occurred near a hospital, and 42 casualties arrived within the first 10 minutes. Code Yellow was announced and HICS was activated; an incident commander was designated within the first 2 minutes. START triage in the emergency department: 8 red, 12 yellow, 15 green and 7 black. The hospital suspended non-urgent surgery, discharged stable patients and opened additional beds (surge capacity). 35 casualties survived. Lesson: regular HICS exercises and continuous re-triage.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an emergency department (ER) nurse. An alert comes in: a car explosion, with 30 or more casualties on their way. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I immediately start treating the first casualty who arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Without triage and organization, resources are used wrongly."},{text:"I announce Code Yellow, ICS is activated and I get the START triage tags ready",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the mass casualty incident (MCI) protocol."},{text:"I close the door until the team has assembled",correct:!1,feedback:"The casualties are left without treatment."},{text:"I wait for the physician",correct:!1,feedback:"There is no time; you have to act."}]},{prompt:"First casualty: 35 years old, conscious, able to walk, minor bleeding from the shoulder, respiratory rate 20 per minute. Which color?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1,feedback:"Conscious and able to walk; not red."},{text:"Green (walking wounded)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in START, anyone who can walk is green at first and is then re-triaged."},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1,feedback:"A person who can walk is green at first, not yellow."},{text:"Black",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the casualty is conscious."}]},{prompt:"Second casualty: 22 years old, unconscious and not breathing. You have opened the airway (repositioning), but there is still no breathing. Which color in START?",options:[{text:"Red (immediate)",correct:!1,feedback:"In START, a casualty who starts breathing after the airway is opened becomes red; but this casualty is still not breathing."},{text:"Black (expectant/deceased)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in an MCI, if there is no breathing after the airway is opened, the casualty is black. CPR is not performed during triage, so that resources remain for those who have a chance of survival."},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1,feedback:"Yellow is a serious injury that can wait; a casualty still not breathing after airway opening is black."},{text:"Immediate CPR",correct:!1,feedback:"In an MCI, triage comes first; CPR takes resources away from other casualties."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What does Code Pink generally mean?",options:[{text:"Fire",correct:!1,feedback:"That is Code Red."},{text:"Infant abduction",correct:!0,feedback:"In the example list, Code Pink is infant abduction; meanings vary, so know your own hospital's list."},{text:"Cardiac arrest",correct:!1,feedback:"That is Code Blue."},{text:"Severe weather warning",correct:!1,feedback:"Code Pink is not a weather code; in the example list it means infant abduction."}],explanation:"In the example code list, Code Pink means infant abduction. Code meanings vary between hospitals, so every staff member must know their own hospital's list."},{question:"In START triage, what does black mean?",options:[{text:"Minor injuries",correct:!1,feedback:"That is green."},{text:"Deceased or expectant",correct:!0,feedback:"Black means dead or with no chance of survival, such as no breathing after the airway is opened."},{text:"Life-threatening, treated first",correct:!1,feedback:"That is red."},{text:"Stable",correct:!1,feedback:"That is yellow."}],explanation:"In START, black means deceased or expectant (no chance of survival), for example a casualty who still does not breathe after the airway is opened."},{question:"Which is the principal section of HICS?",options:[{text:"Operations",correct:!1,feedback:"It falls under Command."},{text:"Command",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the incident commander is at the top."},{text:"Logistics",correct:!1,feedback:"It falls under Command."},{text:"Finance",correct:!1,feedback:"It falls under Command."}]},{question:"In JumpSTART, a child is breathing 50 times per minute. Which color?",options:[{text:"Black (expectant)",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: black is only for a child who is still not breathing after the airway is opened and 5 rescue breaths are given, or who has no pulse."},{text:"Red (immediate)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a respiratory rate below 15 or above 45 = red."},{text:"Yellow (delayed)",correct:!1,feedback:"Yellow only if the respiratory rate is between 15 and 45, a pulse is present and AVPU is appropriate."},{text:"Green (minor)",correct:!1,feedback:"Green is for those who can walk."}]},{question:"During a mass casualty incident, an adult with a cut on his arm walks to the triage area on his own. Which START category is he given first?",options:[{text:"Green (minor), and he is re-triaged later",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in START anyone who can walk is green at first; triage is repeated when the condition changes."},{text:"Red (immediate), because he is bleeding",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: he can walk, so he is not red at first; he is reassessed if his condition gets worse."},{text:"Yellow (delayed), because of the cut on his arm",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: in START a walking casualty is green; yellow is for those who cannot walk and are seriously hurt."},{text:"Black (expectant), because he can wait the longest",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: black is for the dead or those with no chance of survival, not for someone who can wait."}],explanation:"START begins with one question: can the casualty walk? If yes, green. Triage is repeated whenever the condition changes."},{question:"A visitor in the waiting area asks a nurse what the code just announced over the speakers means. What does the nurse do?",options:[{text:"She tells the visitor in plain language what to do, without code words",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: with the public, plain language is used, not codes; say calmly what people should do."},{text:"She says it is a staff secret and walks away without explaining",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the visitor may need instructions to stay safe."},{text:"She repeats the color code so that the visitor can look it up",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: code meanings differ between hospitals and the public does not know them; this adds confusion and fear."},{text:"She tells the visitor to leave quickly by the elevator before it gets busy",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: this may go against the plan, and elevators are not used in a fire or an earthquake; follow the plan's instructions."}],explanation:"Outside the hospital and with the public, use plain language, not codes. Codes are for staff only and follow your hospital's list."}],tips:["Keep a copy of your hospital's code list at your workstation and learn what each code asks you to do.","Know before an incident where you report and to whom under your hospital's emergency plan.","During an incident, take tasks from your supervisor in the command structure and report back when each task is done.","Take part in every emergency exercise as if it were real; that is where gaps in the plan are found."],activity:{prompt:"Your hospital's code for a mass casualty incident (MCI) is announced. You are a nurse on a surgical ward, and off-duty colleagues start arriving at your ward. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I follow the plan: I report to my supervisor and send arriving staff to the point the plan names",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in HICS, staff are assigned through the command structure; going wherever you choose creates crowds in one place and gaps in another."},{text:"I go straight to the emergency department, because that is where the casualties will be",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: staff who were not called crowd the emergency department and leave your own patients without care."},{text:"I phone my patients' families to warn them that a disaster has happened",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: information for the public comes from the designated officer (PIO), and the phone lines are needed for the response."},{text:"I carry on with my usual work and ignore the code, since I am not in the emergency department",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: every ward has a role in the plan, for example preparing beds to receive patients."}]}},{id:15,code:"QUAKE-15",icon:"Activity",accent:"#a16207",category:"Disaster",title:"Earthquakes in the hospital",subtitle:"Drop, Cover, Hold On, protecting patients and inspecting the building",summary:"Earthquakes are a major hazard for hospitals: bedbound patients cannot escape immediately, and equipment, gas cylinders and heavy devices can fall. Under FEMA and the WHO Hospital Safety Index, hospitals must be built and prepared so that they keep functioning after an earthquake as well. The Kurdistan Region lies on the Zagros belt, an active earthquake zone. This lesson explains what to do during the shaking, how to protect patients and how the building is inspected after the earthquake.",facts:[{label:"Kurdistan's seismic zone",value:"The Zagros belt"},{label:"During the shaking",value:"Drop, Cover, Hold On"},{label:"Standard",value:"FEMA + WHO Hospital Safety Index"},{label:"Building inspection",value:"ATC-20 (green · yellow · red)"}],steps:[{title:"1. During the shaking — Drop, Cover, Hold On",detail:"Drop: immediately get down on your hands and knees. Cover: protect your head and neck under a sturdy table, away from windows and heavy equipment. Hold On: hold on to the table and stay there until the shaking stops. Do not run to the door, outside or to the elevator.",critical:!0},{title:"2. Bedbound patients",detail:"Lock the bed wheels. The patient stays in bed and protects their head with a pillow. If there is time, move objects that could fall away from the patient. ICU patients and patients on ventilators stay where they are and are protected with pillows. Do not move patients out during the shaking; the building is still moving.",critical:!0},{title:"3. After the shaking — the first five minutes",detail:"1. Check yourself and your colleagues: is anyone injured? 2. Count and check the patients. 3. Check the ventilators and the oxygen supply. 4. Check the gas lines; if you smell gas or hear it escaping, only trained, designated staff shut the gas valve, and only if they can reach it safely, without entering the area where the gas has collected and without any spark; otherwise keep everyone away from that area and call 115. 5. Notify engineering and the ICS.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Building inspection — rapid damage assessment",detail:`ATC-20 has three placard categories:
• Green (inspected): inspected and safe to use.
• Yellow (restricted use): limited use.
• Red (unsafe): not safe; do not enter.
Only engineers or trained, designated inspectors post the placards. Do not go back into a damaged building without an inspection.`,critical:!0},{title:"5. Aftershocks",detail:"After the main earthquake, aftershocks occur most often in the early period and can continue for days or weeks; they can bring down weakened buildings. Keep patients in safe areas until the engineering inspection. With every aftershock, Drop, Cover, Hold On again.",critical:!0},{title:"6. Evacuating the building",detail:"Only if the building receives a red placard or engineering requests it. Prepare the patient list before evacuating. Horizontal evacuation before vertical. 3 people for every ICU or ventilated patient. Do not carry valuables. The assembly point must be away from buildings, power lines and walls."}],reference:[{heading:"Drop, Cover, Hold On",points:["DROP: immediately onto your knees.","COVER: under a sturdy table, protect your head and neck.","HOLD ON: hold on to the table and stay there.","Stay until the shaking stops.","Do not run to the door.","Do not use the elevator.","Do not stay near windows."]},{heading:"ATC-20 placards",points:["Green: inspected, can be used.","Yellow: restricted use, limited entry.","Red: unsafe, do not enter.","Do not go back into a building without a placard.","Only engineers or designated inspectors post the placards.","After every strong aftershock, the inspection is repeated."]},{heading:"Weak points in a hospital",points:["Nonstructural elements: gas cylinders, CT, MRI and cabinets.","Falling objects: equipment and supplies on shelves.","Breaking glass: windows.","Power outage: generators and UPS.","Water outage: reserve tanks.","Communications: radios and satellite phones."]}],warnings:["Do not use elevators during or after the shaking. Do not go back into a damaged building without an engineering inspection and placard. Aftershocks can bring down weakened buildings; keep patients in safe areas. The gas valve is shut only if there is a smell of gas or a leaking sound, only by trained, designated staff and only if it can be reached safely; do not cut off the oxygen supply to patients who need it."],realCase:"On February 6, 2023, two strong earthquakes (Mw 7.8 and Mw 7.5, according to the USGS) struck southern Türkiye and northern Syria. According to news reports, in Iskenderun, part of the state hospital collapsed and people died. Lessons for Kurdistan: build hospitals to seismic codes, anchor equipment and cylinders to the walls, and train regularly in Drop, Cover, Hold On and evacuation.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an ICU nurse. At 02:47 at night the ground shakes violently. There are two patients on ventilators and four other patients. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I run to the door",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: you may fall or be struck by falling objects."},{text:"Drop, Cover, Hold On under a sturdy table, and if I am close by, I lock the bed wheels",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: your own safety first; an injured staff member cannot protect patients."},{text:"I take the patients outside immediately",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: the building is still shaking."},{text:"I close the room door and stand in front of it",correct:!1,feedback:"In front of the door is not a safe place; take cover under a sturdy table."}]},{prompt:"The shaking stopped after 45 seconds. You are back on your feet. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"I leave right away on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the patients need you, and leaving without an inspection is dangerous."},{text:"I check myself and the patients, check the ventilators and the oxygen, and if I smell gas I report it so that trained staff shut the gas valve only if it is safe to reach",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: check yourself, the patients and the equipment."},{text:"I take the patients to the elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are not used after an earthquake."},{text:"I stay where I am and do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"You need to check the patients and be ready for aftershocks."}]},{prompt:"The check is complete. A patient near the door has been hit by a falling object and has a neck injury. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I lift him immediately and put him back in bed",correct:!1,feedback:"Before moving him, spinal precautions (protecting the neck and back) are needed."},{text:"I call a Code Blue or the trauma team, protect the neck and back and notify engineering to inspect the building",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: treating the injured and building safety together."},{text:"I leave on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the patient needs you."},{text:"I take him to the emergency department by elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Elevators are not used after an earthquake; use the stairs."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is Drop, Cover, Hold On?",options:[{text:"Running to the door and standing in the doorway until the shaking stops",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: a doorway does not protect you, and running can make you fall."},{text:"Getting down on your knees, under a sturdy table and holding on to the table",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the best protection during the shaking."},{text:"Standing near a window",correct:!1,feedback:"Danger from glass."},{text:"Running to the elevator to get out of the building before the shaking gets worse",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not use elevators during or after the shaking; drop, cover under a sturdy table and hold on."}],explanation:"Drop onto your hands and knees, take cover under a sturdy table away from windows and heavy equipment, and hold on until the shaking stops."},{question:"What does a red placard (ATC-20) mean?",options:[{text:"It is safe",correct:!1,feedback:"That is green."},{text:"Unsafe: do not enter",correct:!0,feedback:"Red (unsafe) means do not enter; only engineers or designated inspectors post the placards."},{text:"Restricted entry",correct:!1,feedback:"That is yellow."},{text:"The building has completely collapsed",correct:!1,feedback:"Not necessarily; red means it is not safe to enter."}],explanation:"Under ATC-20, a red placard means unsafe: do not enter. Green means inspected and safe to use, and yellow means restricted use."},{question:"When do aftershocks occur?",options:[{text:"Never",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: they always follow strong earthquakes."},{text:"Mostly soon after the main earthquake, and they can continue for days or weeks",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: that is why you must not go back into a building without an inspection."},{text:"Only in the first 5 minutes after the main earthquake; after that the building is safe",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: they can also occur much later."},{text:"Only after a week, once the ground has settled after the main earthquake",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: they occur most often in the early period."}]},{question:"After the earthquake, what do you do with gas lines and gas equipment?",options:[{text:"No check is needed",correct:!1,feedback:"There is a risk of a gas leak."},{text:"I check them; if there is a smell of gas or a leaking sound, trained staff shut the gas valve only if it is safe to reach",correct:!0,feedback:"Only trained, designated staff shut the gas valve, only for a smell or leak and only if safe to reach."},{text:"I walk round and shut every valve myself straight away, including the patients' oxygen, and only after that check the gas lines",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: cutting off oxygen puts patients at risk."},{text:"I check them only if a device or pipe is visibly broken; a smell of gas alone is not a reason",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: check all lines and equipment; a smell of gas or a leaking sound is a warning sign."}],explanation:"Check the gas lines. With a smell of gas or a leaking sound, only trained, designated staff shut the gas valve, only if it is safe to reach, and never the patients' oxygen."},{question:"After the shaking, engineering posts a yellow placard (ATC-20) on your wing. What does it mean for your ward?",options:[{text:"Restricted use: enter and work only as engineering allows",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: yellow means restricted use; entry only in the way engineering sets."},{text:"Inspected and safe: normal work carries on everywhere",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: that is the meaning of a green placard."},{text:"Unsafe: nobody may enter, and all patients leave at once",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: that is the meaning of a red placard."},{text:"Not yet inspected: staff decide for themselves where to work",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a placard is posted after inspection, and staff do not decide on building safety by themselves."}],explanation:"ATC-20: green means inspected and usable; yellow means restricted use; red means unsafe, do not enter. Only engineers or designated inspectors post placards."},{question:"An aftershock starts while you are pushing a patient in a wheelchair along a corridor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop away from windows, lock the brakes, protect the patient's head and Drop, Cover, Hold On",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: with every aftershock, Drop, Cover, Hold On again; the patient stays put with his head protected."},{text:"Run with the wheelchair to the nearest exit before the shaking gets stronger",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: running during shaking exposes you and the patient to falls and falling objects."},{text:"Take the elevator down, because the patient cannot use the stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: elevators are not used during or after the shaking; it may stop with you both inside."},{text:"Leave the patient and shelter on my own under the nearest table",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: do not leave the patient alone; lock the brakes, protect his head and protect yourself close by."}],explanation:"Aftershocks follow the main earthquake; with each one, Drop, Cover, Hold On again, do not use the elevator and do not leave the patient alone."}],tips:["Before an earthquake, report unsecured gas cylinders, tall cabinets and heavy equipment in your area to engineering.","Keep bed wheels locked whenever the bed is not being moved.","Know the sturdy table nearest to where you usually work, away from windows and heavy equipment.","Keep an up-to-date patient list and a flashlight where you can reach them quickly."],activity:{prompt:"Strong shaking starts while you are standing next to a bedbound patient on a general ward. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I lock the bed wheels, protect his head with a pillow and Drop, Cover, Hold On nearby",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the patient stays in bed with his head protected, and you protect yourself away from windows and heavy equipment."},{text:"I push the bed quickly to the elevator to get him down to the ground floor",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: elevators are not used during or after the shaking, and patients are not moved while the building is moving."},{text:"I stand in the doorway of the room and hold the frame until the shaking stops",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a doorway is not a safe place; protect yourself under a sturdy table."},{text:"I run outside to see whether the building has been damaged",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: running during the shaking causes injuries, and your patient is left alone."}]}},{id:16,code:"COLD-16",icon:"Snowflake",accent:"#0ea5e9",category:"Safety",title:"Winter hazards",subtitle:"Hypothermia, frostbite, the backup generator and slip prevention",summary:"Winter in Kurdistan brings specific hazards for hospitals: snow and ice, slips, power outages, hypothermia (especially in the elderly) and frostbite. Hypothermia is generally classified into three levels: mild (32–35°C), moderate (28–32°C) and severe (below 28°C). Generators tested according to NFPA 110, reserve fuel and extra blankets are the foundations of protection.",facts:[{label:"Mild hypothermia",value:"32–35°C"},{label:"Moderate hypothermia",value:"28–32°C"},{label:"Severe hypothermia",value:"Below 28°C"},{label:"Power restored by generator",value:"Within 10 seconds (NFPA 110, Type 10)"}],steps:[{title:"Hypothermia — recognition",detail:"Mild (32–35°C): shivering, confusion and slurred speech. Moderate (28–32°C): shivering stops, the heart rate and breathing slow and the pupils dilate. Severe (below 28°C): unconsciousness, heart rhythm disturbances (J wave/Osborn wave), VF or asystole. Core temperature is measured rectally or esophageally, not on the skin.",critical:!0},{title:"Rewarming — three methods",detail:"Passive (mild): blankets, a warm room and removing wet clothing. Active external (moderate): forced warm air (Bair Hugger) and warmed IV fluids (38–42°C). Active core (severe): warm, humidified oxygen, warmed IV fluids, and ECMO or CPB for unstable patients or those in cardiac arrest. Move the patient gently and keep them horizontal; rough handling can trigger VF.",critical:!0},{title:"Frostbite — four degrees",detail:"1st degree: white or yellow skin and numbness. 2nd degree: blisters with clear fluid. 3rd degree: blood-filled blisters and deeper damage. 4th degree: damage reaches muscle and bone (tissue death). Rewarming: water at 37–39°C, for about 15–30 minutes, until the skin is soft. Do not use: hot water, direct heat (fire or a heater) or massage. If there is a risk of refreezing, do not rewarm.",critical:!0},{title:"Backup generator — NFPA 110",detail:"Type 10: power is restored within 10 seconds. Class: the minimum running time in hours without refueling; hospitals use Class X (per NFPA 99), often 96 hours in earthquake zones. Level 1: for life safety and critical loads (ICU, OR, laboratory). Testing: weekly visual inspection, monthly run under load for at least 30 minutes and a 4-hour run under load at least once every 36 months.",critical:!0},{title:"Preventing slips and falls",detail:'Clear snow and ice from entrances and walkways regularly. Spread salt or calcium chloride on walkways. Nonslip shoes for staff. Winter tires for ambulances. "Wet Floor" signs in wet areas.',critical:!0},{title:"Special winter situations",detail:"Carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning (from generators or stoves): headache, confusion and nausea. SpO₂ is not reliable; CO-oximetry is required. Treatment: 100% O₂, and hyperbaric oxygen in severe cases at the specialist's discretion. Winter is the season of rising influenza and RSV in children; have a surge plan ready for the emergency department."}],reference:[{heading:"Hypothermia stages and treatment",points:["Mild (32–35): passive rewarming, and warm drinks if the patient is alert and can swallow safely.","Moderate (28–32): Bair Hugger and warmed IV fluids.","Severe (below 28): active core rewarming and ECMO/CPB.","Move the patient gently and keep them horizontal.",'"Not dead until warm and dead": death is not declared on the basis of cold alone.',"Continue CPR during rewarming (generally until about 32°C) before deciding to stop."]},{heading:"Recognizing CO poisoning",points:["Headache, dizziness and confusion.","Cherry-red lips are a late and rare sign.","SpO₂ can appear falsely normal.","The COHb level is measured by CO-oximetry.","Treatment: 100% O₂ until the symptoms resolve (usually several hours).","Hyperbaric oxygen for severe cases, pregnant women or neurological symptoms, at the specialist's discretion."]},{heading:"Generator testing (NFPA 110)",points:["Weekly: visual inspection of the generator and fuel.","Monthly: run under load for at least 30 minutes.","At least every 36 months: a 4-hour run under load.","Fuel: according to the generator's class (for example, 96 hours).","Power restoration: within 10 seconds (Type 10).","Batteries: starting batteries and UPS."]}],warnings:['"Not dead until warm and dead": a patient with severe hypothermia can be in asystole and still be saved; continue CPR and rewarming before deciding to stop. Do not rewarm frostbite with hot water; use only 37–39°C. Test the generator regularly and under load; an untested generator may fail when it is needed.'],realCase:"Practical case (training example): Heavy snow cut off power to the city for 48 hours. The hospital generator started within 10 seconds (Type 10), but the hospital's fuel was enough for only 12 hours, and a new tanker had difficulty getting through. During this time many elderly patients arrived with mild hypothermia and were treated with blankets, Bair Huggers and warmed fluids. Two staff members at the entrance developed mild frostbite. Lessons: reserve fuel according to the generator's class and a refueling plan, Bair Huggers on every unit, and winter footwear and clothing for staff.",caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are an emergency department nurse. The city's power is out because of snow and the generator is running. A 72-year-old patient arrives after spending 45 minutes in the street. He is shivering violently, is confused and has a GCS of 13. What is the first step?",options:[{text:"Immediate active core rewarming",correct:!1,feedback:"Too aggressive; for mild hypothermia, passive and active external rewarming are enough."},{text:"I measure the core temperature (rectal/esophageal), remove wet clothing and start passive rewarming",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: shivering and confusion indicate mild hypothermia; a Bair Hugger and warmed fluids can be added."},{text:"I just give him a blanket",correct:!1,feedback:"Measuring the core temperature is needed to determine the level."},{text:"I give him hot water to drink",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: he is confused (risk of aspiration), and hot water burns."}]},{prompt:"The core temperature is 27.5°C, the GCS is 6 and the pulse is weak. What is the next step?",options:[{text:"Passive rewarming and blankets",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough for severe hypothermia; active core rewarming is needed."},{text:"Active core rewarming: warm, humidified oxygen, warmed IV fluids and preparing ECMO/CPB",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: severe hypothermia; move the patient gently."},{text:"I start CPR immediately even though there is a pulse",correct:!1,feedback:"CPR is not performed on a patient who has a pulse; active core rewarming is needed."},{text:"I pronounce him dead",correct:!1,feedback:'"Not dead until warm and dead": incorrect.'}]},{prompt:"Active core rewarming has been started. After 3 hours, the core temperature is 29°C. The ECG shows VF and the patient is pulseless. What is the correct step?",options:[{text:"I give no shocks until 32°C",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: shocks must not be withheld until 32°C; AHA recommends defibrillation alongside rewarming."},{text:"I continue CPR and deliver shocks (per the ACLS algorithm; per ERC, up to 3 shocks below 30°C), with active rewarming and ECMO if available",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a cold heart may not respond to shocks until the temperature rises above 30°C, so rewarming is the most important treatment."},{text:"I only give medications and never deliver a shock",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: VF requires a shock."},{text:"I pronounce him dead",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: he can still be saved."}]}],quiz:[{question:"At what temperature is hypothermia severe?",options:[{text:"Below 35°C",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the threshold for mild hypothermia."},{text:"Below 28°C",correct:!0,feedback:"Severe hypothermia is a core temperature below 28°C; moderate is 28–32°C and mild 32–35°C."},{text:"Below 32°C",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the threshold for moderate hypothermia."},{text:"Below 20°C",correct:!1,feedback:"Too low: severe hypothermia already starts below 28°C."}],explanation:"Hypothermia is generally classified by core temperature: mild 32–35°C, moderate 28–32°C and severe below 28°C."},{question:"What water temperature is used to rewarm frostbite?",options:[{text:"25°C",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too cold."},{text:"37–39°C",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: for about 15–30 minutes, until the skin is soft (Wilderness Medical Society)."},{text:"50°C",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too hot; it burns the skin."},{text:"Boiling water",correct:!1,feedback:"Dangerous: burns."}]},{question:"Under NFPA 110, within what time is power restored by the generator?",options:[{text:"Less than 1 minute, after an automatic start",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too slow."},{text:"Less than 10 seconds (Type 10)",correct:!0,feedback:"Type 10 under NFPA 110 means the generator restores power within 10 seconds."},{text:"Less than 1 hour",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too slow."},{text:"Less than half an hour, after a manual start",correct:!1,feedback:"Far too slow."}],explanation:"Under NFPA 110, a Type 10 generator restores power within 10 seconds; it is tested regularly under load so that it works when needed."},{question:'What does "Not dead until warm and dead" mean?',options:[{text:"It has no meaning",correct:!1,feedback:"It has an important meaning."},{text:"Before death is declared, a patient with severe hypothermia must be rewarmed and CPR continued (generally until about 32°C)",correct:!0,feedback:"Death is not declared on cold alone: rewarm the patient and continue CPR, generally until about 32°C."},{text:"Patients with severe hypothermia and asystole are already dead, so CPR is stopped and death is declared at once without rewarming",correct:!1,feedback:"The opposite: a patient with severe hypothermia can be in asystole and still be saved."},{text:"It applies only to mild hypothermia: the patient is rewarmed slowly with warm blankets, and CPR is never started before that",correct:!1,feedback:"It specifically concerns severe hypothermia."}],explanation:"A patient with severe hypothermia can be in asystole and still be saved, so death is not declared on cold alone: rewarm and continue CPR, generally until about 32°C."},{question:"A patient with frostbitten feet asks for a heater to be placed next to his feet. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Explain that direct heat is not used, and rewarm in water at 37–39°C",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: frostbite is rewarmed only in water at 37–39°C; numb skin cannot feel a burn."},{text:"Place the heater close, because faster warming saves more tissue",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: direct heat (fire or a heater) burns numb skin."},{text:"Massage the feet firmly to bring the blood back into them",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: massage causes more damage to frozen tissue."},{text:"Soak the feet in water as hot as he can bear, to speed things up",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: hot water burns numb skin; use only 37–39°C."}],explanation:"Frostbite is rewarmed in water at 37–39°C until the skin is soft. No hot water, no direct heat and no massage."},{question:"A family brings a relative with headache, dizziness and confusion; they ran a generator inside the house. The pulse oximeter shows a normal SpO₂. What do you conclude?",options:[{text:"SpO₂ can be falsely normal; CO poisoning is still suspected and CO-oximetry is needed",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: in CO poisoning SpO₂ can look falsely normal; the COHb level is measured by CO-oximetry."},{text:"A normal SpO₂ rules out CO poisoning, so we look for another cause",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a pulse oximeter cannot tell CO from oxygen, so a normal SpO₂ does not rule it out."},{text:"It is most likely a winter virus, so send him home with advice to rest",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a generator indoors makes CO likely; sending him back home is dangerous."},{text:"CO is suspected only once his lips turn cherry red, so we wait and watch",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: cherry-red lips are a late and rare sign; waiting delays treatment."}],explanation:"In winter, generators and stoves cause CO poisoning. SpO₂ can be falsely normal; CO-oximetry is needed and treatment follows the physician."}],tips:["Report ice or snow at entrances and walkways to the responsible team as soon as you see it.",'Wear nonslip shoes in winter and put out a "Wet Floor" sign where snow is carried in.',"Keep extra blankets ready on your ward for power cuts and for cold patients.","Never use a generator, a charcoal burner or an open-flame heater inside a closed room, even to warm a patient area."],activity:{prompt:"An elderly man found outdoors in the cold is brought to the emergency department. He is shivering and confused, his speech is slurred and his clothes are wet. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I handle him gently, remove the wet clothes, cover him and measure core temperature",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: these are signs of mild hypothermia; gentle handling, removing wet clothing and blankets come first, and core temperature guides the next step."},{text:"I rub his arms and legs hard with a towel to get the blood flowing again",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: rough handling can trigger VF in a cold patient; handle him gently."},{text:"I sit him upright and give him a hot drink straight away to warm him",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: he is confused, so nothing is given by mouth; warm drinks are only for an alert patient who can swallow safely."},{text:"I put his hands and feet in hot water so that he warms up quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: hot water and direct heat injure cold skin; for mild hypothermia use blankets and a warm room."}]}},{id:17,code:"MCI-17",icon:"Users",accent:"#b91c1c",category:"Emergency",title:"Mass-casualty incidents and triage",subtitle:"When casualties outnumber the hospital's resources: the plan, triage colors and every staff member's role",summary:"A mass-casualty incident (MCI) is an incident in which the number of casualties is greater than the staff, space and supplies available. The hospital then moves from routine work to its emergency plan: the incident command system (ICS) is activated, staff work in their assigned roles and triage decides who is treated first. The aim is the best outcome for the greatest number of people. The actual plan, codes and roles are your own hospital's; follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority.",facts:[{label:"Red",value:"Immediate care"},{label:"Yellow",value:"Serious but can wait a little"},{label:"Green",value:"Minor, can walk"},{label:"Black",value:"As your hospital's protocol defines"}],blocks:[{heading:"Triage colors as a concept",body:`Triage sorts casualties by how urgently they need care, not by who arrived first or who shouts loudest. Most systems use colors:
• Red (immediate): life is in danger but treatment can help now.
• Yellow (delayed): serious but can wait a little.
• Green (minor): walking wounded.
• Black: deceased or with very little chance, as your hospital's protocol defines.

Triage is repeated: a patient's condition changes, so the color can change too. Your hospital sets the actual system (for example START for adults and JumpSTART for children) and who may triage.`},{heading:"Hospital surge: what changes",points:["The emergency plan is announced with your hospital's code and the incident commander leads.","Planned operations and non-urgent work may be postponed and stable patients discharged or moved, by decision of the command team.","Off-duty staff come in only when called and report to the staff pool, not straight to a ward.","One entrance for casualties, a separate area for each triage color and a clear record for every patient."]}],steps:[{title:"Hear the code and go to your role",detail:"When the MCI code is announced, finish only what can be stopped safely, then go to the role your hospital's plan gives you. If you have no role, report to the staff pool. Do not go to the emergency entrance on your own.",critical:!0},{title:"Triage at the entrance",detail:"Trained staff triage every casualty at the entrance and give a color; the casualty goes to the area of that color. Triage is quick: check walking, breathing, circulation and responsiveness as your protocol says; do not stop for long treatment during triage.",critical:!0},{title:"Life-saving actions first",detail:"In the red area, the first actions are those that save life: open the airway, stop severe bleeding and get help from the team. Treatment follows your hospital's protocols.",critical:!0},{title:"Re-triage and records",detail:"Check patients again, especially in the yellow and green areas; a patient who gets worse moves to a more urgent color. Each patient gets an identification and a record (tag or form) that stays with them."},{title:"Communicate through the command chain",detail:"Report numbers and needs to your team leader, not by phone to everyone. Information for families and the media goes only through the people the command team assigns. When the incident ends, take part in the debriefing."}],warnings:["Do not treat casualties in the order they arrive or by who shouts loudest: the quiet casualty may be the most critical.","Do not go to the incident scene or leave your post on your own: the hospital needs you where the plan puts you.","Do not give information about casualties to families, the media or social media yourself: only the assigned people do that."],tips:["Learn your role in the MCI plan before an incident: where you go and who you report to.","Know where the triage tags, forms and extra supplies are kept in your department.","Keep your phone number up to date with your department so the call-in message reaches you.","Take part in drills; an MCI plan works only if staff have practised it."],activity:{prompt:"You are a nurse in a surgical ward. The MCI code is announced while you are giving routine medicines. Your ward has free beds and two patients ready for discharge. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I finish what can be stopped safely, tell my charge nurse about the free beds and the patients ready to go, then follow my role",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the command team needs to know the available beds, and you work in your assigned role."},{text:"I run to the emergency department to help with triage",correct:!1,feedback:"Going on your own causes confusion; triage is done by assigned trained staff, and your ward needs you too."},{text:"I discharge the two patients myself straight away to free the beds",correct:!1,feedback:"Discharge during a surge is decided by the command team and the treating doctor, not by one person alone."},{text:"I carry on my normal work and wait until someone calls me",correct:!1,feedback:"The code means the plan is active now; report the free beds and your status to your charge nurse."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are assigned to triage at the emergency entrance after a building collapse. Casualties arrive: a man shouting with a broken arm, a quiet, pale woman breathing fast and a young man walking with minor cuts. Who gets the red color?",options:[{text:"The quiet, pale woman who is breathing fast",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: signs of shock and breathing problems threaten life; being quiet does not mean the injury is minor."},{text:"The shouting man, because he is in great pain",correct:!1,feedback:"Pain and noise are not the measure; a broken arm alone is usually yellow."},{text:"The young man who is walking, because he arrived first",correct:!1,feedback:"Someone who can walk is usually green; the order of arrival does not decide."},{text:"All three get red, just to be safe",correct:!1,feedback:"If everyone is red, triage fails and the most critical wait longer."}]},{prompt:"Later, the young man with minor cuts, who was green, becomes confused and very pale in the green area. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell the team leader and he is re-triaged to a more urgent color",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: triage is repeated; a worse condition means a more urgent color."},{text:"He stays green because his tag is already written",correct:!1,feedback:"The tag is not final; the patient's condition decides."},{text:"I ask him to wait until the red patients are finished",correct:!1,feedback:"He may now be critical; re-triage cannot wait."},{text:"I send him home because his cuts are small",correct:!1,feedback:"Confusion and paleness are danger signs; never send home a patient who has not been checked."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative safety scenario: After a bus crash, many casualties arrived at a hospital within a short time. The emergency plan was announced and the incident commander took charge. Two trained nurses triaged at a single entrance and each patient went to the area of their color. A quiet elderly woman with no visible wound was given red after her breathing and pulse were checked; she had internal bleeding and went to surgery quickly. Off-duty staff who came in reported to the staff pool and were sent where they were needed. In the debriefing, the team noted that drills and clear roles had prevented crowding at the entrance.",quiz:[{question:"Many casualties arrive at once. What decides the order of treatment?",options:[{text:"Triage by trained staff, by how urgent each case is",correct:!0},{text:"The order in which casualties reach the entrance",correct:!1},{text:"The casualties' families, who know them best",correct:!1},{text:"The casualty who calls loudest for help",correct:!1}],explanation:"Triage sorts casualties by urgency so that those whose life is in danger and who can be saved are treated first."},{question:"A casualty walks in with a small cut on the hand and talks normally. Which color usually fits?",options:[{text:"Green (minor)",correct:!0},{text:"Red (immediate)",correct:!1},{text:"Yellow (delayed)",correct:!1},{text:"Black (per protocol)",correct:!1}],explanation:"Someone who walks and talks with a small wound is usually green; check again later because the condition can change."},{question:"You are off duty and hear about a major incident. What is correct?",options:[{text:"I come only if called and report to the staff pool",correct:!0},{text:"I drive straight to the scene to help the casualties",correct:!1},{text:"I go to the emergency entrance and start treating",correct:!1},{text:"I phone the ward many times to ask for news",correct:!1}],explanation:"Coming on your own creates crowding and confusion; the plan calls staff in and assigns them through the staff pool."},{question:"A patient tagged yellow gets worse. What happens?",options:[{text:"He is re-triaged and moves to a more urgent color",correct:!0},{text:"He keeps the yellow tag until the incident ends",correct:!1},{text:"He waits until all the red patients are treated",correct:!1},{text:"He is moved to the green area to free space",correct:!1}],explanation:"Triage is repeated; the color follows the patient's current condition, not the earlier tag."},{question:"A journalist asks you how many casualties have died. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I refer him to the person the command team assigned",correct:!0},{text:"I give him the number I heard from colleagues",correct:!1},{text:"I post a short update on social media myself",correct:!1},{text:"I tell him the names of the most serious cases",correct:!1}],explanation:"Information about casualties is given only by assigned people; wrong or private information harms patients and families."},{question:"Which statement about an MCI is wrong?",options:[{text:"Everyone is treated in the order they arrive",correct:!0},{text:"Triage aims at the best outcome for the most people",correct:!1},{text:"Each patient's record stays with the patient",correct:!1},{text:"Staff work in the roles the plan gives them",correct:!1}],explanation:"In an MCI, treatment follows triage priority, not the order of arrival."}]},{id:18,code:"SEC-18",icon:"ShieldAlert",accent:"#7c3aed",category:"Safety",title:"Violence and security in the hospital",subtitle:"Recognize warning signs, calm the situation, raise the alarm and protect patients and staff",summary:"Health workers face verbal abuse, threats and physical violence, often from frightened and stressed patients or relatives. Most incidents can be prevented or reduced by noticing warning signs early and speaking calmly. When there is danger, your task is not to fight: raise the alarm, keep a safe distance, protect patients and colleagues and let security staff and the police (104) deal with the person. Every incident is reported, even when no one is hurt. Your hospital's code (often called Code Gray or Violet) and its procedure apply.",blocks:[{heading:"Warning signs",points:["Raised voice, swearing or repeated demands.","Clenched fists, pacing or standing too close.","Angry staring, threats or hitting objects.","Signs of intoxication or severe distress."]},{heading:"Talking to calm (de-escalation)",points:["Stay calm and speak slowly in a low voice.","Listen and repeat back in your own words what the person needs.","Explain what will happen and when, without promises you cannot keep.","Keep a safe distance, keep your hands visible and do not block the person's way out."]}],steps:[{title:"Notice early",detail:"Watch for warning signs in the waiting area and at the bedside. Tell a colleague or your team leader early; do not wait until the person becomes violent."},{title:"Calm the situation",detail:"Speak calmly, listen and explain. Stand at a safe distance with the exit behind you. One staff member talks; the others stay near and ready.",critical:!0},{title:"Raise the alarm",detail:"If the person threatens or the situation gets worse, use the alarm button or the internal emergency number and announce your hospital's code. If there is a weapon or a serious threat, call the police on 104 as your hospital's procedure says.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect patients, colleagues and yourself",detail:"Move patients and visitors away from the person, close doors between you if it is safe and leave the area if you are in danger. Do not try to restrain or disarm anyone: that is the work of trained security staff and the police.",critical:!0},{title:"Report and support",detail:"Report every incident, including verbal abuse, on your hospital's form: what happened, when and who was there. Get medical care if you are hurt and ask for support afterwards."}],warnings:["Never try to fight, restrain or disarm a violent person: you and others can be badly hurt. Raise the alarm and keep away.","Do not turn your back on an angry person or let them stand between you and the exit.","Do not argue with, shout at or threaten the person: it makes violence more likely."],tips:["Know where the alarm buttons are and how your hospital's code is announced.","Tell a colleague where you are when you see a patient or relative who has been aggressive before.","Give waiting relatives clear and regular updates; long waits without information cause much anger.","Report verbal abuse too: reports help the hospital plan security and staffing."],activity:{prompt:"You are a receptionist in the emergency department. A relative shouts that his father has waited too long, hits the counter and comes closer. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I speak calmly, keep the counter between us, listen and signal a colleague to call security",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: calm words, a safe distance and early help together reduce the risk."},{text:"I shout back that he must wait his turn like everyone else",correct:!1,feedback:"Shouting back raises tension and makes violence more likely."},{text:"I leave the counter and turn my back on him",correct:!1,feedback:"Turning your back on an angry person can be dangerous; keep your distance but stay alert."},{text:"I tell him to calm down or the police will arrest him",correct:!1,feedback:"Threats raise tension; call for help without threatening him."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse on a ward. A patient's brother becomes angry and starts throwing objects near the nurses' station. Two other patients are close by. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I raise the alarm with the hospital's code, move the nearby patients away and keep my distance",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: alarm, protect the patients and stay safe."},{text:"I grab his arms to stop him",correct:!1,feedback:"Restraint is for trained security staff; you may be hurt."},{text:"I carry on working and ignore him",correct:!1,feedback:"Violence can grow fast; ignoring it puts patients at risk."},{text:"I lock myself in the office and leave the patients",correct:!1,feedback:"Protect yourself, but take the patients with you or move them away if you can do so safely."}]},{prompt:"Security staff arrive and take the man away. No one was hurt. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I check on patients and colleagues, then report the incident on the hospital form",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: every incident is reported, even with no injury."},{text:"Nothing, because no one was hurt",correct:!1,feedback:"Reports help prevent the next incident; an injury is not needed."},{text:"I post a video of the incident online to warn others",correct:!1,feedback:"This breaks patient privacy; report through the hospital."},{text:"I tell his family he is no longer allowed in the hospital",correct:!1,feedback:"Visiting decisions are made by management under hospital rules, not by one staff member."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative safety scenario: Late at night in an emergency department, a young man arrived with his injured friend and became angry at the wait. A nurse noticed he was pacing and shouting. She spoke to him calmly by name, explained what the doctors were doing and gave him an update. When he hit the wall and came closer, she stepped back to keep the exit behind her and pressed the alarm button. Security staff arrived and calmed him. No one was hurt. The nurse reported the incident, and the department later added regular updates for waiting relatives.",quiz:[{question:"A visitor is pacing, swearing and standing very close to you. What is the best first action?",options:[{text:"Keep a safe distance, speak calmly and get a colleague's help",correct:!0},{text:"Tell him firmly to leave or you will call the police",correct:!1},{text:"Turn away and ignore him until he stops",correct:!1},{text:"Hold his arm and lead him out of the ward",correct:!1}],explanation:"Calm words, distance and early help lower the risk; threats, ignoring or touching can make violence worse."},{question:"Where should you stand when talking to an angry person in a room?",options:[{text:"With the exit behind me and at a safe distance",correct:!0},{text:"Between him and the door to stop him leaving",correct:!1},{text:"Very close so he can hear me clearly",correct:!1},{text:"In a corner so he cannot get behind me",correct:!1}],explanation:"You need a clear way out; never block the person's exit or get trapped in a corner."},{question:"A person shows a knife in the waiting area. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Raise the alarm, move people away and call the police on 104",correct:!0},{text:"Try to take the knife while he is distracted",correct:!1},{text:"Ask him calmly to hand the knife to you",correct:!1},{text:"Call 115 because it is a Civil Defense matter",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never try to disarm anyone; the alarm, distance and the police (104), following your hospital's procedure, are the safe response."},{question:"A relative insulted and threatened you but did not hit anyone. Should you report it?",options:[{text:"Yes, every incident, including verbal abuse, is reported",correct:!0},{text:"No, only incidents with injuries are reported",correct:!1},{text:"Only if the relative does it a second time",correct:!1},{text:"No, it is part of the job and must be accepted",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reports of verbal abuse help the hospital prevent violence; abuse is not part of the job."},{question:"Which action makes violence more likely?",options:[{text:"Arguing with the person and shouting back",correct:!0},{text:"Listening and repeating back what they need",correct:!1},{text:"Explaining what will happen and when",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping your hands visible and relaxed",correct:!1}],explanation:"Arguing and shouting raise tension; listening, explaining and an open posture lower it."},{question:"Relatives have waited a long time in the emergency department and are getting upset. What helps prevent violence?",options:[{text:"Giving them clear and regular updates on the patient",correct:!0},{text:"Closing the waiting-room door so they stay outside",correct:!1},{text:"Asking security to stand beside them all the time",correct:!1},{text:"Telling them nothing until the doctor has finished",correct:!1}],explanation:"Uncertainty fuels anger; clear, regular information is a simple way to prevent it."}]},{id:19,code:"UTIL-19",icon:"PlugZap",accent:"#ca8a04",category:"Emergency",title:"Power, water and medical-gas failure",subtitle:"Keep life-support patients safe when the electricity, water or oxygen supply stops",summary:"A hospital depends on electricity, water and medical gases such as oxygen. When one of them fails, the first people at risk are patients on ventilators, oxygen, infusion pumps and other life-support equipment. Hospitals have backup systems, such as generators and reserve oxygen, but staff at the bedside must check patients at once and be ready to ventilate them by hand. Follow your hospital's utility-failure plan and report to engineering; do not repair or switch systems yourself.",blocks:[{heading:"Backup power: what to know",points:["When the power fails, the generator takes over after a short break; only some outlets and equipment are on backup power.","Know which outlets in your area are on emergency power, as your hospital marks them.","Life-support equipment is plugged into emergency-power outlets; battery equipment is kept charged.","Lights, lifts and air conditioning may not all work on backup power."]},{heading:"Medical-gas failure",points:["An alarm panel shows low or high pressure in the oxygen or other gas lines.","Check patients on oxygen first and switch them to portable cylinders as the plan says.","Keep a bag-mask ready next to every patient on a ventilator.","Zone shut-off valves are closed only on the order of the person in charge or the engineering team."]}],steps:[{title:"Check life-support patients first",detail:"When the power or gas fails, go first to patients on ventilators, oxygen, infusion pumps and monitors. Check that each one is breathing and that the equipment is working or running on battery.",critical:!0},{title:"Be ready to ventilate by hand",detail:"If a ventilator stops, ventilate the patient with a bag-mask, connected to a portable oxygen cylinder if available, as you were trained, and call for help. Never leave a ventilated patient alone during a failure.",critical:!0},{title:"Switch to portable oxygen",detail:"If the oxygen pipeline pressure drops, switch patients to portable cylinders as your plan says, starting with those in greatest need. Keep cylinders secured and away from heat and flames.",critical:!0},{title:"Report and follow the plan",detail:"Report the failure at once to engineering and your team leader through the internal number. Follow the hospital's utility-failure plan; the command team decides on moving patients or stopping planned procedures."},{title:"Water failure",detail:"Use stored drinking water, and alcohol hand rub for hand hygiene when hands are not visibly dirty, and save water for essential care. Departments that depend on water, such as dialysis and sterilization, follow their own plans."}],warnings:["Never leave a ventilated patient without someone ready to ventilate by hand during a power or gas failure.","Do not open or close medical-gas valves, reset panels or connect equipment to the generator yourself: this is the work of engineering and authorized staff.","Do not plug life-support equipment into an outlet that is not marked for emergency power: it may lose power."],tips:["At the start of each shift, check that a bag-mask with a mask of the right size is at the bedside of every ventilated patient.","Keep battery equipment on charge and know from its manual how long it can run on battery.","Know where the portable oxygen cylinders and flashlights are kept in your department.","Take part in utility-failure drills."],activity:{prompt:"You work the night shift in the ICU. The lights go out for a moment and come back dimmer; an alarm sounds on one ventilator. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I check the patient on that ventilator and ventilate by hand with the bag-mask if it is not working",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the patient comes first; manual ventilation keeps them breathing."},{text:"I call engineering and wait for them before touching the patient",correct:!1,feedback:"Report it, but the patient cannot wait; check and ventilate first or let a colleague call."},{text:"I unplug the ventilator and plug it into another outlet",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving plugs without checking can make things worse; check the patient and the device and use emergency-power outlets."},{text:"I restart the ventilator several times until it works",correct:!1,feedback:"Restarting wastes time while the patient may not be breathing; ventilate by hand."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse on a medical ward. The oxygen-pressure alarm sounds and the wall oxygen stops. Three patients use wall oxygen; one is very short of breath. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I switch the most breathless patient to a portable cylinder, then the others, and report the alarm",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: those in greatest need first, then report."},{text:"I close the zone oxygen valve to find the leak",correct:!1,feedback:"Valves are closed only on the order of the person in charge or engineering."},{text:"I wait for the alarm to stop by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"The patients need oxygen now; act and report."},{text:"I give the patients water and ask them to breathe slowly",correct:!1,feedback:"That does not replace oxygen; switch them to portable cylinders."}]},{prompt:"Engineering says the pipeline will be off for some time and the portable cylinders are running low. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell my team leader the number of patients and cylinders so the command team can arrange supply or move patients",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the command team needs exact information to decide."},{text:"I turn down all the patients' oxygen to make it last",correct:!1,feedback:"Oxygen is a medicine; changes are made only on the treating doctor's order."},{text:"I take cylinders from another ward without telling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"This can endanger patients on the other ward; supply goes through the command chain."},{text:"I move the patients to another floor myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving patients is decided by the command team under the plan."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative safety scenario: During a storm, a hospital lost mains power. The generator started, but one ward's outlets were not on backup power and an infusion pump and a ventilator alarmed. A nurse checked the ventilated patient first and began ventilating by hand with the bag-mask kept at the bedside, while a colleague called engineering. The ventilator was moved to an outlet marked for emergency power and worked again. In the review, the hospital checked which outlets were on backup power and added a bedside bag-mask check to every shift.",quiz:[{question:"The power fails in the ICU. Who do you check first?",options:[{text:"Patients on ventilators and other life-support equipment",correct:!0},{text:"Sleeping patients who need their lights on",correct:!1},{text:"The nurses' computer and the patient records",correct:!1},{text:"Visitors who may be frightened in the dark",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life-support patients are at risk within moments; check them first."},{question:"A ventilator stops and does not restart. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Ventilate by hand with a bag-mask and call for help",correct:!0},{text:"Wait for engineering to repair the ventilator first",correct:!1},{text:"Put an oxygen face mask on and leave",correct:!1},{text:"Restart the ventilator again and again until it works",correct:!1}],explanation:"Manual ventilation keeps the patient breathing until help arrives; the patient cannot wait."},{question:"The oxygen pipeline alarm sounds. Who may close the zone oxygen valve?",options:[{text:"The person in charge or engineering, under the plan",correct:!0},{text:"Any nurse who hears the alarm on the ward",correct:!1},{text:"The patient's relatives if no staff are near",correct:!1},{text:"The first person who finds the valve box",correct:!1}],explanation:"Closing a zone valve stops oxygen for every patient in the zone; it is done only on order, after patients are switched to portable supply."},{question:"Where should life-support equipment be plugged in?",options:[{text:"An outlet marked for emergency power",correct:!0},{text:"Any outlet closest to the bed",correct:!1},{text:"An extension lead with many sockets",correct:!1},{text:"The outlet used for the room's lamp",correct:!1}],explanation:"Only emergency-power outlets receive generator supply after a failure."},{question:"The water stops on the ward. How do you clean your hands when they are not visibly dirty?",options:[{text:"With alcohol hand rub",correct:!0},{text:"I stop hand hygiene until water returns",correct:!1},{text:"I just wipe my hands on a clean towel",correct:!1},{text:"I use water from the toilet cistern",correct:!1}],explanation:"WHO recommends alcohol hand rub when hands are not visibly dirty; save stored water for essential care."},{question:"Which action is wrong during a power failure?",options:[{text:"Resetting the generator panel myself",correct:!0},{text:"Checking every ventilated patient at once",correct:!1},{text:"Reporting the failure to engineering",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping a bag-mask at the bedside",correct:!1}],explanation:"Generator and electrical panels are worked on only by engineering and authorized staff."}]},{id:20,code:"SHARP-20",icon:"Scissors",accent:"#be123c",category:"Safety",title:"Sharps and needle-stick injuries",subtitle:"Prevent injuries from needles and sharp tools, and know what to do at once if one happens",summary:"Injuries from needles, blades and other sharp items can pass blood-borne infections such as hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV from a patient to a health worker. Most can be prevented by safe work habits: using devices with safety features, never recapping needles and putting sharps into a sharps container at once. If an injury or a blood splash happens, first aid takes moments, but reporting at once matters just as much: any medicine after exposure is decided by a doctor under your hospital protocol, and it works best when started early.",blocks:[{heading:"Safe habits with sharps",points:["Never recap, bend or break a used needle.","Use devices with safety features and activate the feature right after use.","Put each sharp into a sharps container at once, at the place of use; the person who used it disposes of it.","Do not fill a container past its fill line; close it and replace it.","Never pass a sharp by hand; use a tray."]},{heading:"Who is at risk",points:["Nurses and doctors during injections, blood draws and surgery.","Laboratory staff when handling samples.","Cleaners and laundry staff when sharps are left in bins, bedding or bags."]}],steps:[{title:"Wash the wound at once",detail:"If a needle or sharp item injures you, wash the area at once with soap and running water. Do not scrub hard, and do not use bleach or strong chemicals on the skin.",critical:!0},{title:"Rinse splashes",detail:"If blood or body fluid splashes into the eyes, nose or mouth, rinse at once with plenty of clean water or saline. Wash splashes on the skin with soap and water.",critical:!0},{title:"Report at once",detail:"Tell your supervisor straight away and go to the unit your hospital names for staff exposures (for example staff health or the emergency department). Do not wait until the end of your shift.",critical:!0},{title:"Medical assessment",detail:"A doctor assesses the risk and decides on tests and any medicine after exposure (post-exposure prophylaxis) under your hospital protocol. Do not take any medicine on your own. The source patient may be tested with consent, as hospital rules allow."},{title:"Record and follow up",detail:"Fill in the injury report: what, when, how and with which device. Attend all follow-up visits and tests. Reports help the hospital find unsafe devices and practices."}],warnings:["Never recap, bend or break a used needle by hand: this is a common cause of needle-stick injuries.","Do not hide an injury or delay reporting it: any medicine after exposure works best when started early.","Never put your hand into a sharps container or a waste bag to push the contents down."],tips:["Take the sharps container to the bedside before you start the procedure.","Make sure you are vaccinated against hepatitis B, as your hospital's staff health program offers.","Ask the patient to keep still before the procedure, and get help with a restless patient.","Know your hospital's exposure procedure and where to go, at any hour."],activity:{prompt:"You are a cleaner emptying a waste bag on a ward. A needle left in the bag pricks your hand through your glove. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I wash the area with soap and running water and report at once to my supervisor",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: first aid, then immediate reporting for a medical assessment."},{text:"I squeeze the wound hard and put bleach on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Bleach and strong chemicals harm the skin; wash with soap and water."},{text:"I finish my shift and report it tomorrow if it hurts",correct:!1,feedback:"Delay matters; any medicine after exposure works best when started early."},{text:"I put a plaster on it and say nothing because it is small",correct:!1,feedback:"Even a small prick can pass infection; every injury is reported and assessed."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse drawing blood from a restless patient. The needle slips and pricks your finger. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I make the needle safe, wash the wound with soap and running water and tell my supervisor",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: safe disposal, washing and reporting."},{text:"I finish the blood draw first, then think about it",correct:!1,feedback:"Stop and care for the injury; a colleague can finish."},{text:"I recap the needle so it cannot prick anyone else",correct:!1,feedback:"Recapping causes more injuries; put it in the sharps container."},{text:"I clean it with alcohol hand rub and carry on",correct:!1,feedback:"Wash with soap and running water and report; hand rub is not the first aid here."}]},{prompt:"In the staff clinic, the doctor says the patient's test results are not known yet and offers medicine after exposure under the protocol. What is correct?",options:[{text:"I follow the doctor's advice under the protocol and attend every follow-up visit",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the decision belongs to the doctor and follow-up is part of care."},{text:"I wait for the patient's results before deciding anything",correct:!1,feedback:"The doctor decides the timing; waiting can reduce the benefit of the medicine."},{text:"I take medicine from the ward stock without a prescription",correct:!1,feedback:"Never take medicine on your own; follow the doctor and the protocol."},{text:"I ask the patient directly on the ward about his illnesses",correct:!1,feedback:"Testing and information about the source patient go through the hospital process, with consent and privacy."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative safety scenario: A laundry worker in a hospital was sorting used bedding when a needle left in a sheet pricked his hand. He knew the procedure from training: he washed the area with soap and running water and told his supervisor at once. He was seen in the staff clinic during the same shift, where a doctor assessed the risk and decided on follow-up under the hospital protocol. The report led the ward to check how it disposed of sharps, and staff were reminded to put every sharp into the container at the bedside.",quiz:[{question:"A needle has just been used for an injection. What do you do with it?",options:[{text:"Put it straight into the sharps container at the bedside",correct:!0},{text:"Recap it carefully with both hands and throw it away",correct:!1},{text:"Leave it on the tray to dispose of after the round",correct:!1},{text:"Bend it so that no one can use it again",correct:!1}],explanation:"Immediate disposal at the place of use prevents most injuries; recapping and bending cause them."},{question:"A used needle pricks your finger. What is the first aid?",options:[{text:"Wash with soap and running water",correct:!0},{text:"Pour bleach on the wound to kill germs",correct:!1},{text:"Suck the wound and spit out the blood",correct:!1},{text:"Cover it with a plaster and carry on",correct:!1}],explanation:"Soap and water is the first aid; bleach and strong chemicals harm the skin, and immediate reporting must follow."},{question:"Blood splashes into a colleague's eye. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Rinse the eye at once with plenty of clean water or saline",correct:!0},{text:"Rub the eye with a dry tissue to remove the blood",correct:!1},{text:"Put antibiotic eye drops in it from the ward stock",correct:!1},{text:"Wait to see whether the eye becomes red later",correct:!1}],explanation:"Prompt, plentiful rinsing with clean water or saline removes the blood; the exposure is then reported."},{question:"Who decides on medicine after a needle-stick injury?",options:[{text:"A doctor, under the hospital protocol",correct:!0},{text:"The injured worker, from the ward medicines",correct:!1},{text:"The supervisor, based on the size of the wound",correct:!1},{text:"No one; a prick never needs medicine",correct:!1}],explanation:"Medicine after exposure depends on a medical risk assessment; take it only as the doctor and protocol decide."},{question:"The sharps container is full up to its fill line. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Close it and use a new container",correct:!0},{text:"Press the contents down with a gloved hand",correct:!1},{text:"Shake it so more sharps fit inside",correct:!1},{text:"Put the extra needles in a plastic bag",correct:!1}],explanation:"A full container is closed and replaced; pressing, shaking or plastic bags cause injuries."},{question:"Which statement is wrong?",options:[{text:"A small prick does not need to be reported",correct:!0},{text:"Every sharps injury is reported at once",correct:!1},{text:"Safety features are activated after use",correct:!1},{text:"Sharps are never passed by hand",correct:!1}],explanation:"Even a small prick can pass infection; every injury is reported and assessed."}]},{id:21,code:"DECON-21",icon:"Biohazard",accent:"#0f766e",category:"Emergency",title:"Contaminated patients at the emergency department",subtitle:"Protect staff and the hospital first, then decontaminate and treat",summary:"After a chemical incident, casualties may arrive at the emergency department with the substance still on their skin and clothes, often on their own and before any warning. If they come inside, staff and other patients can be exposed and the department may have to close. The rule is: protect staff and the hospital first. Stop contaminated patients at the entrance, take them to the decontamination area your hospital's plan names and let only trained staff in the right personal protective equipment (PPE) care for them. Civil Defense (115) deals with the incident scene; follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority.",blocks:[{heading:"Signs that a patient may be contaminated",points:["Several patients from one place with the same symptoms.","A smell, powder or liquid on the skin or clothes.","Burning eyes, coughing or breathing problems in patients and in staff near them.","Information from 115, the ambulance or the patient about a chemical release."]},{heading:"The decontamination area as a concept",points:["It is outside the main building, near the emergency entrance, as your hospital's plan names it.","It has a dirty side, where patients arrive, and a clean side, where they go after decontamination.","Removing clothing and washing with water remove much of the contamination.","Only trained staff in the PPE set by the plan work on the dirty side."]}],steps:[{title:"Recognize and stop at the entrance",detail:"If you suspect contamination, stop the patient outside the entrance, calmly and from a distance. Do not let them into the department and do not touch them without PPE.",critical:!0},{title:"Raise the alarm",detail:"Tell the person in charge and announce your hospital's code for hazardous materials. Close the doors so contamination does not reach the department; if a patient has already come in, keep that area closed and wait for instructions.",critical:!0},{title:"Put on PPE before contact",detail:"Only staff trained for decontamination put on the PPE the plan sets and work in the decontamination area. If you are not trained, guide patients from a distance and do not enter the dirty side.",critical:!0},{title:"Life-saving care and decontamination",detail:"Trained staff in PPE give urgent life-saving care at the decontamination area, such as opening the airway or stopping severe bleeding. Patients remove their clothing, which is put into bags, and are washed with water, as the plan says.",critical:!0},{title:"Clean entry and records",detail:"After decontamination, patients enter through the clean route for treatment. Record each patient and the bags of belongings, and tell the treatment team and 115 which substance is suspected."}],warnings:["Never bring a contaminated patient into the emergency department before decontamination: staff and other patients can be harmed and the department closed.","Never touch or help a contaminated patient without the right PPE and training: a staff member who becomes a casualty cannot help anyone.","Do not use hot water, bleach or hard scrubbing on patients' skin."],tips:["Know where your hospital's decontamination area is and who is trained to work in it.","Learn your hospital's code for hazardous materials and how it is announced.","Keep spare clothing or hospital gowns and bags for belongings ready near the decontamination area."],activity:{prompt:"You work at the emergency department reception. Three workers from the same factory walk in coughing, with wet clothes and a strong chemical smell. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly ask them to stay outside the entrance, keep my distance and tell the person in charge to start the hazardous-materials plan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stop them at the entrance, keep your distance and raise the alarm."},{text:"I take them inside to a treatment room because they are coughing",correct:!1,feedback:"This brings contamination into the department; they must be decontaminated first."},{text:"I help them take off their wet clothes in the waiting room",correct:!1,feedback:"Without PPE you become a casualty, and the waiting room becomes contaminated."},{text:"I give them water to drink and ask them to wait in the corridor",correct:!1,feedback:"They must stay outside and be decontaminated; the corridor spreads contamination."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are a nurse at the emergency entrance. An ambulance team reports that it is bringing casualties from a chemical spill. Some patients have already arrived by car. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I make sure the code is announced, stop patients outside and keep untrained staff away",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the hospital's plan starts at once and staff are protected."},{text:"I run outside to help the patients out of the cars without PPE",correct:!1,feedback:"You may be exposed; only trained staff in PPE make contact."},{text:"I open all the doors so patients can come in quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Open doors let contamination into the department."},{text:"I wait for 115 to arrive before doing anything",correct:!1,feedback:"The hospital's own plan starts at once; 115 handles the incident scene."}]},{prompt:"In the decontamination area, a patient is not breathing well and is still in contaminated clothes. You are trained and wearing PPE. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I give urgent airway care while the team removes his clothing and decontaminates him",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: for trained staff in PPE, life-saving care and decontamination go together."},{text:"I carry him inside to the resuscitation room as he is",correct:!1,feedback:"This contaminates the department; care starts in the decontamination area."},{text:"I wait until decontamination is fully finished before any care",correct:!1,feedback:"Life-saving actions should not wait if trained staff in PPE can give them."},{text:"I take off my mask to hear his breathing better",correct:!1,feedback:"Removing PPE exposes you; keep it on."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative safety scenario: Workers from a workshop arrived at a hospital by private car after a chemical leak. Before any warning, two of them walked into the waiting room with a strong smell, and a receptionist began coughing. The person in charge closed the waiting room, announced the hazardous-materials code and sent new arrivals to the decontamination area outside. Trained staff in PPE removed the patients' clothing, bagged it and washed them, then the patients entered through the clean route. In the review, the hospital trained reception staff to recognize contamination and stop patients at the entrance.",quiz:[{question:"A patient walks in with a chemical smell and a damp powder on his clothes. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Stop him outside the entrance and raise the alarm",correct:!0},{text:"Take him to a treatment room before he gets worse",correct:!1},{text:"Brush the powder off his clothes with your hands",correct:!1},{text:"Ask him to sit in the waiting room until called",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stopping at the entrance and raising the alarm keep contamination out of the department and protect staff."},{question:"Who may work on the dirty side of the decontamination area?",options:[{text:"Trained staff wearing the PPE set by the plan",correct:!0},{text:"Any staff member with gloves and a surgical mask",correct:!1},{text:"Relatives who came with the patients, to help",correct:!1},{text:"Security staff who guard the emergency door",correct:!1}],explanation:"The dirty side needs training and specific PPE; people without them can become casualties."},{question:"What removes much of the contamination from a patient?",options:[{text:"Removing the clothing and washing with water",correct:!0},{text:"Giving the patient milk or water to drink",correct:!1},{text:"Covering the patient with a clean blanket",correct:!1},{text:"Scrubbing the skin hard with bleach solution",correct:!1}],explanation:"Clothing holds much of the substance; removing it and washing with water are the main method. Bleach and hard scrubbing harm the skin."},{question:"A contaminated patient at the decontamination area has severe bleeding. What happens?",options:[{text:"Trained staff in PPE control the bleeding during decontamination",correct:!0},{text:"He is taken straight inside to the operating room as he is",correct:!1},{text:"Bleeding control waits until decontamination is completed",correct:!1},{text:"Anyone nearby presses on the wound with bare hands",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life-saving actions are given by trained staff in PPE during decontamination; not inside the department and not with bare hands."},{question:'A colleague wants to help a contaminated patient without PPE "to save time". What is the best reply?',options:[{text:"If you are exposed, you become a casualty and cannot help",correct:!0},{text:"It is fine if you wash your hands well afterwards",correct:!1},{text:"It is fine if the patient does not look very ill",correct:!1},{text:"It is fine if you hold your breath while helping",correct:!1}],explanation:"An exposed staff member becomes another patient and the team loses a member; PPE and training come before any contact."},{question:"Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Letting contaminated patients wait inside the department",correct:!0},{text:"Bagging the patients' clothing and belongings",correct:!1},{text:"Using a separate clean route after decontamination",correct:!1},{text:"Telling the team which substance is suspected",correct:!1}],explanation:"Contaminated patients stay outside until decontaminated; inside, they contaminate staff and other patients."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
